// StdToX64Conversion — Std → Target (x64) lowering. Takes a StdModule and
// produces a fresh TargetModule of x64 `TargetOp`s. Each StdOp is lowered to its
// x64 op sequence; the result still uses VIRTUAL registers (TargetVReg.virtual),
// which the register allocator colors to physical GPRs afterward.
//
// This is the ONLY tier boundary below Std — shv2 has no MIR tier (see
// StdDialect's header for why v1's was dropped). The virtual registers this pass
// consumes are Std's own `ValueId`s, which have BEEN SSA virtual registers since
// the Maxon→Std lowering minted them; v1's MIR tier never added a value model,
// only a copy of one.
//
// THIN for maxon-shv2 M1: lowers exactly the `return 42` slice —
//   StdOp.const(id, v, valueType) -> constMaterializationOp (movRegImm32 / movRegImm)
//   StdOp.ret(id)  terminator     -> mov R8, <retval>   + ret
// The primary return value goes to **R8**, not RAX — Maxon's self-designed
// calling convention (v1 does the same). Later milestones GROW the dispatch with
// the ALU / compare-branch / general-call / memory arms + the float (XMM) return
// path as the Std dialect grows. v1's per-function lowering structure (a skeleton
// clone of the block graph, then a per-function op walk) is preserved so the
// per-function fan-out driver (M5) can wrap one function at a time.
//
// NOTE ON FRAMES: unlike v1's ISel — which emits `epilogue` at every return and
// lets the prologue/epilogue pass strip/re-emit it — shv2's return lowering emits
// ONLY the R8 move + `ret`. Frame setup AND teardown are owned entirely by the
// prologue/epilogue pass (InsertPrologueEpilogue), which inserts `prologue` at
// entry and `epilogue` before the return terminator when the function needs a
// frame. This removes the emit-then-strip dance; at M1 no user function needs a
// frame, so main lowers to a flat `mov reg,42; mov r8,reg; ret`.

// The low end of the range the 32-bit immediate form can carry. `mov r32, imm32` ZERO-
// extends into the full 64-bit register, so it spans the UNSIGNED 32-bit domain and
// nothing below it — see constMaterializationOp for what happens when this bound is
// dropped.
let Imm32ZeroExtendedMin = 0 as MachineWord

// THE size choice for materializing an integer constant into a GPR — the single place it
// is made, because two copies of it have to agree BYTE FOR BYTE: the live-range splitter
// REMATERIALIZES a constant at each use, and the op it re-emits must be identical to the
// one this pass emitted (SplitLiveRanges.rematerializeConstant).
//
//   0 <= value <= u32.max -> movRegImm32   the 5-byte `mov r32, imm32`, which ZERO-extends
//                                          its immediate across the whole 64-bit register
//   otherwise             -> movRegImm     the 10-byte `mov r64, imm64`
//
// THE LOWER BOUND IS LOAD-BEARING, and it was missing. `value <= u32.max` alone is true of
// every NEGATIVE value as well (MachineWord is signed), so `-1` took the zero-extending
// form and landed in the register as 0x00000000FFFFFFFF — while a later 64-bit `cmp reg,
// -1`, whose imm32 SIGN-extends, compared it against 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF and found them
// unequal. It stayed latent because no `const` op ever carried a negative value: a source
// `-7` parses as `7` under a `neg`. Type-qualified bounds (`u64.max` = -1, `i64.min`) are
// the first negative constants to reach here.
//
// EVERY form this emits is a `mov`, and `mov r, imm` WRITES NO FLAGS. `StdOp.const` declares
// `clobbersFlags: false` on the strength of exactly that, and compare/branch fusion depends on
// it: a short-circuit's seed literal sits between a compare and its branch, so a `const` that
// claimed to clobber flags cost 3 instructions on every `if a > 0 and b > 0`.
//
// ⚠ SO IF YOU ADD THE value==0 `xor reg, reg` ZEROING IDIOM HERE — the shorter encoding, still
// tempting — YOU MUST FIRST DEAL WITH `StdOp.const.clobbersFlags`. `xor` DOES write flags, so
// `const`'s flag behaviour would become VALUE-DEPENDENT (flag-neutral for every value but 0),
// which per-variant metadata cannot express: split the variant, or take `clobbersFlags: true`
// back and accept the lost fusion. Landing the idiom alone is a SILENT MISCOMPILE — a `jcc`
// would read flags the `xor` had already destroyed. This is the flag-neutral half of the
// "flag-neutral-vs-shorter" tradeoff, and it is not free.
export function constMaterializationOp(dest TargetVReg, value MachineWord) returns TargetOp
	if value >= Imm32ZeroExtendedMin and value <= u32.max 'fitsUnsigned32'
		return TargetOp.movRegImm32(dest, value: value)
	end 'fitsUnsigned32'
	return TargetOp.movRegImm(dest, value: value)
end 'constMaterializationOp'

// The custom-ABI integer ARGUMENT registers, in slot order: argument `k` arrives in
// (and is passed in) `argRegisters[k]`. This is v1's order MINUS its 7th slot rbx —
// rbx is CALLEE-saved in shv2, so it must never carry an argument. All six are
// caller-saved and in the allocatable pool. The RETURN value uses R8, which is not an
// argument register, so there is no return/argument register conflict. The array LENGTH
// is this file's REGISTER capacity (`x64RegisterArgCapacity`): an argument past it
// travels in an outgoing STACK slot (`win64OutgoingArgDisp`).
let argRegisters = [X64Register.rcx, X64Register.rdx, X64Register.rax, X64Register.r9, X64Register.rsi, X64Register.rdi]

// The custom-ABI FLOAT argument registers, in slot order: a FLOAT parameter/argument travels in
// `argFpRegisters[fpSlot]` = xmm0..xmm5, where `fpSlot` is counted by a SEPARATE FP counter from the
// integer `argRegisters` (v1's internal convention — see `abiFileSlotIndex`). The two files
// count independently, so a program may spend all its arguments on floats; the array therefore
// holds exactly as many slots as `argRegisters` does, which `x64RegisterArgCapacity` asserts —
// the shared placement rule asks ONE capacity of whichever file an argument belongs to, so two
// depths would overflow floats onto the stack while the callee still read a register.
// xmm0 doubles as the float RETURN register
// (`callResultRegister`), exactly as an integer first-arg register never conflicts with R8: an
// argument is consumed by the callee before the call, and the return value overwrites xmm0 after it.
// All six are CALLER-saved (`callerSavedMask`) — the whole caller-saved XMM half since Wave 2 made
// xmm6–15 callee-saved — so a float live ACROSS a call is confined to xmm6–15 by the liveness census
// with no hand-holding at a call site.
//
// EXPORTED for the reason `argRegisterAt` is: `__gt_morestack` (X64GtRuntime) runs from a stack guard
// emitted BEFORE the `param` captures, so an incoming float argument is still sitting in these
// registers when it calls VirtualAlloc/VirtualFree — which are Win64-volatile in exactly them. It must
// therefore save and restore this list, and a second hand-written spelling of xmm0–5 over there is the
// one-fact-written-twice bug this file's header warns of.
export let argFpRegisters = [X64Register.xmm0, X64Register.xmm1, X64Register.xmm2, X64Register.xmm3, X64Register.xmm4, X64Register.xmm5]

// ⭐ **THE HIGH HALF OF AN UNSIGNED DIVIDEND** — RDX, for the group-3 `div` that reads RDX:RAX as a
// single 128-bit number. Zero, because Maxon's unsigned divide is a 64-bit ÷ 64-bit one: the dividend
// IS the 64 bits in RAX and there is no high half to supply. Named rather than a bare `0` beside
// `TargetOp.movRegImm32` because a `0` there reads as an arbitrary constant, and this one is a
// PRECONDITION of the instruction — with anything else in RDX the quotient can overflow and `div`
// raises #DE (which is also why the unsigned form, unlike `idiv`, has no overflow case at all).
let UnsignedDividendHighHalf = 0x0

// The `VirtualAlloc` call shape `lowerOsAllocPages` emits. Named rather than spelled as bare
// hex at the call site: `0x3000` is unreadable and `MEM_COMMIT|MEM_RESERVE` is the fact.
//
// ⭐ `MEM_COMMIT` IS WHAT MAKES THE ZEROING CONTRACT TRUE. Windows guarantees newly-committed
// pages are zero-filled, which is the entire basis for `__slab_alloc` handing out bump slots
// without memzeroing them (ARCHITECTURE.md → "Allocator: the zeroing contract"). Reserving
// without committing would hand back untouched address space, and the first read of a bump slot
// would fault rather than answer 0. The two flags travel together for that reason.
let VirtualAllocLetOsChooseBase = 0x0

// EXPORTED because the hand-assembled `__gt_morestack` chunk (X64GtRuntime) makes the SAME
// `VirtualAlloc(NULL, size, MEM_COMMIT|MEM_RESERVE, PAGE_READWRITE)` call to grow a GT stack — one
// spelling of the two flags, shared by the Std lowering here and the byte code there, not two copies.
export let VirtualAllocCommitAndReserve = 0x3000  // MEM_COMMIT (0x1000) | MEM_RESERVE (0x2000)
export let VirtualAllocPageReadWrite = 0x4  // PAGE_READWRITE

// The `VirtualFree(addr, 0, MEM_RELEASE)` shape `lowerOsFreePages` and the hand-assembled `__gt_morestack`
// chunk both emit (P1.5-B1a′). `MEM_RELEASE` releases the WHOLE reservation from its base — which is why
// `dwSize` MUST be 0 and `osFreePages` needs no size operand. EXPORTED for the same one-spelling reason as
// the two flags above.
export let VirtualFreeMemRelease = 0x8000  // MEM_RELEASE
export let VirtualFreeSizeMustBeZero = 0x0  // dwSize is 0 for a MEM_RELEASE (release the whole reservation)

// F1a — the DECOMMIT half of `VirtualFree`, and the COMMIT-only half of `VirtualAlloc`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`MEM_DECOMMIT` TAKES THE REAL `dwSize`, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE RULE FROM `MEM_RELEASE` ONE LINE UP.** A
// release frees the WHOLE reservation and demands `dwSize == 0`; a decommit drops the backing of the SUB-RANGE
// it is given and keeps the reservation, so a zero there would decommit nothing at all. The two constants sit
// together precisely so the pair of rules is read together.
let VirtualFreeMemDecommit = 0x4000  // MEM_DECOMMIT — drop the physical backing, KEEP the reservation.

// `MEM_COMMIT` ALONE, deliberately NOT the `MEM_COMMIT|MEM_RESERVE` pair `osAllocPages` uses: the address space
// is already the caller's — reserved by `osReservePages`, or reserved-and-then-decommitted — so a commit asks
// only for pages, never for address space. Windows zero-fills what it newly commits; `StdDialect.osCommitPages`
// says why no caller may rely on that, since the other three lanes answer differently.
let VirtualAllocCommitOnly = 0x1000  // MEM_COMMIT

// F1c — the RESERVE half of `VirtualAlloc`, and the protection a reservation carries.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`MEM_RESERVE` ALONE IS THE ONE ALLOCATION TYPE THAT COSTS NO COMMIT CHARGE**, which is the entire
// reason the op exists: Windows charges every committed byte to the process whether or not it is touched
// (Go's own reason for shrinking its Windows heap arena — `vendor/go/src/runtime/malloc.go:259`), and a
// reservation is charged nothing but address space.
//
// ⭐ `PAGE_NOACCESS` rather than `PAGE_READWRITE`, which is where this DIVERGES from Go's `sysReserveOS`
// (`mem_windows.go:118` passes `PAGE_READWRITE`). Neither is wrong — for `MEM_RESERVE` the protection is only
// the initial page protection of a region that has no pages yet, and a touch of reserved-uncommitted memory
// faults under either. `PAGE_NOACCESS` is chosen so that the WINDOWS lane says the same thing the three POSIX
// lanes say: a reservation permits nothing (`PosixMmap.MmapProtNone`), and the commit is what grants access.
// One reservation state described one way, rather than a lane that reads as writable and a lane that does not.
let VirtualAllocReserveOnly = 0x2000  // MEM_RESERVE — take the address space, commit nothing.
let VirtualAllocPageNoAccess = 0x1  // PAGE_NOACCESS — a reservation permits nothing until it is committed.

// `GetStdHandle(STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE)` — the handle `osWriteStdout` writes to (P1.2 wave A). The
// parameter is a DWORD, so the 32-bit `mov` (which zero-extends) puts exactly (DWORD)-11 in ECX.
let StdOutputHandle = 0xFFFFFFF5  // (DWORD)-11 — GetStdHandle's STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE
// ⚠ **THE THIRD OF THIS FAMILY, `StdErrorHandle`, IS `X64Runtime`'s AND IS READ FROM THERE.** It was the
// panic writer's alone until `printError` made this isel a second reader; it is now `export`ed rather than
// respelled here, because one number with two spellings is the duplication a second reader creates. See its
// declaration for why it stays in that file.

// ⭐ The Win64 half of `StdLoweringShared.posixStreamFd` — which of the two selectors above a stream asks
// `GetStdHandle` for. Exhaustive over `OsOutputStream` for that door's reason: a third stream must fail to
// compile here rather than silently pick one.
function win64StdHandleSelector(stream OsOutputStream) returns ParsedInt
	return match stream 's'
		standardOutput gives StdOutputHandle
		standardError gives StdErrorHandle
	end 's'
end 'win64StdHandleSelector'

// `GetModuleFileNameA`'s `hModule` — NULL asks for the path of the RUNNING image, which is the only
// module this op is ever about. Named rather than reusing `NullPointer` below because that constant is
// WriteFile's `lpOverlapped`, a different argument of a different call that merely happens to be zero
// too; folding them would make a future change to either one silently change the other.
let CurrentModuleHandle = 0x0

// WriteFile's `lpOverlapped` — NULL for a synchronous write.
let NullPointer = 0x0

// WaitForMultipleObjects' `bWaitAll` — FALSE: the netpoll wakes on the FIRST signaled handle (any child that
// exited), not when ALL have. WaitForSingleObject / WaitForMultipleObjects return WAIT_OBJECT_0 (0) for the
// (first) signaled handle; `__gt_proc_check` tests that value.
let WaitAnyHandle = 0x0

// --- P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the IOCP overlapped-read substrate's Win32 magic values ---
// The exact constants v1's `__subp_create_overlapped_pipe` (read path) and the bootstrap's IOCP init use, named
// once here so the pipe/iocp/event/thread lowerings cannot restate them. `INBOUND|OVERLAPPED|FIRST_PIPE_INSTANCE`
// is what makes the parent read end usable with FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED + IOCP (an anonymous `CreatePipe` handle
// cannot be overlapped).
let InheritHandlesTrue = 1  // CreateProcessA bInheritHandles=TRUE (the child inherits the pipe write end)
let PipeReadOpenMode = 0x40080001  // PIPE_ACCESS_INBOUND | FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED | FILE_FLAG_FIRST_PIPE_INSTANCE
let PipeMessageMode = 0x0  // PIPE_TYPE_BYTE | PIPE_READMODE_BYTE | PIPE_WAIT
let PipeMaxInstances = 1  // one server instance per uniquely-named pipe
let PipeBufferSize = 0x10000  // nOutBufferSize / nInBufferSize (64 KB, v1's SubpPipeBufferSize)
let PipeDefaultTimeout = 0x0  // nDefaultTimeOut (0 = the 50 ms system default; unused for a byte-mode read)
let GenericWrite = 0x40000000  // the child write end's dwDesiredAccess (CreateFileW)
let GenericRead = 0x80000000  // the child stdin-read end's dwDesiredAccess (CreateFileW, streaming slice 1b)
let PipeWriteOpenMode = 0x00080002  // PIPE_ACCESS_OUTBOUND | FILE_FLAG_FIRST_PIPE_INSTANCE — the sync stdin server pipe (no OVERLAPPED)
let FileShareNone = 0x0  // dwShareMode (the pipe is not shared)

// R4.1: a file opened for READING permits concurrent readers. `FileShareNone` is right for a pipe end,
// which must not be shared, and would be wrong here — it would make a second `openRead` of the same path
// fail with a sharing violation, which `managed-file.exists` (open, close, re-open) would trip over.
let FileShareRead = 0x1  // dwShareMode for a read-only open

// R4.2: a file opened for WRITING permits concurrent readers AND writers. `FileShareRead` alone would make
// `__ManagedFile.openWrite` fail with `ERROR_SHARING_VIOLATION` whenever an editor, an indexer or a virus
// scanner happens to hold a write handle on the path — an intermittent failure that depends on what else is
// running on the box. The bootstrap's sync worker makes the same choice and says so.
let FileShareReadWrite = 0x3  // dwShareMode for a write open: FILE_SHARE_READ | FILE_SHARE_WRITE

// FILE_MAP_ALL_ACCESS — the access both `OpenFileMappingA` and `MapViewOfFile` take for the DebugStream
// ring. It is SECTION_ALL_ACCESS minus its standard-rights bits (0xF001F), and it must be ALL_ACCESS
// rather than FILE_MAP_WRITE because the producer READS the segment header (magic, versions, cursors) as
// well as writing it — a write-only view would fault on the first magic check.
let FileMapAllAccess = 0xF001F
let OpenExisting = 3  // dwCreationDisposition — connect to the already-created named pipe

// R4.2: `openWrite` CREATES OR TRUNCATES. `specs/managed-file.md` documents it as "Opens a file for writing
// (creates or overwrites)", which is exactly CREATE_ALWAYS — not OPEN_ALWAYS (which would leave a longer
// previous file's tail behind the new bytes) and not CREATE_NEW (which fails on an existing path).
let CreateAlways = 2  // dwCreationDisposition — create, truncating an existing file

// R4.2: `MoveFileExA`'s dwFlags. REPLACE_EXISTING makes publishing the destination atomic rather than a
// delete-then-rename with a window where neither name exists; WRITE_THROUGH flushes the metadata so a crash
// straight after the call cannot lose the new name. Both are the bootstrap's, kept for its stated reasons.
let MoveFileReplaceWriteThrough = 9  // MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING (1) | MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH (8)
let FileAttributeNormal = 0x80  // dwFlagsAndAttributes

// --- The socket slice's ws2_32 constants ---
//
// ⚠⚠ **`AfInet` / `SockStream` / `IpprotoTcp` / `SockaddrInBytes` DO NOT LIVE HERE ANY MORE — THEY ARE
// `ManagedSocketRuntime`'s, AND THIS FILE READS THEM.** The note this paragraph replaces said they lived here
// "and nowhere else", on the argument that only the x64-windows lowering knows a "create a TCP socket" op
// means `AF_INET`/`SOCK_STREAM`/`IPPROTO_TCP`. That held only while nothing above the target tier had to
// WRITE those values into an OS structure. `__ms_tcp_connect` does: it fills a 48-byte `addrinfo` hints
// record with the SAME three values `socket()` is called with, and a 16-byte `sockaddr_in` whose length must
// equal what `connect` is handed below. Resolve for one family and create a socket in another and `connect`
// fails on a family mismatch, with no diagnostic anywhere — one fact, so one home. See
// `ManagedSocketRuntime`'s protocol block for the full argument and for what a POSIX or IPv6 lane changes.

// `MAKEWORD(2, 2)` - the Winsock version `WSAStartup` is asked for. Both references request 2.2. This one IS
// still only this file's: nothing above the target tier reads a Winsock version.
let WinsockVersion22 = 0x0202

// The `flags` argument of `send`/`recv`, always zero: no `MSG_OOB`, no `MSG_PEEK`. `specs/managed-socket.md`
// documents a plain stream send and receive, and a partial transfer is a normal answer rather than a flag.
let SendRecvNoFlags = 0
let HandleFlagInherit = 1  // SetHandleInformation dwMask/HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT — stripped from a parent pipe end, SET on a child stdio handle
let HandleFlagClear = 0  // SetHandleInformation dwFlags = 0 (strip inheritance)
let IocpNoCompletionKey = 0x0  // CreateIoCompletionPort CompletionKey for an associate
let IocpNoConcurrentThreads = 0x0  // CreateIoCompletionPort NumberOfConcurrentThreads for an associate
let IocpInvalidHandleValue = 0 - 1  // CreateIoCompletionPort FileHandle = INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE (-1) creates a new port
let IocpSingleConcurrentThread = 1  // NumberOfConcurrentThreads for a fresh port (the one completion thread)
let FileSkipCompletionPortOnSuccess = 1  // SetFileCompletionNotificationModes — a SYNC read posts no IOCP packet
let CreateEventManualReset = 0x0  // CreateEventA bManualReset = FALSE → AUTO-reset (one wait consumes one signal)
let CreateEventInitialUnsignaled = 0x0  // CreateEventA bInitialState = FALSE (unsignaled until the first SetEvent)
let ThreadDefaultStackSize = 0x0  // CreateThread dwStackSize = 0 → the executable's default stack
let ThreadRunImmediately = 0x0  // CreateThread dwCreationFlags = 0 (not CREATE_SUSPENDED)
// GetActiveProcessorCount GroupNumber = ALL_PROCESSOR_GROUPS — the count for the WHOLE machine rather than
// for the calling thread's processor group. Its own named constant rather than a bare 0xFFFF because a
// sentinel group number reads as an arbitrary WORD at the call, and choosing it over group 0 is the whole
// difference between reporting the machine and reporting 64 of its 128 processors (see `lowerOsCpuCount`).
let AllProcessorGroups = 0xFFFF

// ⭐ **`GetCurrentThread()`'s PSEUDO-HANDLE, `(HANDLE)-2`, AS AN UNSIGNED MACHINE WORD.** It is a
// compile-time CONSTANT that always names the calling thread, not a handle to open or close, so
// `QueryThreadCycleTime`'s first argument costs neither an import nor a call — the bootstrap's Windows
// emitter materializes the identical value for the identical reason (`X86CodeEmitter.Backend.cs`'s
// `CurrentThreadPseudoHandle`). Spelled as an arithmetic negation rather than as a transcribed
// two's-complement pattern, which is `IocpInvalidHandleValue`'s idiom in this same band and for its reason:
// a hand-written 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE is sixteen digits nothing checks.
let CurrentThreadPseudoHandle = 0 - 2

// The two WriteFile outgoing stack SLOTS `__print_string`'s frame reserves, past the shadow space:
// slot 0 = the 5th argument `lpOverlapped` (NULL), slot 1 = a scratch DWORD `lpNumberOfBytesWritten`
// points at (WriteFile writes the count there; it must be a valid pointer, not NULL). These are SLOT
// INDICES, not byte displacements — `win64OutgoingArgDisp` turns each into `[rsp + shadow + slot*8]`, and
// `win64OutgoingArgBytes` sizes the frame region — so the byte layout lives with ShadowSpaceBytes/
// SpillSlotBytes rather than being respelled as a literal.
let WriteFileOverlappedSlot = 0
let WriteFileBytesWrittenSlot = 1

// ⭐ THE STACK-ARGUMENT COUNT, stated ONCE. lpOverlapped (slot 0) is the ONLY word the KERNEL reads from the
// stack — one true stack argument. lpNumberOfBytesWritten (slot 1) is an out-param the kernel WRITES through
// the r9 pointer, so it needs a reserved scratch slot but is NOT a stack argument. The GT syscall shim copies
// exactly `WriteFileStackArgWords` words — the low, real-argument slots — from the GT frame onto the system
// stack (`X64GtRuntime.gtShimStackArgWords`); this file reserves those PLUS the scratch slot. Both the shim's
// copy count and the reservation derive from this one constant, so the shim can never silently disagree with
// the arg layout about how many words CreateProcessA/WriteFile pass on the stack.
export let WriteFileStackArgWords = 1 as MachineWord  // lpOverlapped (slot 0)
let WriteFileScratchSlots = 1 as MachineWord  // lpNumberOfBytesWritten out-param scratch (slot 1) — not a stack arg
let WriteFileOutgoingSlots = (WriteFileStackArgWords + WriteFileScratchSlots) as MachineWord

// The six CreateProcessA outgoing stack SLOTS `__gt_process_run`'s frame reserves, past the shadow space.
// CreateProcessA takes TEN arguments; the first four (lpApplicationName, lpCommandLine, lpProcessAttributes,
// lpThreadAttributes) go in rcx/rdx/r8/r9, and arguments 5-10 go on the stack. Only the last two are real
// values (the STARTUPINFOA and PROCESS_INFORMATION pointers); the middle four are FALSE/0/NULL. SLOT INDICES,
// not byte displacements — `win64OutgoingArgDisp` turns each into `[rsp + shadow + slot*8]` — so the byte
// layout lives with ShadowSpaceBytes/SpillSlotBytes rather than being respelled as a literal.
// ⭐ THE STACK-ARGUMENT COUNT, stated ONCE (like WriteFile above). All six of args 5-10 are real stack
// arguments the kernel reads — there is NO scratch out-param slot — so the outgoing reservation IS the
// stack-argument count. The GT syscall shim copies `CreateProcessStackArgWords` words from the GT frame onto
// the system stack; deriving the reservation from the same constant keeps the shim's copy count from drifting.
export let CreateProcessStackArgWords = 6 as MachineWord  // args 5-10, all real stack arguments
// `PeekNamedPipe`'s two stack arguments, past the four register ones: `lpBytesLeftThisMessage` is the last
// and `lpTotalBytesAvail` the one before it. Named for `CreateProcess*Slot`'s reason — a bare 0/1 at a
// `[rsp+disp]` store says nothing about which of the six arguments is being filled.
let PeekPipeAvailSlot = 0  // arg5 lpTotalBytesAvail
let PeekPipeLeftSlot = 1  // arg6 lpBytesLeftThisMessage
// EXPORTED for `X64GtRuntime`'s system-stack shim, which must reserve the same words this lowering writes
// into — one number, read by the producer and by the shim, exactly as `CreateProcessStackArgWords` is.
export let PeekPipeStackArgWords = 2 as MachineWord  // args 5-6, both real stack arguments

let CreateProcessInheritHandlesSlot = 0  // arg5 bInheritHandles = FALSE
let CreateProcessCreationFlagsSlot = 1  // arg6 dwCreationFlags = 0
let CreateProcessEnvironmentSlot = 2  // arg7 lpEnvironment = NULL
let CreateProcessCurrentDirSlot = 3  // arg8 lpCurrentDirectory = NULL
let CreateProcessStartupInfoSlot = 4  // arg9 lpStartupInfo
let CreateProcessProcInfoSlot = 5  // arg10 lpProcessInformation
let CreateProcessOutgoingSlots = CreateProcessStackArgWords

// --- P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the IOCP-substrate imports' outgoing stack-argument counts ---
// Stated ONCE per import, exactly as `WriteFileStackArgWords`/`CreateProcessStackArgWords` are — the SAME
// constant sizes the GT-frame reservation (`stdOpOutgoingArgBytes`) AND the syscall shim's per-call copy
// (`gtShimStackArgWords`), so the two can never disagree about how many words each kernel call reads off the
// stack. All are REAL stack arguments the kernel reads (no out-param scratch slot on the stack — ReadFile's
// bytes-read out-param is the 4th argument in r9, a register). EXPORTED because the shim reads them.
export let CreateNamedPipeStackArgWords = 4 as MachineWord  // args 5-8: nOutBufferSize, nInBufferSize, nDefaultTimeOut, lpSecurityAttributes
export let CreateFileStackArgWords = 3 as MachineWord  // args 5-7: dwCreationDisposition, dwFlagsAndAttributes, hTemplateFile
export let CreateThreadStackArgWords = 2 as MachineWord  // args 5-6: dwCreationFlags, lpThreadId
export let ReadFileStackArgWords = 1 as MachineWord  // arg 5: lpOverlapped
// O3: the DebugStream producer's one import that overflows the register file. Stated in this band with its
// five siblings rather than beside the lowering, for the band's own reason — the SAME constant sizes the
// frame reservation and the syscall shim's copy, so the two cannot disagree about the one word the kernel
// reads off the stack.
export let MapViewOfFileStackArgWords = 1 as MachineWord  // arg 5: dwNumberOfBytesToMap

// ⚠ **NOT RESERVED BY `stdOpOutgoingArgBytes`, AND DELIBERATELY STATED ANYWAY (7a).** Unlike its five
// neighbours above, `GetQueuedCompletionStatus` is never emitted as an `iatCall`: the completion loop is
// hand-assembled and calls it DIRECTLY, so no Std op reserves a GT frame for it and there is nothing here
// for a reservation to derive from. It is stated because `gtShimStackArgWordsOf` is exhaustive over
// `IatSlot` and must give it an answer — and the honest answer is its real ABI count, not the 0 its current
// unreachability would excuse. If it is ever routed through the shim, the copy is already right.
export let GetQueuedCompletionStatusStackArgWords = 1 as MachineWord  // arg 5: dwMilliseconds

// `osPipeCreate` reserves the MAX outgoing stack-arg count across its calls (CreateNamedPipeW's 4 dominates
// CreateFileW's 3), so its single GT-frame region holds either call's stack args at `[rsp + shadow + k*8]`.
let PipeCreateOutgoingSlots = CreateNamedPipeStackArgWords

// The `[rsp + disp]` displacement of an OUTGOING stack-argument slot — past the 32-byte shadow space,
// one `SpillSlotBytes`-sized slot per index. The Win64 ABI fact shared by EVERY >4-argument import call the
// isel lowers (WriteFile's 2 stack slots, CreateProcessA's 6): the 5th-and-later arguments land at
// `[rsp + shadow + slot*8]`. `spillSlotBaseDisp` places every spill slot ABOVE `ShadowSpaceBytes +
// outgoingArgSize`, so these never overlap a spill. Computed here (not a top-level const) so the two
// cross-file frame constants are read at runtime — the same reads `computeFrameBytes` makes — rather than
// folded, which a cross-file constant initializer cannot do.
//
// ⭐ **shv2's OWN calls use this SAME region and this SAME formula.** An internal 7th-and-later argument is
// an outgoing stack argument exactly as an import's 5th is, so there is ONE outgoing area per frame and one
// displacement rule for it — not an internal layout beside the Win64 one. v1 instead gave its internal ABI
// a shadow-space-free area at `[rsp + 0]`; shv2 does not, and the reason is that shv2 reserves
// `ShadowSpaceBytes` for EVERY caller unconditionally (`computeFrameBytes`) whether or not it calls an
// import. Starting internal args at `[rsp + 0]` would therefore lay them INSIDE a region whose whole
// definition is "scratch a callee may clobber", and `outgoingArgSize` would have to mean two different
// things depending on which kind of call needed it. Skipping the 32 bytes costs a frame that is already
// reserving them nothing at all.
// ⭐ **EXPORTED: THIS IS THE ONE ADDRESSING RULE FOR AN OUTGOING STACK ARGUMENT, AND TWO OTHER PLACES MUST
// REPRODUCE THE EXACT ADDRESSES IT PLACES (7b2).** The ISEL stores every >4-argument import's stack arguments
// through this; the GT syscall SHIM then copies those same words onto the system stack, and the hand-assembled
// IO completion loop writes `GetQueuedCompletionStatus`'s 5th argument into slot 0 of its own frame. While it
// was file-private both of those respelled it — the shim as an inline sum (with the WRONG stride, which 7a's
// review caught), the loop as the bare literal `0x20`. Three spellings of one rule, on a path where a
// disagreement is uninitialised KERNEL arguments and where no test fails, because the corpus combines no
// `async` with OS calls. Exported so there is one.
export function win64OutgoingArgDisp(slot MachineWord) returns Imm32
	return (ShadowSpaceBytes + slot * SpillSlotBytes) as Imm32
end 'win64OutgoingArgDisp'

// The two words a `call` and the prologue's `push rbp` put between the CALLER's rsp-at-the-call and the
// callee's own rbp: the return address the `call` pushed, then the saved rbp. Named because the incoming
// displacement below is a CLAIM about the prologue's shape, not arithmetic.
let SavedRbpAndReturnAddressBytes = 16 as Imm32

// The `[rbp + disp]` displacement of an INCOMING stack-argument slot — the exact mirror of the caller's
// `[rsp + win64OutgoingArgDisp(slot)]`, shifted by the two words pushed between the two moments. ONE
// derivation from the outgoing rule, so the two ends of a stack argument cannot come to disagree about
// where it is; a hand-written `+48` here would be that disagreement waiting to happen.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS FRAME-SIZE- AND PUSH-COUNT-INDEPENDENT, AND THAT IS WHY `lowerParam` MAY COMPUTE IT.** The
// isel runs long before the allocator decides how many callee-saved registers this function pushes or how
// large its frame is. Reading an incoming argument off RSP would need both. Reading it off RBP needs
// neither — but only because `X64PrologueEpilogue.insertPrologueAtEntry` establishes the frame pointer
// FIRST, before any callee-saved push (it does that for the saved-rbp CHAIN, so the chain has no hole; the
// stack-argument displacement is a second thing that ordering buys). Push the callee-saved registers ahead
// of `mov rbp, rsp` and every displacement here silently gains `8 * pushCount`.
//
// ⭐ **AND IT IS WHY A FRAMELESS LEAF CANNOT MISREAD ITS ARGUMENTS HERE.** Both reference compilers had to
// add a guard forcing a frame onto a leaf that has stack parameters but no locals, spills or calls — v1's
// `FrameScanState.needsFrame`, the bootstrap's `hasStackParams` — because without a prologue `[rbp + 16]`
// reads through the CALLER's frame pointer. shv2 frames EVERY function unconditionally, so there is no such
// leaf and no guard to forget.
function win64IncomingArgDisp(slot MachineWord) returns Imm32
	return SavedRbpAndReturnAddressBytes + win64OutgoingArgDisp(slot)
end 'win64IncomingArgDisp'

// The frame region an import call reserves for its `slotCount` outgoing stack args — the single source of
// BOTH the frame reservation (via `stdOpOutgoingArgBytes`, applied to the function by `lowerFunctionBlocks`)
// and the slot displacements the lowering writes, so the two are ONE fact per call (WriteFile passes
// `WriteFileOutgoingSlots`, CreateProcessA passes `CreateProcessOutgoingSlots`). A FUNCTION rather than a
// const so the `SpillSlotBytes` read is at runtime (see above). NOT exported: this is an x64 frame fact, set
// here in the x64 isel — the target-neutral runtimes deliberately no longer set `outgoingArgSize`.
function win64OutgoingArgBytes(slotCount MachineWord) returns StackSize
	return (slotCount * SpillSlotBytes) as StackSize
end 'win64OutgoingArgBytes'

// The frame bytes ONE shv2-to-shv2 call needs for the arguments that overflow the register files — the
// merged stack slots the shared placement rule assigns, sized by the same `win64OutgoingArgBytes` an import
// call's reservation uses. Given the argument COUNT, `abiStackSlotsBefore` answers "how many of them landed
// on the stack", which is the one number the reservation and the per-argument displacements both derive
// from.
function callOutgoingArgBytes(argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, argCount ParamCount) returns StackSize
	return win64OutgoingArgBytes(abiStackSlotsBefore(argFloatMask, index: argCount, capacity: x64RegisterArgCapacity()))
end 'callOutgoingArgBytes'

// How many arguments of ONE file this ABI passes in registers — the capacity the shared placement rule
// (`abiArgIsOnStack`) is asked against. Read off the array itself, so adding a register to the file moves it
// with no second number to update.
//
// ⚠ THE TWO FILES MUST BE THE SAME DEPTH, and this is where that precondition is checked rather than
// assumed: the rule takes ONE capacity and applies it to whichever file an argument belongs to, so a
// shallower float file would send floats to stack slots the callee still expected in xmm registers — a
// silent wrong answer, and one no existing test could see while every program's arguments fit.
function x64RegisterArgCapacity() returns RegisterArgCapacity
	if argRegisters.count() != argFpRegisters.count() 'filesDiffer'
		panic("x64RegisterArgCapacity: the integer file holds {argRegisters.count()} argument registers and the float file {argFpRegisters.count()} — the shared placement rule asks ONE capacity of both files, so they must be the same depth")
	end 'filesDiffer'
	return argRegisters.count() as RegisterArgCapacity
end 'x64RegisterArgCapacity'

// The physical register integer argument slot `index` occupies. A slot past the file means the placement
// rule said "register" where the file has none, so it is a compiler-invariant break here, not a user error:
// every caller tests `abiArgIsOnStack` first.
// Exported so the GT trampoline (X64GtRuntime) loads its arguments in the SAME register order the ABI
// passes them — the trampoline's arg-load order IS the arg-register order, and a second copy of the
// `[rcx, rdx, rax, r9, rsi, rdi]` list would be the one-fact-written-twice bug this file's header warns of.
export function argRegisterAt(index ParamCount) returns X64Register
	return try argRegisters.get(index) otherwise panic("argRegisterAt: argument index {index} exceeds the {argRegisters.count()} register-argument slots — a slot past the file must have been routed to an outgoing stack slot by `abiArgIsOnStack`")
end 'argRegisterAt'

// The physical XMM register FP argument slot `index` occupies — the float twin of `argRegisterAt`, and a
// compiler-invariant break past the file for the same reason. It must reject cleanly rather than
// mis-storing from the wrong file, which is the latent v1 caller bug this deliberately does not port.
function argFpRegisterAt(index ParamCount) returns X64Register
	return try argFpRegisters.get(index) otherwise panic("argFpRegisterAt: float argument slot {index} exceeds the {argFpRegisters.count()} XMM register-argument slots — a slot past the file must have been routed to an outgoing stack slot by `abiArgIsOnStack`")
end 'argFpRegisterAt'

// The physical register a callIndirect captures its result from — **XMM0** for a float-returning
// function value, **R8** otherwise (P1.5 #78). The indirect twin of `callResultRegister`, but keyed on
// the `resultType` the op carries rather than a callee name (an indirect callee has none). It must agree
// with the value-class column, which classes the result via the SAME `stdTypeIsFloat(resultType)`, so the
// capture `movRegReg` sees two same-file ends (a `movsd` for a float) instead of a cross-file move.
function indirectResultRegister(resultType StdType) returns X64Register
	return X64Register.xmm0 if stdTypeIsFloat(resultType) else X64Register.r8
end 'indirectResultRegister'

// Maps a BRANCHING BLOCK to the predicate the `cmp` fused into its terminator compared with.
// Populated when a fusable `cmp` is lowered and consumed at the `condBranch` terminating that
// block — the two become `cmp` + `jcc`, and no boolean is written to a register. Keyed by
// `BlockRef`, which is function-local, so the map is reset per function. **Why the key is the
// block and not the compare's value is the ⛔⛔ block below** — it was a ValueId until
// 2026-08-02 and that was a wrong answer, not a style choice.
//
// A cmp that is NOT fusable is deliberately ABSENT here, which is what makes
// `lowerCondBranch`'s lookup the single source of truth: a hit means "the flags this branch
// reads were set by the compare that defined its condition", and that fact is established at
// exactly one place (`computeFusedCmps`).
//
// ⛔⛔ **KEYED BY THE BRANCHING BLOCK, NOT BY THE COMPARE'S VALUE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
// CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT.** Fusion is a fact about a *(compare, branch)* PAIR — "the flags this
// `jcc` reads are the ones that `cmp` set" — and a compare's value can feed MORE THAN ONE
// branch (`let sub = e < -1022` tested once here and again fifty ops later). Keyed by ValueId,
// the entry recorded for the first branch answered for the second one too: the later `jcc` was
// emitted BARE, reading whatever flags the intervening calls and compares had left, and
// `computeValuesReadFromRegisters` — which excludes a fused branch's condition — excluded BOTH
// uses, so no `setcc` was emitted anywhere and the boolean existed in no register at all.
// MEASURED as a silent wrong answer: `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s `__float_bitsFromDecimal` took
// its `isSubnormal` branch on a false condition and `0.5` printed as `4503599627370496`.
// A block has exactly ONE terminator, so keyed by the block the fact is exact.
typealias CmpPredMap = Map with (BlockRef, StdCmpPred)

// A block whose float-equality branch was split → the id of the `ordered` block minted for it.
// See `reserveFloatEqualityBlocks`, which is the only writer, and `lowerCondBranch`, the only
// reader. A MISS means "this block's branch is not a float equality", which `try/otherwise`
// says without a sentinel.
//
// It holds the block's ID and not its ref on purpose: the walk reads this while holding a live
// borrow of `targetModule.blocks` (its `dstBlock`), and recovering an id from a ref would need
// a second borrow of that same array — the thing `moveBranchEdgeToSplit` scopes its helpers
// apart to avoid. An id is a scalar and needs no borrow at all.
typealias OrderedBlockIdMap = Map with (BlockRef, BlockId)

// The immediate a boolean condition is tested against. x64 branches on FLAGS, so a
// condition that is a VALUE (a call's `bool` return, a `param`, a short-circuit merge phi,
// a materialized compare) must first be compared with zero: `cmp reg, 0` + `jne` is "is it
// true?". Named because it is a CONTRACT — the same 0/1 domain `setccReg` and the logical
// `not` (`xor reg, 1`) produce.
let BoolFalseImm = 0 as Imm32

// The mask the LOGICAL `not` flips: a bool lives in the 0/1 domain, so complementing it is
// flipping bit 0 — `xor reg, 1` — and NOT the 64-bit one's complement `not` reg (which
// would turn true into -2, still truthy). The INTEGER `not` is a separate Std opcode, so
// this constant can never be applied to one by accident.
let LogicalNotMask = 1 as Imm32

// The two FIXED GPRs a materialized float `!=` builds its two halves in — the `setne` answer and
// the `setp` (unordered) answer, which `appendFloatInequality` then sums with an `lea`. See
// `materializeFloatEquality` for why the halves cannot live in virtuals.
//
// NOTHING pins these two in particular: any pair of DISTINCT caller-saved GPRs would serve, so
// they are named here rather than picked at the use site. RCX and RDX are the pair `lowerShiftCl`
// and `lowerDivMod` already reach for, which keeps the set of registers this backend pins to fixed
// roles as small as possible. They must be distinct — the `lea` sums them.
//
// Writing them is a PHYSICAL DEF, which is what makes them safe: liveness forbids each to every
// value live ACROSS that write, so the fold cannot destroy anything a later op still needs.
let FloatInequalityUnequalReg = X64Register.rcx
let FloatInequalityUnorderedReg = X64Register.rdx

// Map a Std comparison predicate to the x64 condition code its `jcc`/`setcc` tests. ONE table
// per FAMILY (not one encoder per predicate), and it drives BOTH consumers of a predicate: the
// fused `jcc` and the materializing `setcc`. They therefore cannot disagree about what `<`
// means.
//
// ⭐ THE FAMILY IS CHOSEN BY THE OPERAND TYPE, NOT BY THE PREDICATE — and it always was. It
// only looked otherwise while every Std compare was a signed i64. `cmp` on two i64s is a
// subtraction that answers in OF/SF, which the SIGNED JL/JG family reads. `ucomisd` leaves
// OF=SF=AF=0 always and answers in ZF/PF/CF — the same three flags an UNSIGNED compare writes
// — so a float compare branches with the UNSIGNED JB/JA family, and the same `StdCmpPred.less`
// lowers to `jl` for an i64 and (via the operand swap) `ja` for an f64. A float compare
// lowered to `jl` would read flags `ucomisd` never wrote.
// `isFloat` rather than the whole `StdType`, and the distinction is the ONLY thing the operand
// type decides here — `cmp` vs `ucomisd` is a flag-semantics question, not a width one. Taking
// the projection lets the FUSED-branch caller answer it from a dense bool column instead of
// carrying a full type per compare that every reader would throw all but one bit of away (see
// `fusedFloatCmps`). The contract's point stands unchanged: the family is chosen by the operand
// type and never by the predicate.
function condCodeForPred(pred StdCmpPred, isFloat bool) returns X64CondCode
	if isFloat 'float'
		return floatCondCodeForPred(pred)
	end 'float'
	return signedCondCodeForPred(pred)
end 'condCodeForPred'

// The SIGNED integer condition codes — every Std integer is a signed i64.
function signedCondCodeForPred(pred StdCmpPred) returns X64CondCode
	return match pred 'map'
		eq gives X64CondCode.equal
		ne gives X64CondCode.notEqual
		lt gives X64CondCode.less
		gt gives X64CondCode.greater
		le gives X64CondCode.lessEqual
		ge gives X64CondCode.greaterEqual
	end 'map'
end 'signedCondCodeForPred'

// The UNSIGNED condition codes — the family `ucomisd` answers in (ZF/PF/CF), and the exact
// mirror of `signedCondCodeForPred`. `cmp`'s signed family reads OF/SF, which `ucomisd` leaves
// at 0 always, so a float compare must read this one.
function unsignedCondCodeForPred(pred StdCmpPred) returns X64CondCode
	return match pred 'map'
		eq gives X64CondCode.equal
		ne gives X64CondCode.notEqual
		lt gives X64CondCode.below
		gt gives X64CondCode.above
		le gives X64CondCode.belowEqual
		ge gives X64CondCode.aboveEqual
	end 'map'
end 'unsignedCondCodeForPred'

// ⭐⭐ THE ONE TABLE THE WHOLE FLOAT COMPARE LOWERING IS DERIVED FROM: the predicate a float
// compare is ACTUALLY emitted as, once its operands have been put in the order `ucomisd` needs.
//
// A float `a < b` is emitted as `ucomisd b, a` + `ja` — i.e. it is lowered AS `b > a`. So this
// maps `lt` to `gt` and `le` to `ge`, and leaves the other four alone. Both facts the lowering
// needs fall out of it: WHETHER to swap (`floatCmpSwapsOperands` = "did this table move the
// predicate?") and WHICH condition code (`floatCondCodeForPred` = the unsigned code OF the
// swapped predicate). They cannot disagree, because there is only one of them.
//
// ⭐ WHY SWAP AT ALL — IT BUYS THE NaN ANSWER FOR NOTHING. IEEE-754 requires every relational
// predicate be FALSE when either operand is NaN, and `ucomisd` SETS CF on unordered precisely so
// the `above` family gets that free: `ja` needs CF=0 and `jae` needs CF=0, so both are NOT TAKEN
// on a NaN — correct, with no parity test. The `below` family is exactly the opposite: `jb` needs
// CF=1, which unordered SETS, so `a < b` lowered to `jb` would be TAKEN on a NaN and answer TRUE
// where IEEE demands FALSE. `jbe` is worse still — unordered sets ZF too.
//
// ⇒ AND THAT IS NOW STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN REMEMBERED: this table's RANGE never contains `lt` or
// `le`, so `floatCondCodeForPred` can never produce `below`/`belowEqual`. The NaN-wrong family is
// UNREACHABLE for floats by construction, not by a rule someone has to keep in mind.
//
// `==`/`!=` map to themselves and are not helped: they are SYMMETRIC, so swapping their operands
// changes nothing at all. They need the parity bit instead.
//
// ⚠ A `jb` lowering PASSES the obvious test — `3.5 < 9.5` is true either way. Only a NaN operand
// tells the two apart, which is what `lt-nan-is-false` exists for.
function floatCmpEmittedPred(pred StdCmpPred) returns StdCmpPred
	return match pred 'p'
		lt gives StdCmpPred.gt
		le gives StdCmpPred.ge
		eq gives StdCmpPred.eq
		ne gives StdCmpPred.ne
		gt gives StdCmpPred.gt
		ge gives StdCmpPred.ge
	end 'p'
end 'floatCmpEmittedPred'

// The FLOAT condition code: the UNSIGNED code of the predicate actually emitted. DERIVED, so it
// cannot drift from the operand order — see `floatCmpEmittedPred`.
//
// ⚠ `eq`/`ne` ARE NOT COMPLETE ON THEIR OWN. Both alias with UNORDERED — `ucomisd` reports a NaN
// as ZF=PF=CF=1, which is "equal" in every bit but PF — so the parity bit is a SECOND condition
// that no single condition code can carry. `lowerFloatEqualityBranch` supplies it as a second
// BLOCK; `materializeFloatEquality` folds it into the byte. This gives only the ORDERED half of
// those two, and is wrong if used without the other.
function floatCondCodeForPred(pred StdCmpPred) returns X64CondCode
	return unsignedCondCodeForPred(floatCmpEmittedPred(pred))
end 'floatCondCodeForPred'

// Whether a float compare emits its operands SWAPPED — true exactly when the lowering had to
// move the predicate to reach the NaN-correct family. DERIVED from the same one table, so the
// swap and the condition code are one decision and not two that must agree.
function floatCmpSwapsOperands(pred StdCmpPred) returns bool
	return floatCmpEmittedPred(pred) != pred
end 'floatCmpSwapsOperands'

// `==`/`!=` — the two SYMMETRIC predicates, and exactly the two the operand swap cannot help.
// They are the float compares that need the parity bit, and therefore a second jump.
function predicateIsEquality(pred StdCmpPred) returns bool
	return match pred 'p'
		eq gives true
		ne gives true
		lt gives false
		gt gives false
		le gives false
		ge gives false
	end 'p'
end 'predicateIsEquality'

// The register-FILE doors (`regClassForStdType`, `unaryResultClass`, `returnTypeResultClass`,
// `setValueClass`) and the whole value-class-column producer are TARGET-INDEPENDENT and live in
// `Targets/Shared/StdLoweringShared.maxon`, shared with the arm64 lowering — `lowerStdToX64` calls
// `buildValueClassColumns`. See that file for the walk and why it must not be forked.

// ⚠ THE WHOLE SSE LOWERING IS SCALAR-DOUBLE, AND THIS IS WHERE IT SAYS SO. `movsd`, `addsd`,
// `divsd`, `ucomisd` and an 8-byte `.rdata` island: every one of them is the `sd` (scalar-double)
// form. An `f32` is FOUR bytes and needs the `ss` family throughout, so it is a real GAP and not a
// spelling — and it would fail SILENTLY in the worst place: `lowerFloatConst` sizes its constant
// island from the TYPE (4 bytes for an f32) while `movsdRegRip` unconditionally loads EIGHT,
// reading half of whatever constant was laid down next.
//
// Nothing produces one — Maxon's `float` IS f64, and the shared `accessWidthFor` says the same thing
// for the memory band — so this is unreachable today. It is asserted at each float lowering entry
// anyway, because `stdTypeIsFloat` (the test that routes values here) is true of f32, so the day
// one exists this fails loudly at the door instead of quietly in the constant pool.
function requireScalarDouble(t StdType, what String)
	if t != StdType.f64 'notDouble'
		panic("{what}: {t.name} reached the SSE lowering, which is scalar-DOUBLE throughout (movsd/addsd/ucomisd + an 8-byte .rdata island) — an f32 needs the `ss` family and a 4-byte island, and would otherwise load 8 bytes out of a 4-byte constant. Maxon's `float` is f64, so nothing produces one.")
	end 'notDouble'
end 'requireScalarDouble'

// The successor UNORDERED decides for — the target of the split's FIRST block `jp`.
//
// `nan == nan` is FALSE, so an unordered `==` takes the ELSE edge. `nan != nan` is TRUE — IEEE's
// one predicate that HOLDS on unordered — so an unordered `!=` takes the THEN edge. That is the
// whole of what the parity jump decides, and it is why the two are mirror images rather than one
// jump with a different condition code.
function parityTargetOf(pred StdCmpPred, thenBlockId BlockId, elseBlockId BlockId) returns BlockId
	return match pred 'p'
		eq gives elseBlockId
		ne gives thenBlockId
		lt to ge panic("parityTargetOf: {pred.name} is an ORDERED float predicate — it is answered by a single `ja`/`jae` on swapped operands and has no parity jump at all (see floatCmpSwapsOperands)")
	end 'p'
end 'parityTargetOf'

// Record `condId` as fusable, but only when `pred`'s compare is the one that DEFINES it.
//
// NO `Optional`-shaped return anywhere in this analysis, and that is a MEASURED constraint
// rather than a style preference. A payload-carrying union is HEAP-BOXED in Maxon (see
// StdDialect's header), and the natural spelling of the two questions this analysis asks —
// "what value does this compare define?" and "what predicate does it carry?" — asks them
// ONCE PER STD OP, which puts a fresh heap object on every op of every function the backend
// lowers. The first cut of this pass did exactly that, and `scale-test` charged it ~3,500
// allocations on the SMALLEST rung. So every classifier here either returns a `bool` or
// writes its answer through an accumulator it is handed, and allocates nothing.
// Both columns are written HERE, by this one call, which is what keeps them in lockstep — the
// predicate and the float-ness of the compare carrying it can never disagree, because nothing
// can write one without the other.
function fuseIfDefines(fused CmpPredMap, floatCmps StdValueUseSet, blockRef BlockRef, defId ValueId, condId ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType)
	if defId != condId 'someOtherCompare'
		return
	end 'someOtherCompare'
	// The PREDICATE is recorded against the branching BLOCK (see `CmpPredMap`); the FLOAT-NESS
	// stays against the compare's VALUE, and the asymmetry is exact rather than convenient. "Which
	// flags does this jcc read?" is a question about a pair, so only a block can answer it; "is the
	// compare defining v a float compare?" is a property of v's one defining op and cannot differ
	// between readers. Every reader of the second column reaches it through a hit in the first.
	fused.upsert(blockRef, value: pred)
	if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
		floatCmps.insert(condId)
	end 'float'
end 'fuseIfDefines'

// The three group-2 shift instructions x64 has, and the whole of what the shift lowering needs
// to know about a Std shift opcode. Named for the INSTRUCTIONS rather than for the opcodes,
// because the mapping between the two is exactly the fact worth stating once: `shl`→SHL,
// `shr`→SAR (arithmetic — a signed left operand), `shrLogical`→SHR (zero-filling — an unsigned
// one). See `shiftInstructionOf`.
enum ShiftInstruction
	shl
	sar
	shr
end 'ShiftInstruction'

// The label prefix every f64 constant island carries: an immutable IEEE bit pattern the code only
// ever loads, so it belongs in read-only `.rdata`.
//
// ⚠ THE PREFIX DOES NOT PUT IT THERE — the RELOCATION KIND does. A float island lands in `.rdata`
// because it is filed as `RdataRelocKind.ripRelDisp32`, and a global lands in `.data` because it is
// filed as `dataSectionRipRelDisp32`; the label's text is never consulted on the way. This comment
// used to say the routing was by NAME, crediting a `classifyGlobalLabel` that had no callers and was
// deleted; `DataLabelPrefix` in SignatureIndex.maxon records the measurement that settled it. So the
// prefix buys READABILITY in a golden fragment, and nothing else.
let FloatConstantLabelPrefix = "__fconst_"

// The label every `ordered` block carries — the second half of a split float equality (see
// `reserveFloatEqualityBlocks`). A file-scope constant, beside `CritSplitLabel`'s reasoning: one
// slice, shared by every split, rather than a fresh allocation per branch on the lowering's path.
let FloatOrderedLabel = b"fordered"

// The x86 instruction an atomic ADD lowers to, chosen by the fate of its PRIOR value (F2). Two
// instructions do the same atomic update and differ only in whether they hand the old word back:
// `lock xadd` does and needs its addend IN the destination (a two-address form, hence a reuse-def),
// `lock add` does not and needs no destination at all. See `lowerAtomicRmw` for why the discarding
// form is worth a second path — it is a saved REGISTER on the refcount path, not a saved byte.
function x64AtomicAdd(resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, operandId ValueId, priorValueIsRead bool) returns TargetOp
	if not priorValueIsRead 'priorValueDiscarded'
		return TargetOp.x64LockAdd(TargetVReg.virtual(addrId), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(operandId))
	end 'priorValueDiscarded'
	return TargetOp.x64LockXadd(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), src: TargetVReg.virtual(operandId), addrReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId))
end 'x64AtomicAdd'

// `globalData` is threaded in because a float CONSTANT is `.rdata` content, and `.rdata` is
// resolved at concat time against this same table — the one the `.data` globals already live in.
// Registering here (where the constant is first seen) and resolving there (where sections are laid
// out) is exactly the split `leaRegGlobal` already uses for `.data`; the float path reuses it
// rather than growing a second table with a second offset index.
// ⭐ IT RETURNS THE CLASS COLUMN WITH THE MODULE, because the two are one artifact: the Target tier
// carries no types, so a `TargetModule` alone does not say which file any of its values lives in,
// and the allocator cannot recover it (see `ValueClassColumn`). Handing them back together is what
// makes it impossible to have one without the other.
export function lowerStdToX64(stdModule StdModule, target Target, globalData GlobalDataTable, funcNames FunctionNameIndex) returns (TargetModule, ValueClassColumns)
	var lowering = StdToX64Lowering.create(stdModule, target: target, globalData: globalData, funcNames: funcNames)
	lowering.prepareSkeleton()
	// BY INDEX, never by iteration: `lowerFunction` MUTATES the function it lowers — the
	// float-equality split appends to its `blockRefs` and bumps its `nextBlockId` — so no
	// `srcFunc` borrowed out of `stdModule.functions` may be handed to it. This is the rule
	// `splitCriticalEdges` states for the identical reason, and the borrow checker enforces it
	// (E3019).
	for funcIdx in 0 upto stdModule.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		lowering.lowerFunction(funcIdx)
	end 'eachFunc'

	// AFTER the walk, not during it: the column is sized by `scanFunctionValueCount` over the
	// TARGET ops, so every op has to exist before it can be measured. `buildValueClassColumns` is the
	// shared, target-independent producer (see `Targets/Shared/StdLoweringShared.maxon`); bind it
	// FIRST so its borrow of `targetModule` closes before the tuple moves `targetModule` out.
	let valueClasses = buildValueClassColumns(lowering.stdModule, targetModule: lowering.targetModule, funcNames: lowering.funcNames)
	return (lowering.targetModule, valueClasses)
end 'lowerStdToX64'

type StdToX64Lowering
	// Exported like `targetModule`: `lowerStdToX64` hands both to the shared `buildValueClassColumns`.
	export var stdModule as StdModule
	// The compilation target — read for its OS by the three OS-primitive lowerings
	// (`lowerOsExit`/`lowerOsWriteStdout`/`lowerOsAllocPages`), which route through `x64OsCallKind` to a
	// Windows IAT call or a raw Linux syscall. The arm64 isel carries the same field for the same reason.
	var target as Target
	// The backend's data table — where a float constant's `.rdata` island is registered. Shared
	// with concat, which resolves each `movsdRegRip`'s label against it once sections are laid out.
	var globalData as GlobalDataTable
	// Exported so the free-function entry can read the finished module after
	// driving the per-function loop (M5's fan-out driver reads it too).
	export var targetModule as TargetModule
	// Per-function `cmp`-value → predicate table for the fused compare/branch
	// lowering. Reset at the start of each function (ValueIds are function-local).
	var cmpPreds as CmpPredMap
	// ⭐ THE FUSED COMPARES WHOSE OPERANDS ARE FLOATS — `cmpPreds`' second column, written at the
	// same instant by the same call (`fuseIfDefines`).
	//
	// It stores the QUESTION ITS READERS ACTUALLY ASK, and that is the whole design. Both of them
	// — the condition-code family and the parity split — need to know only whether the compare is
	// a float one; nobody asks f32-vs-f64 or i8-vs-i64. A `Map with (ValueId, StdType)` here would
	// store a full type per compare so that every reader could immediately throw all but one bit
	// of it away, and MEASURED it cost ~4.7 allocations PER FUSED COMPARE — ~1,300 of a +1,528
	// rung-0 delta — on a corpus that contains no float at all. A column nobody reads is not free.
	//
	// A dense bool column (`StdValueUseSet`), so an all-integer function inserts NOTHING and the
	// backing array is never allocated: the cost is now zero for programs that use no floats,
	// rather than proportional to their compares.
	//
	// ⚠ IT IS A SUBSET OF `cmpPreds`' KEYS AND IS ONLY EVER CONSULTED AFTER A `cmpPreds` HIT. That
	// is what makes `settleFloatEqualityCondition`'s un-fuse safe without removing from it (there is no
	// `remove` on a dense set): dropping the `cmpPreds` entry makes the entry here unreachable,
	// because every reader gates on the predicate lookup first.
	var fusedFloatCmps as StdValueUseSet
	// The `ordered` block minted for each float-equality branch — see OrderedBlockIdMap and
	// `reserveFloatEqualityBlocks`. Reset per function with the two columns above.
	var orderedBlockIds as OrderedBlockIdMap
	// Every value this function reads AS A VALUE — out of a REGISTER, rather than as the EFLAGS
	// a fused branch tests. A compare whose result lands in here needs a `setcc` to write its
	// boolean into a register; one that does not stays in the flags.
	//
	// It is deliberately NOT narrowed to just the compare results. The only thing that ever
	// asks is a compare, about ITSELF, so narrowing it could not change a single answer — and
	// would cost a second full walk over every op of every function to do it.
	//
	// Computed once per function, BEFORE the op walk, because a compare is lowered long before
	// the phi edge or the later `if` that reads it is reached. INDEPENDENT of `cmpPreds`: a
	// compare can be both fused (its own branch reads its flags) AND read as a value (a `let`
	// binds it), and then it emits `cmp` + `setcc` + `jcc` — sound because `setcc` and `movzx`
	// READ the flags rather than writing them.
	var valuesReadFromRegisters as StdValueUseSet
	// The module's CHECKED name index (`FunctionNameIndex`, A2l), built by `eliminateDeadFunctions` over the
	// array it left behind and threaded down from `buildBackend`. It answers "what register FILE does this
	// callee's result come back in?" — XMM0 for a float-returning callee, R8 otherwise (see
	// `callResultRegister`) — WITHOUT a second name-keyed map: `calleeResultClass` reads the callee's own
	// `returnType` through it, so a float result's CAPTURE register and its allocation CLASS are the one
	// fact rather than two that could disagree. Exported like `targetModule`: `lowerStdToX64` reads it to
	// thread the same index into `buildValueClassColumns`.
	export let funcNames as FunctionNameIndex
	// Callee name → its parameters' register FILES as a bitmask. Built ONCE here (not per call site), FROM
	// the index above — so its key set is the checked one — and read by `emitCallArgMoves` to route each
	// argument to a GPR or an XMM arg register by the callee's declared parameter type. It is a real FOLD
	// over a parameter list rather than a field read, which is why it stays a map where the result-class
	// map did not. Only the argument routing consults it — the value-class column already classes a `param`
	// result from its own carried f64 type — so it is not threaded into `buildValueClassColumns`.
	var calleeParamClasses as CalleeParamClassMap

	static function create(stdModule StdModule, target Target, globalData GlobalDataTable, funcNames FunctionNameIndex) returns StdToX64Lowering
		return Self{stdModule: stdModule, target: target, globalData: globalData, targetModule: TargetModule.create(), cmpPreds: CmpPredMap.create(), fusedFloatCmps: StdValueUseSet.create(), orderedBlockIds: OrderedBlockIdMap.create(), valuesReadFromRegisters: StdValueUseSet.create(), funcNames: funcNames, calleeParamClasses: buildCalleeParamClasses(funcNames)}
	end 'create'

	// The value-class column producer — the pass that answers "what register file does this op's
	// result land in?" — is TARGET-INDEPENDENT and lives in `Targets/Shared/StdLoweringShared.maxon`
	// (`buildValueClassColumns`), shared with the arm64 lowering. `lowerStdToX64` calls it after the
	// op walk. It is NOT a method here anymore: a value's class is what its Std TYPE says, identically
	// on both targets, so forking it is exactly the drift that file's header warns against.

	// Whole-module setup: relocate every StdModule block 1:1 into `targetModule.blocks`
	// (preserving the flat-block indices `IrFunction.blockRefs` references) and
	// transfer every function (its StdReturnType / paramTypes carry across
	// unchanged). Must run before any lowerFunction call.
	//
	// Both `blockArgs` (the phis) AND `branchEdges` (each predecessor's per-phi
	// incoming values) are carried across: the register allocator consumes them
	// DIRECTLY and does SSA destruction after coloring (EliminatePhis is retired),
	// so the phi model must survive into the Target tier intact.
	//
	// WHAT THE TARGET TIER OWNS, AND WHY. It owns each block's `BranchEdgeArray` and
	// the `BranchEdge` objects in it, because it REPLACES them: `extractPredEdge`
	// rebuilds the array to move an edge onto a split block, and `rewriteEdgeArgsIn`
	// swaps an edge's `argIds` for a private copy before repointing it at a reload.
	// Both are field assignments, so they must land on Target-owned objects.
	// It does NOT own `blockArgs` or the `argIds` arrays — nothing here mutates those
	// in place, so they are SHARED with the Std tier (and through it the Maxon module
	// the parse memo holds). See IrBlock's header for the full ownership rules.
	function prepareSkeleton()
		for srcBlock in self.stdModule.blocks 'relocateBlocks'
			var dstBlock = IrBlock.create(srcBlock.id, label: srcBlock.label)
			dstBlock.blockArgs = srcBlock.blockArgs
			for srcEdge in srcBlock.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
				dstBlock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(srcEdge.targetBlockId, argIds: srcEdge.argIds))
			end 'eachEdge'
			self.targetModule.blocks.push(dstBlock)
		end 'relocateBlocks'

		for srcFunc in self.stdModule.functions 'transferFuncs'
			self.targetModule.functions.push(srcFunc)
		end 'transferFuncs'
	end 'prepareSkeleton'

	// The OUTGOING stack-argument bytes an `StdOp`'s x64 lowering needs — for an IMPORT call, the Win64
	// arguments past the fourth (`osWriteStdout`'s `lpOverlapped` + its bytes-written scratch,
	// `osProcessSpawn`'s six); for a shv2-to-shv2 CALL, the arguments past the six register slots of their file.
	// Every other op lowers register-only or in-frame → 0. This is an x64 ABI fact — arm64's `write` is
	// all-register, and its files are eight deep — so it lives in the x64 isel, and `lowerFunctionBlocks` folds
	// it to a per-function max. EXHAUSTIVE over `StdOp`, so a band-appended op cannot slip in unclassified.
	//
	// A METHOD rather than a free function because the four CALL arms need a mask: an indirect or witness call
	// carries its own on the op, and a DIRECT call's comes from the callee's declared parameter files, which is
	// `self.calleeParamClasses`.
	function stdOpOutgoingArgBytes(op StdOp) returns StackSize
		return match op 'o'
			// Both stream writes marshal the SAME `WriteFile` call, so both reserve the same two slots.
			osWriteStdout gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(WriteFileOutgoingSlots)
			osWriteStderr gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(WriteFileOutgoingSlots)
			// A frame address is a `lea`; it calls nothing and so passes no outgoing argument.
			stackRecordAddr gives 0
			// O3: `GetEnvironmentVariableA` (3 arguments) then `OpenFileMappingA` (3) are register-only, and
			// the tick read takes none at all.
			osOpenNamedSegment gives 0
			osTickCountMs gives 0
			// ⚠⚠ **`MapViewOfFile` TAKES FIVE, SO ITS `dwNumberOfBytesToMap` IS A REAL OUTGOING STACK
			// ARGUMENT AND THE FRAME MUST RESERVE THE SLOT.** `lowerOsMapNamedSegment` writes it at
			// `win64OutgoingArgDisp(0)`; reserving nothing puts `spillSlotBaseDisp`'s SPILL SLOT 0 at that
			// exact displacement, so the store lands on a spilled value — the silent stack corruption this
			// band's own header exists to prevent.
			osMapNamedSegment gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(MapViewOfFileStackArgWords)
			// CreateProcessA passes its last six of ten arguments on the stack (see `win64OutgoingArgDisp`).
			// Every other op of the ORIGINAL subprocess band is ≤4-argument and register-only.
			osProcessSpawn gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(CreateProcessOutgoingSlots)
			// PeekNamedPipe passes its last two of six arguments on the stack — so `osProcessSpawn` above is
			// not the only stack-arg subprocess op, and this comment used to claim it was.
			osPipePeek gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(PeekPipeStackArgWords)
			osHandleSetInherit gives 0
			// ⚠ **THIS ARM NOW COVERS TWELVE OPS, AND ITS COMMENT ONCE SAID "the other three appended ops".**
			// `osEnvRead` and `osProcessTerminate` finish the attached-subprocess band, then the eight
			// `ws2_32` socket ops follow, then `osCpuCount` and `osThreadCpuTicks` close it. All twelve are
			// register-only because the widest Win64 call any of them marshals takes FOUR arguments —
			// `getaddrinfo`, `send` and `recv`
			// — which is exactly the register file's capacity, so nothing overflows onto the stack.
			// ⛔ A ws2_32 call with a fifth argument (`WSARecv`, `WSASend`) would belong on its OWN arm with
			// a reserved slot count, never in here.
			osEnvRead to osThreadCpuTicks gives 0
			// F1a: `VirtualFree(MEM_DECOMMIT)` is three register arguments — inside the Win64 register file's
			// capacity — so it reserves no outgoing stack space. Its Linux twin is a three-argument syscall,
			// which never uses the stack at all.
			osDecommitPages gives 0
			// F1b: the byte fill calls nothing at all — it is a store ladder emitted inline — so there is no
			// outgoing-argument region for it to reserve.
			memFill gives 0
			// F1c: `VirtualAlloc` is four register arguments — exactly the Win64 register file's capacity — and
			// the Linux `mmap` is a SIX-argument syscall, which passes all six in registers and never uses the
			// stack. So neither the reserve nor the commit reserves outgoing stack space.
			osCommitPages to osReservePages gives 0
			// F2: the two atomics are single inline instructions and reserve nothing. The thread token is one
			// inline load on Windows, and on Linux a ZERO-argument `gettid` syscall — a syscall never touches
			// the stack, so the largest of the three still reserves no outgoing-argument region.
			atomicCas to currentThreadToken gives 0
			const to param gives 0
			// The two NAME-callee calls: the callee's declared parameter files decide which arguments overflow,
			// exactly as they decide which register each of the others travels in (`emitCallArgMoves` reads the
			// same mask through the same `calleeArgFloatMask`).
			call(_, callee, args) gives callOutgoingArgBytes(calleeArgFloatMask(self.calleeParamClasses, callee: callee, argCount: args.count(), registerCapacity: x64RegisterArgCapacity()), argCount: args.count())
			retVoid to osAllocPages gives 0
			rdataAddr gives 0
			tryCall(_, _, callee, args) gives callOutgoingArgBytes(calleeArgFloatMask(self.calleeParamClasses, callee: callee, argCount: args.count(), registerCapacity: x64RegisterArgCapacity()), argCount: args.count())
			// `errorReturn`/`osExit` have no outgoing args; a `funcAddr` is a register-only `lea`.
			errorReturn to funcAddr gives 0
			// The two VALUE-callee calls carry their own per-argument mask (there is no callee name to look one
			// up by), and overflow through the identical shared rule.
			callIndirect(_, _, _, argFloatMask, args) gives callOutgoingArgBytes(argFloatMask, argCount: args.count())
			osPanic to osSleepMs gives 0
			// The four ≤4-argument subprocess ops (spawn is named above) reserve no outgoing stack space.
			// `osFreePages`'s `VirtualFree(addr, 0, MEM_RELEASE)` is three register args, so it reserves none either.
			osProcessExitCode to osFreePages gives 0
			// The jump table makes no call — it reserves no outgoing argument stack.
			switch gives 0
			witnessCall(_, _, _, _, argFloatMask, args) gives callOutgoingArgBytes(argFloatMask, argCount: args.count())
			// The throwing witness call marshals its arguments identically (its extra result is a REGISTER
			// capture, not an outgoing argument), so it reserves the same outgoing space.
			witnessTryCall(_, _, _, _, _, argFloatMask, args) gives callOutgoingArgBytes(argFloatMask, argCount: args.count())
			// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the three >4-argument IOCP calls reserve their stack-arg region (osPipeCreate the
			// max across CreateNamedPipeW/CreateFileW; osThreadCreate CreateThread's 2; osReadOverlapped ReadFile's 1);
			// osProcessSpawnInherit is CreateProcessA, the same six as osProcessSpawn. The rest are ≤4-argument (register
			// only): osIocpCreate/osEventCreate/osLockInit/osLockEnter/osLockLeave/osLastError reserve none.
			osPipeCreate gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(PipeCreateOutgoingSlots)
			osThreadCreate gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(CreateThreadStackArgWords)
			osReadOverlapped gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(ReadFileStackArgWords)
			osProcessSpawnInherit gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(CreateProcessOutgoingSlots)
			osIocpCreate to osEventCreate gives 0
			osLockInit to osLockLeave gives 0
			osLastError gives 0
			// CancelIoEx (2 args) and GetCurrentProcessId (0 args) are register-only — no outgoing stack space.
			osCancelIo to osGetPid gives 0
			// P1.5 dogfood slice 1b: the stdin pipe reserves the same CreateNamedPipeW/CreateFileW region as
			// `osPipeCreate`; `osWriteFile` reserves ONE word for WriteFile's `lpOverlapped` (its bytes-written
			// out-param is a caller-supplied pointer passed in r9, not a stack scratch like `osWriteStdout`'s).
			osPipeCreateOutbound gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(PipeCreateOutgoingSlots)
			osWriteFile gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(WriteFileStackArgWords)
			// GetSystemTimeAsFileTime takes ONE argument, in rcx — register-only, no outgoing stack space.
			osReadWallClock gives 0
			// R4.1: CreateFileA takes SEVEN arguments — four in registers, three on the stack.
			osFileOpenRead gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(CreateFileStackArgWords)
			// ⚠ `CloseHandle` takes ONE argument, in rcx — register-only, like `osCancelIo` two arms up. It
			// used to reserve `CreateFileStackArgWords` here, which was three dead frame words per file-using
			// function and a comment claiming a shape the call does not have (R4.2).
			osFileClose gives 0
			// R4.2. `ReadFile` is `WriteFile`'s mirror (one stack word, `lpOverlapped`); `CreateFileA` for the
			// write open is the read open's seven. The remaining five are ≤4-argument register-only calls:
			// `GetFileSizeEx`/`FindFirstFileA` take two, `MoveFileExA` three, `DeleteFileA`/`FindClose` one.
			osReadFile gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(ReadFileStackArgWords)
			osFileOpenWrite gives win64OutgoingArgBytes(CreateFileStackArgWords)
			osFileSize to osFileAttributes gives 0
			// A rendered panic calls `mrt_panic` with one REGISTER argument, exactly as `osPanic` does.
			osPanicValue gives 0
			// `GetCommandLineA` takes NO arguments at all, so nothing lands on the stack.
			osCommandLine gives 0
			// W5: `GetStdHandle` takes one argument and `GetModuleFileNameA` three, so both are
			// register-only under Win64's rcx/rdx/r8/r9 and neither lands anything on the stack.
			osStdHandle gives 0
			osExecutablePath gives 0
		end 'o'
	end 'stdOpOutgoingArgBytes'

	// Per-function lowering: walk the function's blocks and lower each op into the
	// matching target block (indexed by the same blockRef the function carries).
	// The function's StdReturnType drives whether the return materializes an R8 move.
	// THREE STRICTLY SEQUENTIAL PHASES, and the split between them is a BORROW boundary rather
	// than a stylistic one: phase 2 MUTATES the function (it appends blocks), so neither the reads
	// before it nor the walk after it may hold a borrow of it across it. The function is
	// therefore threaded by INDEX and re-fetched inside each phase — the discipline
	// `splitCriticalEdgesInFunc` follows for the same reason, and which E3019 enforces.
	function lowerFunction(funcIdx FuncIndex)
		self.prepareFunctionTables(funcIdx)

		// ⚠ CAPTURED BEFORE PHASE 2, which appends its `ordered` refs to this very array. The walk
		// must cover exactly the blocks the STD module holds: an `ordered` block exists only in
		// `targetModule.blocks`, so its ref is out of range in `stdModule.blocks` and the walk
		// would panic on it — and it is already emitted COMPLETE, so revisiting it would lower the
		// original block's ops into it a second time.
		let originalBlockCount = self.stdBlockCountOf(funcIdx)
		self.orderedBlockIds = self.reserveFloatEqualityBlocks(funcIdx)
		// The op walk returns the OUTGOING stack-argument bytes the function's x64 lowering needs (nonzero
		// only for a `__print_string`, whose `WriteFile` lowering has two stack arguments). Applied to the
		// TARGET function AFTER the walk, in a scope holding no `srcFunc` borrow of the same object.
		let outgoingArgBytes = self.lowerFunctionBlocks(funcIdx, originalBlockCount: originalBlockCount)
		self.applyOutgoingArgSize(funcIdx, bytes: outgoingArgBytes)
	end 'lowerFunction'

	// Record the function's outgoing stack-argument reservation on the TARGET function. This is set by
	// the x64 ISEL — not the target-neutral String runtime — because the need is an x64 ABI fact (arm64's
	// `write` is all-register and reserves nothing). Skips the assignment when zero, so a non-printing
	// function is untouched.
	function applyOutgoingArgSize(funcIdx FuncIndex, bytes StackSize)
		if bytes == 0 'none'
			return
		end 'none'
		var func = self.targetModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		func.outgoingArgSize = bytes
	end 'applyOutgoingArgSize'

	// Phase 1 — the per-function analyses, all READS. Fresh tables each time: a `cmp`'s ValueId
	// only means anything within its own function, and both the fused branch and the value uses
	// that consume it are in the same function. All are computed BEFORE the op walk, because a
	// compare is lowered long before the branch or the phi edge that decides what it must produce.
	function prepareFunctionTables(funcIdx FuncIndex)
		let srcFunc = self.stdModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		self.cmpPreds = CmpPredMap.create()
		self.fusedFloatCmps = StdValueUseSet.create()
		self.computeFusedCmps(srcFunc, fused: self.cmpPreds, floatCmps: self.fusedFloatCmps)
		self.valuesReadFromRegisters = self.computeValuesReadFromRegisters(srcFunc)
	end 'prepareFunctionTables'

	// The function's block count as the STD module knows it — a scoped read, so the caller holds
	// no borrow of the function across the mint that follows.
	function stdBlockCountOf(funcIdx FuncIndex) returns BlockCount
		let srcFunc = self.stdModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		return srcFunc.blockRefs.count()
	end 'stdBlockCountOf'

	// Phase 3 — the op walk. `originalBlockCount` bounds it to the Std module's own blocks; see
	// `lowerFunction`.
	function lowerFunctionBlocks(funcIdx FuncIndex, originalBlockCount BlockCount) returns StackSize
		let srcFunc = self.stdModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		// The max outgoing stack-arg bytes any op in this function needs — a running MAX (a call REUSES the
		// region, so it is the largest single call's need, not their sum). An import call past four arguments
		// and a shv2 call past six of one file both contribute. Accumulated from the op already in hand, so no
		// extra pass, and returned for `lowerFunction` to record once the walk's borrow is released.
		var outgoingArgBytes = 0 as StackSize
		// This function's OWN parameter files, folded ONCE — every `param` op then reads a scalar instead of
		// re-walking the parameter types, which is what the per-parameter `computeParamSlotIndex` did (Θ(params²)
		// per function, for an answer that never changes within one).
		let paramFloatMask = floatParamMaskOf(srcFunc.paramTypes)
		for i in 0 upto originalBlockCount 'eachBlock'
			let blockRef = try srcFunc.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("lowerStdToX64: blockRefs.get OOB at {i} despite the captured bound")
			let srcBlock = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("lowerStdToX64: blockRef from srcFunc.blockRefs not in stdModule.blocks")
			var dstBlock = try self.targetModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("lowerStdToX64: targetModule.blocks 1:1 with stdModule.blocks but get({blockRef}) failed")

			for opIdx in srcBlock.opRefs 'eachOp'
				let stdOp = try self.stdModule.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("lowerStdToX64: opIdx from srcBlock.opRefs not in stdModule.ops")
				let opOutgoing = self.stdOpOutgoingArgBytes(stdOp)
				if opOutgoing > outgoingArgBytes 'largerArgRegion'
					outgoingArgBytes = opOutgoing
				end 'largerArgRegion'
				self.lowerBodyOp(stdOp, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: srcFunc.returnType, paramFloatMask: paramFloatMask)
			end 'eachOp'

			if srcBlock.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
				let termOp = try self.stdModule.ops.get(srcBlock.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("lowerStdToX64: terminatorIndex not in stdModule.ops")
				self.lowerTerminatorOp(termOp, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: srcFunc.returnType, blockRef: blockRef)
			end 'hasTerm'
		end 'eachBlock'

		return outgoingArgBytes
	end 'lowerFunctionBlocks'

	// ==========================================================================
	// Compare/branch fusion — which compares keep their boolean in the FLAGS, and
	// which must write it to a REGISTER
	// ==========================================================================

	// The compares whose EFLAGS the branch terminating their own block will read — keyed to
	// the predicate that branch's `jcc` must test. `lowerCondBranch` fuses exactly the
	// condition values found here and nothing else.
	//
	// PURELY LOCAL, and that is precisely what makes it sound: x64 EFLAGS survive neither a
	// block boundary nor an intervening flag-clobbering instruction, so the flags a `jcc`
	// reads are ALWAYS those of the LAST flag-clobbering op before it. Fusion is legal
	// exactly when that op is the compare whose result the branch is testing — no more, no
	// less. Nothing about how many OTHER things read the compare enters into it (a compare
	// that is both fused and materialized emits `cmp` + `setcc` + `jcc`, which is correct
	// because `setcc`/`movzx` read the flags rather than writing them).
	//
	// This is the guard that used to be a `panic` ("`condBranch` on a value no `cmp`
	// defined"): a boolean condition that is a call result, a parameter or a merge phi has
	// no flags to fuse, and it is now LOWERED (`cmp reg, 0` + `jne`) rather than rejected.
	//
	// ⚠ THE PRECONDITION NOBODY HAD WRITTEN DOWN, AND THE WHOLE THING RESTS ON IT: this scan
	// runs on STD ops, but the REGISTER ALLOCATOR later inserts TARGET ops into the very
	// window it just proved safe — the gap between a fused `cmp` and its `jcc`. The fusion
	// survives only because **every op the allocator can insert there is flag-neutral**:
	//
	//     storeSlotReg · loadRegSlot (spill/reload) · movRegReg (phi copies, reuse copies) ·
	//     xchgRegReg (permutation cycles) · a REMATERIALIZED `const` (`constMaterializationOp`,
	//     a bare `mov r, imm`)
	//
	// Every one is a `mov`-class instruction that writes no EFLAGS. (`prologue`/`epilogue` DO
	// write flags — they are a `sub`/`add rsp` — but they land at a function's entry and exit
	// blocks, never inside a compare/branch window.)
	//
	// ⇒ **IF A FLAG-WRITING OP IS EVER ADDED TO WHAT THE ALLOCATOR CAN INSERT, THIS FUSION
	// BECOMES A SILENT MISCOMPILE** — a `jcc` would read flags that op had already destroyed,
	// and the Std-tier scan cannot see it coming, because the op does not exist at Std tier.
	// The likeliest candidate is a spill/reload growing an address computation, or a memzero
	// (whose size ladder `cmp`s — see ARCHITECTURE.md). Re-check this list then.
	// Fills BOTH fusion columns in one walk — see `fusedFloatCmps` for why the float-ness is a
	// column beside the predicate rather than a field inside it. They are handed in rather than
	// returned so the pair can only be created together.
	function computeFusedCmps(func IrFunction, fused CmpPredMap, floatCmps StdValueUseSet)
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("computeFusedCmps: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in stdModule.blocks")
			if not block.hasTerminatorOp() 'noTerm'
				continue
			end 'noTerm'
			let term = try self.stdModule.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("computeFusedCmps: terminatorIndex not in stdModule.ops")
			match term 'term'
				condBranch(condId, _, _) then self.recordFusableCompare(block, blockRef: blockRef, condId: condId, fused: fused, floatCmps: floatCmps)
				const to mod then break 'term'
				branch to rdataAddr then break 'term'
				// `funcAddr`/`callIndirect` are value-producing body ops, never a block terminator — a
				// non-`condBranch` terminator fuses nothing, so like every other arm here it breaks (P1.5-A1).
				tryCall to osFreePages then break 'term'
				// The jump table does its OWN `cmp` against the bounds count — it fuses no preceding compare.
				switch then break 'term'
				// A witness call is a call — like `tryCall` it is not the compare, so it fuses nothing. The
				// throwing form is a call for the identical reason.
				witnessCall then break 'term'
				witnessTryCall then break 'term'
				// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the IOCP ops are value-consuming/void body ops, never terminators.
				osPipeCreate to osWriteFile then break 'term'
				// The wall-clock read is a void body op, never a terminator.
				osReadWallClock then break 'term'
				// R4.1 + R4.2: every file op is a value-producing or void body op, never a terminator.
				osFileOpenRead to osFileAttributes then break 'term'
				// A panic is a body op, never a terminator, under either spelling.
				osPanicValue then break 'term'
				// A command-line read is a value-producing body op, never a terminator.
				osCommandLine then break 'term'
				// W5: a handle read and a path fill are value-producing body ops, never terminators.
				osStdHandle then break 'term'
				osExecutablePath then break 'term'
				// W35: a stream write is a value-producing body op, never a terminator.
				osWriteStderr then break 'term'
				// A frame address is a value-producing body op, never a terminator.
				stackRecordAddr then break 'term'
				osOpenNamedSegment to osTickCountMs then break 'term'
				osHandleSetInherit to osThreadCpuTicks then break 'term'
				// F1a: a decommit is a body op, never a terminator — nothing to fuse.
				osDecommitPages then break 'term'
				// F1b: a byte fill is a body op, never a terminator — nothing to fuse.
				memFill then break 'term'
				// F1c: a reserve and a commit are body ops, never terminators — nothing to fuse.
				osCommitPages to osReservePages then break 'term'
				// F2: the two atomics and the thread token are value-producing body ops, never terminators.
				atomicCas to currentThreadToken then break 'term'
			end 'term'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'computeFusedCmps'

	// Record `condId` as fusable iff the LAST flag-clobbering body op of `block` is a
	// compare that DEFINES it. Walked backward from the end of the body, because that is
	// the order the flags are overwritten in.
	function recordFusableCompare(block IrBlock, blockRef BlockRef, condId ValueId, fused CmpPredMap, floatCmps StdValueUseSet)
		var i = block.opRefs.count()
		while i > 0 'scanBackward'
			i = i - 1
			let opIdx = try block.opRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("recordFusableCompare: opRefs.get OOB at {i}")
			let op = try self.stdModule.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("recordFusableCompare: opIdx {opIdx} not in stdModule.ops")
			if not op.rawValue.clobbersFlags 'flagsPreserved'
				continue
			end 'flagsPreserved'

			// The first flag-clobbering op found walking backward IS the one whose flags the
			// branch will read. If it is not the compare being tested, nothing is fusable —
			// and the scan stops HERE rather than looking further back, because whatever was
			// there had its flags overwritten by this very op. The two compare variants answer
			// identically: `foldConstOperands` moved the right operand into an immediate, which
			// changed the instruction, not what the compare means.
			match op 'isTheCompare'
				cmp(defId, _, _, pred, operandType) then fuseIfDefines(fused, floatCmps: floatCmps, blockRef: blockRef, defId: defId, condId: condId, pred: pred, operandType: operandType)
				// `cmpImm` is INTEGER BY CONSTRUCTION — no SSE form takes an immediate, so
				// `foldConstOperands` refuses a float rhs and a float compare can never reach this
				// variant. It used to be spelled `StdType.i64` HERE, which was the right answer to
				// "is it a float?" and a guess at the width beside it; the op now carries its own
				// `operandType` (X5) and this asks it rather than re-deciding.
				cmpImm(defId, _, _, pred, operandType) then fuseIfDefines(fused, floatCmps: floatCmps, blockRef: blockRef, defId: defId, condId: condId, pred: pred, operandType: operandType)
				const to unaryOp then break 'isTheCompare'
				binOpImm then break 'isTheCompare'
				// Extended past `retVoid` to take in the memory band, and past `storeIndirect` to take in
				// the system band. It is reached only for an op that already answered `clobbersFlags:
				// true`, and no memory op does — so the memory arms are unreachable in practice and must
				// still be NAMED rather than swallowed: the day a flag-writing memory op exists, "is it
				// the compare?" is a question to answer here.
				//
				// ⚠ `osAllocPages`/`osWriteStdout` are NOT in that unreachable set — they declare
				// `clobbersFlags: true`, so either genuinely lands here as the last flag-writer before a
				// branch, exactly as `call` already does. `break` is the right answer for the same reason
				// it is for a call: it is not the compare, so the scan stops and no fusion is recorded.
				// Declining a fusion is always safe. (`rdataAddr` never reaches here — the loop guards on
				// `clobbersFlags` and it sets none — but it is named rather than swallowed.)
				div to rdataAddr then break 'isTheCompare'
				// `callIndirect` declares `clobbersFlags: true` (it is a call), so it CAN genuinely land here
				// as the last flag-writer before a branch, exactly as `call`/`tryCall` do — and like them it
				// is not the compare, so the scan stops and no fusion is recorded (declining is always safe).
				// `funcAddr` sets no flags (a `lea`), so the loop guard skips it before it reaches here — it
				// is named rather than swallowed. (P1.5-A1)
				tryCall to osFreePages then break 'isTheCompare'
				switch then break 'isTheCompare'
				// A witness call is a call, not the compare — the scan stops and records no fusion (safe). The
				// throwing form is a call for the identical reason.
				witnessCall then break 'isTheCompare'
				witnessTryCall then break 'isTheCompare'
				// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the IOCP ops declare `clobbersFlags: true` (they lower to calls), so one
				// CAN land here as the last flag-writer before a branch; like a `call`, it is not the compare, so
				// the scan stops and no fusion is recorded (declining is always safe).
				osPipeCreate to osWriteFile then break 'isTheCompare'
				// The wall-clock read declares `clobbersFlags: true` (it lowers to a call), so it CAN be the
				// last flag-writer before a branch; like a `call` it is not the compare, so the scan stops.
				osReadWallClock then break 'isTheCompare'
				// R4.1 + R4.2: every file op declares `clobbersFlags: true` (each lowers to a call), so like the
				// wall-clock read one can be the last flag-writer before a branch — and, like a `call`, it is
				// not the compare, so the scan stops.
				osFileOpenRead to osFileAttributes then break 'isTheCompare'
				// A call, not the compare — the scan stops, exactly as it does at `osPanic`.
				osPanicValue then break 'isTheCompare'
				// An import call, not the compare — the scan stops.
				osCommandLine then break 'isTheCompare'
				// W5: import calls too, and not the compare — the scan stops.
				osStdHandle then break 'isTheCompare'
				osExecutablePath then break 'isTheCompare'
				// W35: an import call or a syscall, not the compare — the scan stops.
				osWriteStderr then break 'isTheCompare'
				// A frame address, not the compare — the scan stops.
				stackRecordAddr then break 'isTheCompare'
				osOpenNamedSegment to osTickCountMs then break 'isTheCompare'
				osHandleSetInherit to osThreadCpuTicks then break 'isTheCompare'
				// F1a: a decommit declares `clobbersFlags: true` (it lowers to a call or a syscall), so it CAN
				// land here as the last flag-writer before a branch; like a `call`, it is not the compare, so the
				// scan stops and records no fusion (declining is always safe).
				osDecommitPages then break 'isTheCompare'
				// ⛔ F1b: the fill ladder `cmp`s the length to pick its arm, so it declares `clobbersFlags: true`
				// and CAN land here as the last flag-writer before a branch — which is precisely the hazard that
				// declaration exists to prevent. It is not the compare, so the scan stops and fuses nothing.
				memFill then break 'isTheCompare'
				// F1c: the reserve and the commit are calls or syscalls, so either can be the last flag-writer
				// before a branch; like a `call`, neither is the compare, so the scan stops.
				osCommitPages to osReservePages then break 'isTheCompare'
				// ⛔ F2: all three declare `clobbersFlags: true` and every one of them CAN be the last
				// flag-writer before a branch — `lock cmpxchg` sets ZF (that IS its answer), `lock xadd` sets the
				// arithmetic flags, and the Linux thread token is a syscall. None is the compare, so the scan
				// stops and fuses nothing; declining is always safe, and fusing across any of them would read
				// flags the atomic has since overwritten.
				atomicCas to currentThreadToken then break 'isTheCompare'
			end 'isTheCompare'
			return
		end 'scanBackward'
	end 'recordFusableCompare'

	// The compare results this function reads as a VALUE — each needs a `setcc` to put its
	// boolean in a register. A value use is any operand of any op, or any phi branch-edge
	// argument, EXCEPT the condition of a branch that `computeFusedCmps` already resolved to
	// read that compare's FLAGS (which is a use of the flags, not of a register).
	//
	// The exception is per-BLOCK, not per-value, and it has to be: one compare's result can
	// feed the `condBranch` of its own block (fused, no register needed) AND the `condBranch`
	// of a LATER block (`if r … end / if r … end`), where the flags are long gone and the
	// value must be in a register. Skipping every `condBranch` use unconditionally would miss
	// that second one and leave the branch reading a value nothing ever wrote.
	function computeValuesReadFromRegisters(func IrFunction) returns StdValueUseSet
		var readAsValue = StdValueUseSet.create()
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("computeValuesReadFromRegisters: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in stdModule.blocks")

			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachBodyOp'
				let op = try self.stdModule.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("computeValuesReadFromRegisters: opIdx {opIdx} not in stdModule.ops")
				collectStdOpUses(op, referenced: readAsValue)
			end 'eachBodyOp'

			// A phi operand is a genuine register read: SSA destruction turns it into a `mov`
			// on the edge, and a compare feeding one (the RHS of a short-circuit `and`/`or`)
			// must therefore have been materialized.
			for edge in block.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
				for argId in edge.argIds 'eachArg'
					readAsValue.insert(argId)
				end 'eachArg'
			end 'eachEdge'

			self.collectTerminatorValueUses(block, blockRef: blockRef, readAsValue: readAsValue)
		end 'eachBlock'

		return readAsValue
	end 'computeValuesReadFromRegisters'

	// Add the terminator's value uses to `readAsValue` — unless it is a `condBranch` whose
	// condition this block FUSES, in which case it consumes EFLAGS and not a register.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE MEMBERSHIP TEST IS THE WHOLE QUESTION, because the map is keyed by BLOCK** (see
	// `CmpPredMap`): an entry exists for exactly those blocks whose terminator is a `condBranch`
	// that `recordFusableCompare` PROVED reads its compare's flags. This used to be a match over
	// every `StdOp` variant asking "is this terminator a fused condBranch?", and every arm but one
	// answered `false` — the arms were re-deriving what a lookup already knows, off a key that
	// could not tell this block's branch from another block's.
	function collectTerminatorValueUses(block IrBlock, blockRef BlockRef, readAsValue StdValueUseSet)
		if not block.hasTerminatorOp() 'noTerm'
			return
		end 'noTerm'
		if self.cmpPreds.contains(blockRef) 'flagsOnly'
			return
		end 'flagsOnly'
		let term = try self.stdModule.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("collectTerminatorValueUses: terminatorIndex not in stdModule.ops")
		collectStdOpUses(term, referenced: readAsValue)
	end 'collectTerminatorValueUses'

	// Lower a regular (non-terminator) Std op. `ret` reaching here is the rare
	// mid-block form (LowerMaxonToStd emits it as a body op for a partially-
	// terminated block); it lowers the same as the terminator form, just appended.
	function lowerBodyOp(op StdOp, dstBlock IrBlock, returnType StdReturnType, paramFloatMask ArgFloatMask)
		match op 'lower'
			const(resultId, value, valueType) then self.lowerConst(resultId, value: value, valueType: valueType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			binOp(resultId, lhs, rhs, opcode, operandType) then self.lowerBinOp(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			unaryOp(resultId, operand, opcode, operandType) then self.lowerUnaryOp(resultId, operand: operand, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			cmp(resultId, lhs, rhs, pred, operandType) then self.lowerCmp(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// `binOpImm`'s `operandType` (X5) is a WIDTH, and every x64 integer op `lowerBinOpImm`
			// selects is 64-bit whatever the Std type says — `lea`, the 3-operand `imul`, the word ALU
			// forms, an imm8 shift count — because a narrow value arrives zero-extended in a full
			// register. Nothing in that lowering can read the field, so it is dropped rather than
			// threaded into a body that would ignore it. It is read by the wasm backend, whose locals
			// are typed.
			//
			// ⚠ `cmpImm`'s is THREADED, and the asymmetry is the point: `lowerCmpImm` reaches
			// `materializeCompareIfRead`, which asks `stdTypeIsFloat` — the same question
			// `recordFusableCompare` asks of the same field one screen up. A dropped field there meant a
			// restated `StdType.i64` inside, i.e. the answer written twice.
			binOpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, opcode, _) then self.lowerBinOpImm(resultId, lhs: lhs, imm: imm, opcode: opcode, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			cmpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, pred, operandType) then self.lowerCmpImm(resultId, lhs: lhs, imm: imm, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			div(resultId, lhs, rhs, signedness) then self.lowerDivMod(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, signedness: signedness, resultReg: X64Register.rax, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			mod(resultId, lhs, rhs, signedness) then self.lowerDivMod(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, signedness: signedness, resultReg: X64Register.rdx, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			param(resultId, index, paramType) then self.lowerParam(resultId, index: index, paramType: paramType, paramFloatMask: paramFloatMask, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			call(resultId, callee, args) then self.lowerCall(resultId, callee: callee, args: args, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			ret(retValId) then self.lowerReturn(retValId, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: false)
			retVoid then self.lowerVoidReturn(dstBlock, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: false)
			condBranch then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `condBranch` appeared as a body op (lowering bug — a branch is always a block terminator)")
			branch then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `branch` appeared as a body op (lowering bug — a branch is always a block terminator)")
			globalAddr(resultId, label) then self.lowerGlobalAddr(resultId, label: label, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			loadIndirect(resultId, addrId, offset, valueType) then self.lowerLoadIndirect(resultId, addrId: addrId, offset: offset, valueType: valueType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			storeIndirect(addrId, offset, valueId, valueType) then self.lowerStoreIndirect(addrId, offset: offset, valueId: valueId, valueType: valueType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osAllocPages(resultId, sizeId) then self.lowerOsAllocPages(resultId, sizeId: sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osWriteStdout(resultId, bufId, lenId) then self.lowerOsWriteStream(resultId, bufId: bufId, lenId: lenId, stream: OsOutputStream.standardOutput, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			rdataAddr(resultId, label) then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegRdata(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), label: label))
			tryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, callee, args) then self.lowerTryCall(resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, callee: callee, args: args, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			errorReturn(retValId, errorFlagId) then self.lowerErrorReturn(retValId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: false)
			osExit(codeId) then self.lowerOsExit(codeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// P1.5-A1: a function's address (`lea dest, [rip+func]`), and a call THROUGH a function value.
			funcAddr(resultId, funcName) then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegFunc(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), funcName: funcName))
			callIndirect(resultId, calleeReg, resultType, argFloatMask, args) then self.lowerCallIndirect(resultId, calleeReg: calleeReg, args: args, resultType: resultType, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osPanic(label) then self.lowerPanic(label, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// P1.5 mid-body-yield: the green-thread sleep substrate's clock read and OS wait.
			osReadClock(ticksPtrId, freqPtrId) then self.lowerOsReadClock(ticksPtrId, freqPtrId: freqPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osSleepMs(msId) then self.lowerOsSleepMs(msId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// P1.5 subprocess seed: the green-thread SUBPROCESS substrate's spawn/wait/exit-code/close.
			osProcessSpawn(cmdlinePtrId, cwdPtrId, creationFlagsId, startupPtrId, procInfoPtrId) then self.lowerOsProcessSpawn(cmdlinePtrId, cwdPtrId: cwdPtrId, creationFlagsId: creationFlagsId, startupPtrId: startupPtrId, procInfoPtrId: procInfoPtrId, inheritHandles: false, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osHandleSetInherit(handleId) then self.lowerOsHandleSetInherit(handleId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osPipePeek(resultId, handleId, availPtrId) then self.lowerOsPipePeek(resultId, handleId: handleId, availPtrId: availPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osEnvRead(resultId, namePtrId, bufPtrId, bufLenId) then self.lowerOsEnvRead(resultId, namePtrId: namePtrId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, bufLenId: bufLenId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osProcessTerminate(handleId, exitCodeId) then self.lowerOsProcessTerminate(handleId, exitCodeId: exitCodeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osSocketStartup(resultId, wsaDataPtrId) then self.lowerOsSocketStartup(resultId, wsaDataPtrId: wsaDataPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osSocketResolve(resultId, hostPtrId, hintsPtrId, outPtrId) then self.lowerOsSocketResolve(resultId, hostPtrId: hostPtrId, hintsPtrId: hintsPtrId, outPtrId: outPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osSocketFreeAddrInfo(addrInfoPtrId) then self.lowerOsSocketFreeAddrInfo(addrInfoPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osSocketCreate(resultId) then self.lowerOsSocketCreate(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osSocketConnect(resultId, sockId, addrPtrId) then self.lowerOsSocketConnect(resultId, sockId: sockId, addrPtrId: addrPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osSocketSend(resultId, sockId, bufPtrId, lenId) then self.lowerOsSocketSend(resultId, sockId: sockId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, lenId: lenId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osSocketRecv(resultId, sockId, bufPtrId, lenId) then self.lowerOsSocketRecv(resultId, sockId: sockId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, lenId: lenId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osSocketClose(sockId) then self.lowerOsSocketClose(sockId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// The MACHINE query behind `__Builtins.cpuCount()`; the clamp is builder-built Std, not here.
			osCpuCount(resultId) then self.lowerOsCpuCount(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osThreadCpuTicks(outPtrId) then self.lowerOsThreadCpuTicks(outPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osProcessExitCode(handleId, codePtrId) then self.lowerOsProcessExitCode(handleId, codePtrId: codePtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osWaitHandles(handleArrayPtrId, countId, timeoutMsId) then self.lowerOsWaitHandles(handleArrayPtrId, countId: countId, timeoutMsId: timeoutMsId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osCloseHandle(handleId) then self.lowerOsCloseHandle(handleId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osWaitHandle(resultId, handleId, timeoutMsId) then self.lowerOsWaitHandle(resultId, handleId: handleId, timeoutMsId: timeoutMsId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// P1.5-B1a′ relocating morestack: release a completed GT's (possibly grown) stack. F1a widened this to
			// the full page-lifecycle trio, which is NOT x64-windows-gated: `osAllocPages` runs on every lane, so
			// its free/decommit/recommit twins must too.
			osFreePages(addrId, sizeId) then self.lowerOsFreePages(addrId, sizeId: sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// The dense-match jump table is ALWAYS a block terminator, never a body op — see `lowerTerminatorOp`.
			switch then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `switch` op appeared as a body op (lowering bug — the jump table is always a block terminator)")
			// P1.7a slice 2a: the fused witness call — arg moves then a memory-indirect `call [witnessAddr + off]`.
			witnessCall(resultId, witnessAddr, methodIndex, resultType, argFloatMask, args) then self.lowerWitnessCall(resultId, witnessAddr: witnessAddr, methodIndex: methodIndex, args: args, resultType: resultType, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			witnessTryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, witnessAddr, methodIndex, resultType, argFloatMask, args) then self.lowerWitnessTryCall(resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, witnessAddr: witnessAddr, methodIndex: methodIndex, args: args, resultType: resultType, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the IOCP overlapped-read substrate's OS-import ops.
			osPipeCreate(namePtrId, saPtrId, iocpId, readPtrId, writePtrId) then self.lowerOsPipeCreate(namePtrId, saPtrId: saPtrId, iocpId: iocpId, readPtrId: readPtrId, writePtrId: writePtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osIocpCreate(resultId) then self.lowerOsIocpCreate(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osEventCreate(resultId) then self.lowerOsEventCreate(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osThreadCreate(resultId, entryPtrId) then self.lowerOsThreadCreate(resultId, entryPtrId: entryPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osLockInit(lockPtrId) then self.lowerOsWin64Call1(IatSlot.InitializeCriticalSection, arg0: lockPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osLockEnter(lockPtrId) then self.lowerOsWin64Call1(IatSlot.EnterCriticalSection, arg0: lockPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osLockLeave(lockPtrId) then self.lowerOsWin64Call1(IatSlot.LeaveCriticalSection, arg0: lockPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osReadOverlapped(resultId, handleId, bufId, capId, bytesOutPtrId, ovlId) then self.lowerOsReadOverlapped(resultId, handleId: handleId, bufId: bufId, capId: capId, bytesOutPtrId: bytesOutPtrId, ovlId: ovlId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osLastError(resultId) then self.lowerOsLastError(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osProcessSpawnInherit(cmdlinePtrId, cwdPtrId, creationFlagsId, startupPtrId, procInfoPtrId) then self.lowerOsProcessSpawn(cmdlinePtrId, cwdPtrId: cwdPtrId, creationFlagsId: creationFlagsId, startupPtrId: startupPtrId, procInfoPtrId: procInfoPtrId, inheritHandles: true, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osCancelIo(handleId, ovlId) then self.lowerOsCancelIo(handleId, ovlId: ovlId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osGetPid(resultId) then self.lowerOsGetPid(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// P1.5 dogfood slice 1b: the streaming subprocess stdin pipe + synchronous write.
			osPipeCreateOutbound(namePtrId, saPtrId, childReadPtrId, parentWritePtrId) then self.lowerOsPipeCreateOutbound(namePtrId, saPtrId: saPtrId, childReadPtrId: childReadPtrId, parentWritePtrId: parentWritePtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osWriteFile(resultId, handleId, bufId, lenId, bytesOutPtrId) then self.lowerOsWriteFile(resultId, handleId: handleId, bufId: bufId, lenId: lenId, bytesOutPtrId: bytesOutPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// The `__Builtins` WALL clock: read the host's calendar into the caller's out-param word.
			osReadWallClock(ftPtrId) then self.lowerOsReadWallClock(ftPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osOpenNamedSegment(resultId, namePtrId, bufPtrId, bufLenId) then self.lowerOsOpenNamedSegment(resultId, namePtrId: namePtrId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, bufLenId: bufLenId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osMapNamedSegment(resultId, handleId, sizeId) then self.lowerOsMapNamedSegment(resultId, handleId: handleId, sizeId: sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osTickCountMs(resultId) then self.lowerOsTickCountMs(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// R4.1: open a file for reading; the raw handle lands in `resultId`.
			osFileOpenRead(resultId, pathPtrId) then self.lowerOsFileOpenRead(resultId, pathPtrId: pathPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// R4.1: close the handle. `CloseHandle`'s BOOL is discarded, so nothing is captured from RAX.
			osFileClose(handleId) then self.lowerOsFileClose(handleId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// R4.2: the rest of the file-IO primitives. Each routes through `requireWin64Import` for the
			// x64-windows gate, then emits its one import call.
			osReadFile(resultId, handleId, bufId, lenId, bytesOutPtrId) then self.lowerOsReadFile(resultId, handleId: handleId, bufId: bufId, lenId: lenId, bytesOutPtrId: bytesOutPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osFileOpenWrite(resultId, pathPtrId) then self.lowerOsFileOpenWrite(resultId, pathPtrId: pathPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osFileSize(resultId, handleId, sizeOutPtrId) then self.lowerOsFileSize(resultId, handleId: handleId, sizeOutPtrId: sizeOutPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osFileDelete(resultId, pathPtrId) then self.lowerOsFileDelete(resultId, pathPtrId: pathPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osFileRename(resultId, oldPathPtrId, newPathPtrId) then self.lowerOsFileRename(resultId, oldPathPtrId: oldPathPtrId, newPathPtrId: newPathPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osFileFindFirst(resultId, pathPtrId, findDataPtrId) then self.lowerOsFileFindFirst(resultId, pathPtrId: pathPtrId, findDataPtrId: findDataPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osFileFindClose(handleId) then self.lowerOsFileFindClose(handleId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// R4.3: the four directory primitives. Each routes through the same `requireWin64Import` gate
			// and emits one register-only import call whose RAX answer is captured.
			osFileFindNext(resultId, handleId, findDataPtrId) then self.lowerOsFileFindNext(resultId, handleId: handleId, findDataPtrId: findDataPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osDirCreate(resultId, pathPtrId) then self.lowerOsDirCreate(resultId, pathPtrId: pathPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osDirCurrentPath(resultId, bufLenId, bufPtrId) then self.lowerOsDirCurrentPath(resultId, bufLenId: bufLenId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osFileAttributes(resultId, pathPtrId) then self.lowerOsFileAttributes(resultId, pathPtrId: pathPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osPanicValue(messagePtrId) then self.lowerPanicValue(messagePtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osCommandLine(resultId) then self.lowerOsCommandLine(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osStdHandle(resultId, whichId) then self.lowerOsStdHandle(resultId, whichId: whichId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osExecutablePath(resultId, bufLenId, bufPtrId) then self.lowerOsExecutablePath(resultId, bufLenId: bufLenId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// W35: the same graph as the stdout write, one selector/descriptor over.
			osWriteStderr(resultId, bufId, lenId) then self.lowerOsWriteStream(resultId, bufId: bufId, lenId: lenId, stream: OsOutputStream.standardError, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// A promoted record's address: `lea dest, [rsp + slotDisp]`, the frame-slot mirror of `globalAddr`'s
			// `lea [rip + disp]`. The slot's displacement is not known until the encoder (see `TargetOp.leaRegSlot`).
			stackRecordAddr(resultId, slot) then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegSlot(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), slot: slot))
			// F1a: the decommit twin of the page release above. Both routes are real on this backend —
			// `VirtualFree(MEM_DECOMMIT)` and `madvise(MADV_DONTNEED)` — so neither is gated.
			osDecommitPages(addrId, sizeId) then self.lowerOsDecommitPages(addrId, sizeId: sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// F1b: the byte fill's size ladder — two pre-moves and the macro-op that IS the dispatch.
			memFill(destId, byteCountId, value) then self.lowerMemFill(destId, byteCountId: byteCountId, value: value, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// F1c: the two halves of the reserve/commit split. Both routes are real on both OSes this backend
			// serves, so neither is gated — see the two lowerings for what each OS is actually asked.
			osCommitPages(resultId, addrId, sizeId) then self.lowerOsCommitPages(resultId, addrId: addrId, sizeId: sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			osReservePages(resultId, sizeId) then self.lowerOsReservePages(resultId, sizeId: sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// F2: the atomics and the per-thread identity word. Neither atomic is OS-routed — a `lock` prefix is
			// the ISA's, not the platform's — while the token IS, because only one of the two OSes here has a
			// thread-pointer register a raw static binary may read (see `lowerCurrentThreadToken`).
			atomicCas(resultId, addrId, expectedId, replacementId) then self.lowerAtomicCas(resultId, addrId: addrId, expectedId: expectedId, replacementId: replacementId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			atomicRmw(resultId, addrId, operandId, opcode) then self.lowerAtomicRmw(resultId, addrId: addrId, operandId: operandId, opcode: opcode, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			currentThreadToken(resultId) then self.lowerCurrentThreadToken(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		end 'lower'
	end 'lowerBodyOp'

	// Lower a block terminator. M4 adds the two branch terminators; `ret` stays. A
	// `const`/`binOp`/`unaryOp`/`cmp` terminator is a lowering bug (none is a terminator).
	function lowerTerminatorOp(op StdOp, dstBlock IrBlock, returnType StdReturnType, blockRef BlockRef)
		match op 'lower'
			ret(retValId) then self.lowerReturn(retValId, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: true)
			retVoid then self.lowerVoidReturn(dstBlock, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: true)
			// `blockRef` is what `lowerCondBranch` looks its reserved `ordered` block up by — the
			// float-equality split is decided per BLOCK, before the walk, not per op.
			condBranch(condId, thenBlockId, elseBlockId) then self.lowerCondBranch(condId, thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId, dstBlock: dstBlock, blockRef: blockRef)
			branch(targetBlockId) then self.lowerBranch(targetBlockId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// The dense-match jump table lowers to the x64 jump-table target op, registering its `.rdata`
			// slot blob. Routed through a helper so the `denseTargetBlockIds` Array is not bound in this big
			// match (the tail-slot-Array corruption `lowerIndirectCall` documents; here `defaultBlockId`
			// follows it, but the helper keeps the discipline uniform).
			switch then self.lowerSwitch(op, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			const then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `const` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a constant is never a terminator)")
			binOp then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `binOp` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a binary op is never a terminator)")
			unaryOp then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `unaryOp` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a unary op is never a terminator)")
			cmp then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `cmp` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a comparison is never a terminator)")
			binOpImm then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `binOpImm` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — an arithmetic op is never a terminator)")
			cmpImm then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `cmpImm` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a comparison is never a terminator)")
			div then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `div` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a division is never a terminator)")
			mod then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `mod` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a modulo is never a terminator)")
			param then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `param` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a parameter is an entry body op)")
			call then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `call` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a call is a body op)")
			globalAddr to storeIndirect then panic("lowerStdToX64: a memory-band op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a global's address, load and store are all body ops)")
			osAllocPages then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osAllocPages` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a page allocation is a body op)")
			osWriteStdout then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osWriteStdout` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a stdout write is a body op)")
			rdataAddr then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `rdataAddr` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — an rdata address is a value-producing body op)")
			tryCall then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `tryCall` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a throwing call is a value-producing body op)")
			errorReturn(retValId, errorFlagId) then self.lowerErrorReturn(retValId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: true)
			osExit then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osExit` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a process exit is a body op followed by a dead return)")
			funcAddr then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `funcAddr` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a function address is a value-producing body op)")
			callIndirect then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `callIndirect` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — an indirect call is a value-producing body op)")
			osPanic then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osPanic` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a range panic is a body op followed by a dead return)")
			osReadClock then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osReadClock` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a clock read is a value-consuming body op)")
			osSleepMs then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osSleepMs` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — an OS sleep is a body op)")
			osProcessSpawn to osWaitHandle then panic("lowerStdToX64: a P1.5 subprocess op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — spawn/wait/exit-code/close are all body ops)")
			osHandleSetInherit to osProcessTerminate then panic("lowerStdToX64: an attached-subprocess host primitive appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — the inherit mark, the pipe peek, the env read and the terminate are all body ops)")
			osSocketStartup to osSocketClose then panic("lowerStdToX64: a socket host primitive appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — every socket primitive is a body op, never a terminator)")
			osFreePages then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osFreePages` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a page release is a body op)")
			witnessCall then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `witnessCall` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a witness call is a value-producing body op)")
			witnessTryCall then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `witnessTryCall` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a throwing witness call is a value-producing body op)")
			osPipeCreate to osWriteFile then panic("lowerStdToX64: a P1.5 IOCP op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — pipe/iocp/event/thread/lock/read/last-error/spawn/cancel/pid are all body ops)")
			osReadWallClock then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osReadWallClock` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a wall-clock read is a value-consuming body op)")
			osFileOpenRead to osFileAttributes then panic("lowerStdToX64: a file-IO op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — every one is a body op)")
			osPanicValue then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osPanicValue` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a rendered panic is a body op followed by a dead return, exactly like `osPanic`)")
			osCommandLine then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osCommandLine` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a command-line read is a value-producing body op)")
			osStdHandle then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osStdHandle` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a standard-handle read is a value-producing body op)")
			osExecutablePath then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osExecutablePath` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — an executable-path read is a value-producing body op)")
			osWriteStderr then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osWriteStderr` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a stderr write is a body op, exactly like its stdout twin)")
			stackRecordAddr then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `stackRecordAddr` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a frame address is a value-producing body op)")
			osOpenNamedSegment to osTickCountMs then panic("lowerStdToX64: a DebugStream host primitive appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — all three are value-producing body ops)")
			osCpuCount then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osCpuCount` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a machine query is a value-producing body op)")
			osThreadCpuTicks then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osThreadCpuTicks` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a thread-CPU read is a body op writing through an out-param)")
			// F1a: a decommit is a body op, exactly as the page release above is — a page-lifecycle op transfers
			// control nowhere.
			osDecommitPages then panic("lowerStdToX64: an `osDecommitPages` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a decommit is a body op, like the page release above)")
			memFill then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `memFill` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a byte fill is a body op; its size dispatch branches INSIDE one macro-op's encoder, never between IR blocks)")
			// F1c: a reserve and a commit are value-producing body ops, for the decommit's reason.
			osCommitPages to osReservePages then panic("lowerStdToX64: a page reserve or commit appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — both are value-producing body ops, like the page allocation above)")
			// F2: all three are value-producing body ops. The arm64 CAS/RMW retry loop BRANCHES, but it does so
			// inside one macro-op's encoder, never between IR blocks — the same division `memFill` states above.
			atomicCas to currentThreadToken then panic("lowerStdToX64: an atomic or a thread-token read appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — all three are value-producing body ops)")
		end 'lower'
	end 'lowerTerminatorOp'

	// Materialize a constant into its virtual register. The op's own StdType picks
	// the register file (GP vs XMM) — this is what v1 encoded as a `constI64` /
	// `constF64` opcode pair and then re-encoded as MIR's `isFloat` bool.
	function lowerConst(resultId ValueId, value ParsedInt, valueType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		if stdTypeIsFloat(valueType) 'float'
			self.lowerFloatConst(resultId, value: value, valueType: valueType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			return
		end 'float'
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), value: value))
	end 'lowerConst'

	// Materialize a FLOAT constant: register its IEEE bits as a `.rdata` island and load them with
	// `movsd dest, [rip + <label>]`.
	//
	// ⭐ A CONSTANT POOL, NOT AN IMMEDIATE, AND IT IS THE HARDWARE THAT SAYS SO: there is no
	// `movsd xmm, imm64`. SSE has no immediate form at ALL — the same fact that makes
	// `foldConstOperands` refuse a float rhs and `lowerBinOpImm`'s `div` arm permanently
	// unreachable. The IEEE bits ARE an integer we are holding right here, but the only routes
	// into an XMM are a load or a `movq` through a GPR, and the load costs no register.
	//
	// The bits are rendered by `scalarImage` — the very serializer the `.data` image uses for an
	// f64 global — so a constant and a global spelled `3.14` are the same eight bytes by
	// construction rather than by agreement.
	//
	// ⚠ THE OP IS NOT REMATERIALIZABLE, which is worth knowing precisely because it reads no
	// register and therefore looks like it should be. See `movsdRegRip` in TargetDialect: rebuilding
	// it needs a fresh RELOCATION, which the remat path has no way to express.
	function lowerFloatConst(resultId ValueId, value ParsedInt, valueType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		requireScalarDouble(valueType, what: "lowerFloatConst")
		let bytes = scalarImage(value, byteCount: stdTypeStorageBytes(valueType))
		let label = self.globalData.registerRdataConstant(FloatConstantLabelPrefix, bytes: bytes)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movsdRegRip(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), label: label.toByteArray()))
	end 'lowerFloatConst'

	// Lower a Std binary op (both operands registers) to x64. NO seed `mov` is emitted
	// (M5.2 deleted the two-address seed): `+` uses the 3-operand `lea dest, [lhs +
	// rhs]` (independent dest, no copy, no flags); `-`/`*` use the genuinely two-address
	// `sub`/`imul` whose operand model declares `dest` a REUSE-of-`lhs` def, so the
	// ALLOCATOR — not ISel — inserts `mov dest, lhs` only when `lhs` outlives the op. A
	// loop-carried update like `sum = sum + i` therefore emits ONE instruction. Operand
	// ORDER matters for the non-commutative `sub`: `subRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs)` computes
	// lhs − rhs (e.g. `20 - 8` → 12).
	function lowerBinOp(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, opcode StdBinOpcode, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
			self.lowerFloatBinOp(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			return
		end 'float'

		let dest = TargetVReg.virtual(resultId)
		let lhsReg = TargetVReg.virtual(lhs)
		let rhsReg = TargetVReg.virtual(rhs)
		match opcode 'op'
			add then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegRegReg(dest, base: lhsReg, index: rhsReg))
			sub then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.subRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			mul then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.imulRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			// The word ALU ops are two-address exactly like `sub` — same reuse-def model, so
			// the allocator (not ISel) supplies the seed copy, and only when `lhs` outlives.
			bitAnd then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.andRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			bitOr then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.orRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			bitXor then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.xorRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			// One arm for all three shifts: they differ only in WHICH group-2 instruction they
			// name, and `shiftInstructionOf` is the one place that is decided.
			shl to shrLogical then self.lowerShiftCl(dest, lhsReg: lhsReg, countId: rhs, instr: shiftInstructionOf(opcode), dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// ⚠ INTEGER `binOp(div)` IS ILLEGAL, and the Std dialect's validator says so before this
			// can fire: `binOp` is the PURE arithmetic band, and integer division is not pure —
			// `idiv` faults on a zero divisor and on `i64.min / -1`, so it must not be reordered or
			// eliminated. It has its own non-pure variant, `StdOp.div`. Reaching here means a float
			// division arrived carrying an integer operand type, which would declare a trapping
			// instruction pure.
			div to max then panic("lowerBinOp: `binOp({opcode.name})` with an INTEGER operand type — all three are FLOAT-ONLY (`stdBinOpcodeIsFloatOnly`). Float division rides the pure arith band because `divsd` cannot fault, while integer division traps and is `StdOp.div`; `min`/`max` have no integer form at all and their operands are promoted by the parser. See StdBinOpcode.")
		end 'op'
	end 'lowerBinOp'

	// Lower a FLOAT binary op to its SSE instruction. All four are genuinely two-address (`addsd
	// xmm1, xmm2` — SSE has no 3-operand form without AVX), so each declares `dest` a REUSE-of-
	// `lhs` def and the ALLOCATOR supplies `movsd dest, lhs` only when `lhs` outlives the op —
	// exactly as `sub`/`imul` do in the integer path. No ISel seed copy.
	//
	// ⚠ `+` DOES NOT GET THE `lea` TREATMENT. The integer `add` lowers to the 3-operand
	// `lea dest, [lhs + rhs]`, which is copy-free and flag-free; there is no SSE analogue, so
	// float `+` is a two-address `addsd` like the rest. Operand ORDER therefore matters for the
	// non-commutative pair: `subsd`/`divsd` compute `lhs - rhs` / `lhs / rhs`.
	//
	// ⭐ `div` IS HERE AND INTEGER DIVISION IS NOT, which looks like an exception and is not: the
	// band's membership rule is its metadata profile, and `divsd` cannot fault (IEEE answers
	// inf/nan) while `idiv` traps. The two divisions differ in the one property the band is
	// defined by. See StdBinOpcode.
	function lowerFloatBinOp(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, opcode StdBinOpcode, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		requireScalarDouble(operandType, what: "lowerFloatBinOp")
		let dest = TargetVReg.virtual(resultId)
		let lhsReg = TargetVReg.virtual(lhs)
		let rhsReg = TargetVReg.virtual(rhs)
		match opcode 'op'
			add then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.addsdRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			sub then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.subsdRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			mul then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.mulsdRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			div then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.divsdRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			// ⭐ `min`/`max` ARE ONE INSTRUCTION HERE AND TWO ON THE OTHER TWO TARGETS — the reverse of
			// `abs`, which x64 alone has to synthesize. `minsd` computes `lhs < rhs ? lhs : rhs` and
			// `maxsd` computes `lhs > rhs ? lhs : rhs`, each including the unordered and signed-zero
			// cases, which IS the language's definition of them (`StdBinOpcode`'s `min`/`max` header), so
			// nothing is corrected and the operand order is passed through unchanged — swapping it would
			// change the answer on a NaN. ONE arm for both: which instruction is a `FloatMinMaxKind`
			// operand, decided by `floatMinMaxKindFor` for all three targets at once.
			min to max then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.minMaxSdRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg, kind: floatMinMaxKindFor(opcode)))
			// The word ALU and the shifts have no float form, and it is the TYPE SYSTEM that keeps
			// them out rather than this arm: `and`/`or`/`xor`/`shl`/`shr` are integer operators, and
			// a float operand is rejected long before lowering. An f64 reaching here under one of
			// them means that check is gone.
			bitAnd to shrLogical then panic("lowerFloatBinOp: {opcode.rawValue} has no float form — the bitwise and shift operators are integer-only, and a float operand is rejected at the Maxon tier")
		end 'op'
	end 'lowerFloatBinOp'

	// Lower a shift by a VARIABLE count. x64 has no register-register shift: the count must
	// be in **CL**, and in no other register. So the count is a FIXED-REGISTER operand and
	// the sequence mirrors `lowerDivMod`'s RAX/RDX handling exactly:
	//   mov rcx, count      ; a PHYSICAL destination — the count into CL's register
	//   shl/sar dest, cl    ; two-address (dest ⟸ lhs); names RCX only IMPLICITLY
	//
	// What keeps this correct is the pair of masks, and it is worth spelling out because the
	// failure mode is silent. The `mov rcx, count` is a physical DEF of RCX, so liveness
	// forbids RCX to every value live ACROSS it — which is every value the shift and anything
	// after it still needs. And the shift op's own `implicitUses` (the RCX bit) forbids RCX to
	// its EXPLICIT operands, `lhs` and `dest`. Without that second mask, `dest` — which is
	// live only from the shift onward, and so is absent from the live-across set — could be
	// coloured RCX, and the allocator's reuse copy (`mov rcx, lhs`) would then destroy the
	// shift count between the two instructions.
	//
	// Which group-2 instruction a shift opcode names. The `cl` form and the `imm8` form BOTH
	// ask this, so a `shrLogical` cannot become a `sar` in one of them and a `shr` in the
	// other — the two forms of one shift must encode the same instruction or the constant and
	// the variable count answer differently, which is precisely the class of bug the Std tier's
	// two right-shift opcodes exist to make impossible.
	//
	// A non-shift opcode PANICS rather than defaulting: it can only be a caller that widened
	// its match arm without widening this map.
	function shiftInstructionOf(opcode StdBinOpcode) returns ShiftInstruction
		return match opcode 'o'
			shl gives ShiftInstruction.shl
			shr gives ShiftInstruction.sar
			shrLogical gives ShiftInstruction.shr
			add to bitXor panic("shiftInstructionOf: {opcode.rawValue} is not a shift — only `shl`/`shr`/`shrLogical` name a group-2 instruction")
			div to max panic("shiftInstructionOf: `{opcode.name}` is a FLOAT-ONLY opcode, not a shift — only `shl`/`shr`/`shrLogical` name a group-2 instruction")
		end 'o'
	end 'shiftInstructionOf'

	function lowerShiftCl(dest TargetVReg, lhsReg TargetVReg, countId ValueId, instr ShiftInstruction, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(countId)))
		match instr 'i'
			shl then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.shlRegCl(dest, lhs: lhsReg))
			sar then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.sarRegCl(dest, lhs: lhsReg))
			shr then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.shrRegCl(dest, lhs: lhsReg))
		end 'i'
	end 'lowerShiftCl'

	// The imm8 twin of `lowerShiftCl`. The cast is CHECKED, and that is the point:
	// `constFitsBinOpImmediate` promises 0..63 for a shift, and this is where that promise is
	// collected. A fold that ever broke it would fail loudly here rather than silently
	// truncating a count.
	function lowerShiftImm8(dest TargetVReg, base TargetVReg, imm Imm32, instr ShiftInstruction, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let count = imm as ShiftCount
		match instr 'i'
			shl then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.shlRegImm8(dest, lhs: base, imm: count))
			sar then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.sarRegImm8(dest, lhs: base, imm: count))
			shr then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.shrRegImm8(dest, lhs: base, imm: count))
		end 'i'
	end 'lowerShiftImm8'

	// Lower a Std unary op to x64. All three are genuinely two-address (`dest = <op> src`,
	// in place), so each declares `dest` a REUSE-of-`src` def and the allocator supplies the
	// copy only when `src` outlives — no ISel seed `mov`.
	//
	// The two readings of the source keyword `not` land on two DIFFERENT instructions, and
	// the Maxon tier already chose between them from the operand's type:
	//   • `bitNot`     — `not dest` (one's complement of all 64 bits): `not 5` == -6.
	//   • `logicalNot` — `xor dest, 1`: a bool is 0/1, so flipping bit 0 negates it. Using
	//     the integer `not` here would make `not true` == -2, which still reads as TRUE in
	//     every condition — a silent miscompile, and the reason these are two opcodes.
	// ⚠ A FLOAT NEGATE IS NOT `neg`, AND IT DOES NOT ARRIVE HERE. `neg` is an integer two's
	// complement; negating an f64 means flipping its SIGN BIT (`xorpd` against a sign mask), which
	// is a different instruction and needs a `.rdata` mask constant. Nothing produces one yet:
	// `-x` on a float would, and the Maxon tier does not fold it to a `unaryOp(neg)` over an f64
	// — the spec corpus reaches negative floats through `0.0 - x` instead. This panics rather than
	// emitting an integer `neg` on a float register, which the class check would catch anyway, but
	// later and further from the cause.
	function lowerUnaryOp(resultId ValueId, operand ValueId, opcode StdUnaryOpcode, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) != stdUnaryOperandIsFloat(opcode) 'domain'
			panic("lowerUnaryOp: no x64 form of {opcode.name} over Std type {operandType.name} — an opcode's operand DOMAIN is part of the opcode (`stdUnaryOperandIsFloat`), and an f64 negate in particular flips the sign bit (`xorpd` against a .rdata mask), not the integer `neg`; nothing lowers to one yet (negative floats are reached through `0.0 - x`)")
		end 'domain'

		let dest = TargetVReg.virtual(resultId)
		let src = TargetVReg.virtual(operand)
		match opcode 'op'
			neg then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.negReg(dest, src: src))
			bitNot then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.notReg(dest, src: src))
			logicalNot then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.xorRegImm32(dest, lhs: src, imm: LogicalNotMask))
			// The CONVERSION PAIR — two of the three ops in the dialect that read one register file
			// and write the other (`bitcastF64ToI64` below is the third, and converts nothing).
			// `operandType` is the SOURCE, and the OPCODE names the result:
			// `siToFp` reads a GPR and writes an XMM, `fpToSi` the reverse. Both are PLAIN defs
			// (dest independent of src), so no seed copy is emitted.
			//
			// ⭐ `cvtTsd2si` — the second `t` is TRUNCATE, and it is why this is the right
			// instruction for `trunc()`. `cvtsd2si` rounds under MXCSR's current mode (banker's
			// rounding by default), so `trunc(3.99)` would answer 4. The spec says 3. Two
			// mnemonics one letter apart, and a wrong answer between them.
			siToFp then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.cvtsi2sdRegReg(dest, src: src))
			fpToSi then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.cvttsd2siRegReg(dest, src: src))
			// The MATH INTRINSICS. `sqrtsd` is its own instruction; the other three are ONE
			// instruction — `roundsd` — differing only in the rounding they ask for, and WHICH rounding
			// each asks for is not an x64 fact: `floatRoundModeFor` answers it once for both native
			// isels, so this arm states the INSTRUCTION and never re-decides the mode.
			floor to round then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.roundsdRegReg(dest, src: src, mode: floatRoundModeFor(opcode)))
			sqrt then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.sqrtsdRegReg(dest, src: src))
			// `abs` is the ONE math intrinsic x64 has no instruction for — SSE2 offers no scalar float
			// absolute value — so it lowers to a sign-bit CLEAR the encoder spells as a `psllq`/`psrlq`
			// pair. `TargetOp.absF64RegReg` carries the whole argument, including why shv2 does not use
			// the `andpd`-against-an-`.rdata`-mask both reference compilers emit.
			abs then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.absF64RegReg(dest, src: src))
			// The BIT REINTERPRETATION (`float.hash`). `movq r64, xmm` moves the SAME 64 bits across
			// the file boundary — it is not a convert, so there is no rounding and no value change.
			// `dest` is GPR-classed and `src` XMM-classed, each from its own value's Std type
			// (`unaryResultClass`), so the operand model needs nothing special for it.
			bitcastF64ToI64 then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movqGprXmm(dest, src: src))
			// Its INVERSE (`__Builtins.bitsToFloat`, the decimal reader's answer becoming a `float`).
			// `movq xmm, r64` — the same 64 bits, back across the boundary. `dest` is XMM-classed and
			// `src` GPR-classed, again each from its own value's Std type, so nothing here changes
			// either; only `unaryResultClass` had to learn the direction.
			bitcastI64ToF64 then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movqXmmGpr(dest, src: src))
		end 'op'
	end 'lowerUnaryOp'

	// Lower a Std `binOpImm` (constant right operand) to a copy-free immediate form:
	// `add`/`sub` to `lea dest, [lhs + disp]` (disp = imm for add, −imm for sub — the
	// literal needs no register and the op sets no flags); `mul` to the 3-operand `imul
	// dest, lhs, imm`. None is two-address, so no reuse copy is ever needed.
	function lowerBinOpImm(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, imm Imm32, opcode StdBinOpcode, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let dest = TargetVReg.virtual(resultId)
		let base = TargetVReg.virtual(lhs)
		match opcode 'op'
			add then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegRegImm32(dest, base: base, disp: imm))
			// `a - imm` is `lea dest, [base - imm]`, a NEGATIVE displacement. `foldConstOperands`
			// only emits a `sub` immediate for |imm| <= i32.max, so `-imm` always fits Imm32; the
			// checked cast documents that the range-widened `0 - imm` cannot overflow here.
			sub then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegRegImm32(dest, base: base, disp: (0 - imm) as Imm32))
			mul then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.imulRegRegImm32(dest, src: base, imm: imm))
			// The word ALU immediate forms are two-address (`and dest, imm` modifies dest in
			// place), UNLIKE the `lea`/`imul` forms above whose destination is independent of
			// their source. So they carry a reuse-def, and the allocator supplies the copy.
			bitAnd then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.andRegImm32(dest, lhs: base, imm: imm))
			bitOr then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.orRegImm32(dest, lhs: base, imm: imm))
			bitXor then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.xorRegImm32(dest, lhs: base, imm: imm))
			// A CONSTANT shift count needs no RCX at all — the whole reason `foldConstOperands`
			// bothers to fold it.
			shl to shrLogical then self.lowerShiftImm8(dest, base: base, imm: imm, instr: shiftInstructionOf(opcode), dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// PERMANENTLY unreachable, and it is the hardware that says so: no SSE instruction
			// takes an immediate operand, so a float `binOp` has no immediate form to fold to
			// and `foldConstOperands` refuses one. If this ever fires, that guard is gone and
			// IEEE bit patterns are riding the integer ALU.
			div to max then panic("lowerBinOpImm: `{opcode.name}` is FLOAT-ONLY and no SSE form takes an immediate — foldConstOperands must never produce this")
		end 'op'
	end 'lowerBinOpImm'

	// --- P1.0d.5b: the data-section band ---
	//
	// All three lower 1:1 — the Std memory band and the x64 data-section band are the same three
	// ops, because x64 has exactly these three instructions. The only real work is the WIDTH
	// decision (the shared `accessWidthFor`), which is the tier boundary doing its job.

	// `globalAddr` → `lea dest, [rip + <label>]`. The address is computed, not loaded; the PE
	// writer supplies the displacement once `.data` has an RVA.
	function lowerGlobalAddr(resultId ValueId, label ByteArray, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegGlobal(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), label: label))
	end 'lowerGlobalAddr'

	// `loadIndirect` → `mov dest, [base + disp]` / `movzx dest, byte [base + disp]`. The width and
	// displacement doors (`accessWidthFor`, `memoryDisplacement`) are shared with the arm64 lowering.
	function lowerLoadIndirect(resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, valueType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), base: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId), disp: memoryDisplacement(offset), width: accessWidthFor(valueType)))
	end 'lowerLoadIndirect'

	// `storeIndirect` → `mov [base + disp], src`.
	function lowerStoreIndirect(addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, valueId ValueId, valueType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(TargetVReg.virtual(addrId), disp: memoryDisplacement(offset), src: TargetVReg.virtual(valueId), width: accessWidthFor(valueType)))
	end 'lowerStoreIndirect'

	// Lower a Std `div`/`mod` to the x64 divide sequence. Both group-3 divides read the
	// 128-bit RDX:RAX and divide it by the divisor, leaving the QUOTIENT in RAX and the REMAINDER in
	// RDX — RAX and RDX are FIXED registers the instruction pins. So the sequence is:
	//   mov rax, dividend   ; dividend (lhs) into RAX's low half
	//   <fill RDX>          ; the HIGH half — see below, and it is the whole signedness fork
	//   idiv/div divisor    ; RDX:RAX / divisor -> quotient in RAX, remainder in RDX
	//   mov result, resultReg ; copy RAX (div) or RDX (mod) out to the result vreg
	// The divisor stays a VIRTUAL register — the allocator keeps it out of RAX/RDX
	// (divideReg's implicit RAX/RDX masks forbid it, see TargetLiveness), so it survives
	// both the `mov rax, dividend` and the RDX fill to be read by the divide. `resultReg`
	// selects which fixed register the answer is read from: RAX for `/` (quotient), RDX
	// for `mod` (remainder) — the ONLY difference between the two, so they share this one
	// lowering rather than duplicating the four-op emit.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **FILLING RDX IS WHERE THE SIGNEDNESS LIVES ON x64, AND IT IS TWO FACTS, NOT ONE** (X10).
	// A signed divide sign-EXTENDS RAX into RDX (`cqo`), so a dividend with bit 63 set reads as a
	// negative number; an unsigned one ZEROES RDX, so the same 64 bits read as a huge magnitude.
	// Emit `cqo` for an unsigned divide and `u64.max / 3` computes `-1 / 3` = 0. The instruction's
	// /ext field and this fill must therefore move TOGETHER — which is why one `DivideSignedness`
	// decides both here rather than each being chosen where it is emitted.
	//
	// `mov edx, 0` is the zeroing form, not `xor edx, edx`: the ops this dialect has are a
	// materialize-a-constant and a three-address `xorRegReg`, and the constant one says what it
	// means at one byte more. It writes the 32-bit half, which zeroes the whole of RDX (every
	// 32-bit write to a GPR clears the upper half in long mode).
	//
	// A divide TRAPS (#DE) on divide-by-zero, and the SIGNED one also on INT_MIN / -1 overflow —
	// the unsigned one cannot overflow at all, because a zeroed RDX makes every quotient fit. That
	// trap is delivered by the runtime fault handler (a Workstream-R / M6 deliverable), NOT by a
	// compiler guard — so `x / 0` here is correct-but-raw-#DE for now, by design.
	function lowerDivMod(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, signedness DivideSignedness, resultReg X64Register, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(lhs)))
		if signedness == DivideSignedness.signed 'signExtendIntoRdx'
			self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.cqo)
		end 'signExtendIntoRdx' else 'zeroRdx'
			self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: UnsignedDividendHighHalf))
		end 'zeroRdx'
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.divideReg(TargetVReg.virtual(rhs), signedness: signedness))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(resultReg)))
	end 'lowerDivMod'

	// Lower a Std `param` to the entry move that captures the parameter's incoming ABI
	// register into its virtual register: `mov virtual(resultId), physical(argReg[slot])`.
	// Modelled as a copy from a PHYSICAL source, so the existing `collectOpHints` fixed-
	// register hint (mov-to-virtual from a physical source) biases the parameter value
	// toward its own argument register — when free, the coloring lands it there and this
	// move self-elides. `collectEntryParamCaptures` finds these moves again, and the
	// cross-param forbidding it drives is what stops one capture's DESTINATION from
	// clobbering a later capture's incoming register before it is read.
	//
	// ⭐ A FLOAT PARAMETER CAPTURES FROM AN XMM ARGUMENT REGISTER, chosen by a SEPARATE FP
	// counter — the integer and float ABI registers are two independent files, so the slot is
	// NOT the source `index` but the count of preceding SAME-FILE parameters
	// (`abiFileSlotIndex`, the cross-target ABI contract). The parameter value is
	// XMM-classed by the value-class column (from its f64 Std type), so `movRegReg` sees two XMM
	// ends and emits `movsd`; before this, a float parameter captured from a GPR arg register and
	// the coloring crossed register files (a `mov rcx, xmm0` the emitter rejects).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ A STACK PARAMETER IS NOT CAPTURED AT ALL — IT IS LOADED. It arrives in the CALLER's frame, so
	// there is no incoming register to race and nothing to preserve: the value simply lives in memory
	// until this load reads it, at a displacement off rbp that no later pass can move
	// (`win64IncomingArgDisp`). Three consequences worth stating, because each is a guard the reference
	// compilers needed and shv2 does not:
	//   • `ParamCrossForbid` never sees it. It collects `movRegReg(virtual, physical)` captures in the
	//     entry block and forbids each parameter its SIBLINGS' incoming registers; a stack parameter has
	//     no incoming register, so its absence from that set is CORRECT and not an omission.
	//   • The GT stack guard and `__gt_morestack` cannot destroy it. Morestack COPIES the whole GT stack
	//     and rebases the saved-rbp chain, and this argument lives in the caller's frame ON that stack —
	//     so it moves with the copy and the rebased rbp still names it. (The register arguments are what
	//     had to be saved by hand; see `morestackSavedGprOrder`.)
	//   • The float case needs no separate path: the load is `word64`, and `emitWord64Load` picks `movsd`
	//     from the DESTINATION's class, which the value-class column set from the f64 Std type. Both
	//     reference compilers panic on an FP overflow argument instead.
	function lowerParam(resultId ValueId, index ParamCount, paramType StdType, paramFloatMask ArgFloatMask, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let capacity = x64RegisterArgCapacity()
		if abiArgIsOnStack(paramFloatMask, index: index, capacity: capacity) 'stackParam'
			let stackSlot = abiStackSlotsBefore(paramFloatMask, index: index, capacity: capacity)
			self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), base: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rbp), disp: win64IncomingArgDisp(stackSlot), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
			return
		end 'stackParam'

		let slot = abiFileSlotIndex(paramFloatMask, index: index)
		let argReg = argFpRegisterAt(slot) if stdTypeIsFloat(paramType) else argRegisterAt(slot)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(argReg)))
	end 'lowerParam'

	// The physical register a call to `callee` returns its PRIMARY value in — **XMM0** for a
	// float-returning callee, **R8** otherwise. Read off the SAME checked name index the
	// value-class column uses (`calleeResultClass`), so the value the capture move writes into is coloured
	// in the file the register it reads from lives in: a float result is XMM-classed, so `movRegReg` sees
	// two XMM ends (a `movsd`), never a cross-file `mov r, xmm`. The error-flag register (R10) is separate
	// and unconditional — it is always a GPR.
	function callResultRegister(callee ByteArray) returns X64Register
		return X64Register.xmm0 if calleeResultClass(self.funcNames, callee: callee) == RegClass.xmm else X64Register.r8
	end 'callResultRegister'

	// The DIRECT-call argument moves: each argument's register FILE is the CALLEE's declared PARAM class,
	// looked up by name and collected into an `ArgFloatMask` (`calleeArgFloatMask`), then slotted and emitted
	// through the shared `emitArgMovesByFloatMask`. Reading the callee's own parameter files is what makes a
	// float written to xmm0 here read back from xmm0 in the callee — the mask this hands the placement rule
	// is the very one the callee's `lowerParam` folds out of its declared parameter types. Shared by
	// `lowerCall` and `lowerTryCall`, whose argument passing is identical (extracted so the two cannot drift).
	function emitCallArgMoves(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.emitArgMovesByFloatMask(args, argFloatMask: calleeArgFloatMask(self.calleeParamClasses, callee: callee, argCount: args.count(), registerCapacity: x64RegisterArgCapacity()), dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'emitCallArgMoves'

	// Lower a Std `call` to the x64 call sequence: move each argument into its ABI register
	// (`emitCallArgMoves`), `callDirect` the callee, then move the return value (`callResultRegister`)
	// into the result.
	function lowerCall(resultId ValueId, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.emitCallArgMoves(callee, args: args, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.callDirect(callee))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(self.callResultRegister(callee))))
	end 'lowerCall'

	// Lower a Std `callIndirect` to the x64 call-through-register sequence (P1.5-A1): move each argument
	// into its ABI register, `callReg` THROUGH the callee register, then capture the return value into the
	// result. The mirror of `lowerCall` with one difference: the call goes through a register (`callReg`
	// carries `calleeReg` as an explicit USE, so the allocator keeps the callee address live to the call in
	// a register the argument moves have not written). There is no callee NAME to class the result or
	// arguments against, so both come from the function value's SIGNATURE carried on the op (P1.5 #78): a
	// float result is captured from XMM0 (else R8), and each argument is routed by its own type.
	function lowerCallIndirect(resultId ValueId, calleeReg ValueId, args ValueIdArray, resultType StdReturnType, argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.emitArgMovesByFloatMask(args, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.callReg(TargetVReg.virtual(calleeReg)))
		self.captureIndirectResult(resultId, resultType: resultType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerCallIndirect'

	// Capture the result of a callee expressed by VALUE (an indirect or witness call) from its ABI result
	// register into `resultId`. A VALUE callee's result comes back from XMM0/R8 (`indirectResultRegister`); a
	// VOID callee (a raw element destructor) leaves nothing to capture — `resultId` is a dead slot with no def,
	// so no move is emitted and it never enters liveness/coloring. ONE home, shared by `lowerCallIndirect` and
	// `lowerWitnessCall` so the two cannot drift on how an indirect result is captured (a divergence would be a
	// silently miscaptured return, not a compile error).
	function captureIndirectResult(resultId ValueId, resultType StdReturnType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match resultType 'r'
			void then break 'r'
			value(inner) then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(indirectResultRegister(inner))))
		end 'r'
	end 'captureIndirectResult'

	// Lower the fused `witnessCall` (P1.7a slice 2a) — `callIndirect` whose callee is a MEMORY OPERAND: move
	// each argument into its ABI register (the SHARED `emitArgMovesByFloatMask`, so witness and indirect calls
	// cannot drift on argument slotting), then a memory-indirect `call qword [witnessAddr + 24 + 8*methodIndex]`
	// (`callMem`) — the loaded function pointer is dereferenced by the call itself and NEVER occupies a virtual
	// register. `witnessAddr` is carried as `callMem`'s base-register USE, so the allocator keeps the table
	// address live across the argument moves in a register they have not written, exactly as `callReg` keeps an
	// indirect callee. The result is captured from XMM0/R8 (a void method captures nothing), identical to
	// `lowerCallIndirect`.
	function lowerWitnessCall(resultId ValueId, witnessAddr ValueId, methodIndex WitnessMethodIndex, args ValueIdArray, resultType StdReturnType, argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.emitWitnessCallSequence(witnessAddr, methodIndex: methodIndex, args: args, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.captureIndirectResult(resultId, resultType: resultType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerWitnessCall'

	// The argument marshalling and the fused memory-indirect call itself — everything a witness call does
	// BEFORE its results are captured. ONE home, shared by the throwing and non-throwing forms so the two
	// cannot drift on the slot offset or on argument slotting (a divergence would be a silently mis-dispatched
	// method, not a compile error).
	function emitWitnessCallSequence(witnessAddr ValueId, methodIndex WitnessMethodIndex, args ValueIdArray, argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.emitArgMovesByFloatMask(args, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.callMem(TargetVReg.virtual(witnessAddr), disp: witnessMethodSlotOffset(methodIndex) as Imm32))
	end 'emitWitnessCallSequence'

	// ⭐ **LOWER THE THROWING FUSED `witnessTryCall` (P1.7a slice 2b-v)** — `lowerWitnessCall` plus the one
	// thing `lowerTryCall` adds to `lowerCall`: the error flag captured from **R10**. The two capture moves are
	// a PARALLEL COPY, and they are resequenced by exactly the machinery `lowerTryCall`'s pair is
	// (`colorAndDropSelfMoves`), which keys on "a call op followed by two virtual-dest/physical-src moves" —
	// `callMem` declares `isCall: true`, so the fused witness call satisfies that test as `callDirect` does,
	// and a result/flag colouring cycle is broken with `xchg` rather than collapsing the two into one register.
	//
	// A VOID throwing method captures nothing for its primary (`captureIndirectResult` emits no move), leaving
	// the flag capture alone after the call — a single move, with no cycle to break and nothing for the
	// resequencer to pair, which is correct: one move can never overwrite its own source.
	function lowerWitnessTryCall(resultId ValueId, errorFlagId ValueId, witnessAddr ValueId, methodIndex WitnessMethodIndex, args ValueIdArray, resultType StdReturnType, argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.emitWitnessCallSequence(witnessAddr, methodIndex: methodIndex, args: args, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.captureIndirectResult(resultId, resultType: resultType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(errorFlagId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.r10)))
	end 'lowerWitnessTryCall'

	// Place each argument given a per-argument float mask (bit k set iff argument k is a float): into its
	// file's ABI register while that file has one, otherwise into this frame's outgoing STACK slot. Every
	// placement decision comes from the shared rule (`abiArgIsOnStack` / `abiFileSlotIndex` /
	// `abiStackSlotsBefore`) — the SAME calls the callee's `lowerParam` makes — so a caller and a callee
	// cannot disagree about where an argument is. This routine used to run its own gpr/fp counters, a second
	// spelling of that rule; see the rule's header for why an overflowing argument makes that unsafe.
	//
	// The ONE slotting+emit path a DIRECT call (`emitCallArgMoves`, mask from the callee's declared param
	// files), an INDIRECT call (`lowerCallIndirect`) and a WITNESS call all route through, so the three
	// differ ONLY in where the mask comes from.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **EVERY STACK STORE IS EMITTED BEFORE EVERY REGISTER MOVE, AND THAT ORDER IS A CORRECTNESS
	// REQUIREMENT, NOT A STYLE.** Both reference compilers say so in their own words — v1's
	// `sequentializeCallArgSetup` runs a "Phase 0: emit all stack-writes first", and the bootstrap's caller
	// carries "CRITICAL ORDERING: compute the stack args FIRST (which may clobber RCX/RDX/R8/R9 …), then
	// load the arg registers last" — and the corpus proves it: `specs-shv2/x64-stack-arg-disp32.md` returned
	// 400 instead of 200 with source-order emission.
	//
	// The reason is that a stack store's SOURCE may still need materializing, and materializing it takes a
	// register the allocator is free to choose — including an ARGUMENT register that an earlier move has
	// already loaded. The protection that makes the register moves safe among themselves does not reach
	// this: `forbiddenPhys` stops a value LIVE ACROSS a physical def from being coloured onto it, but an
	// argument register is not a *value*, so nothing marks it live from its move to the call. In the failing
	// program `movRegImm32 rax, 0` established argument slot 2, and the 22nd argument's constant was then
	// rematerialized into rax — `movRegImm32 rax, 200` — three instructions before the `call`.
	//
	// Ordering the stores first closes it completely rather than narrowing it: while they run, NO argument
	// register has been established, so nothing they use can destroy one. The register moves then sit
	// consecutively at the end with only the call after them, which is exactly the shape the parallel-copy
	// argument below was written for.
	//
	// SOUND without an explicit parallel-copy sequencer, for BOTH files: each `mov argReg, arg_k` is a
	// physical def that the liveness pass adds to `forbiddenPhys` for every value LIVE ACROSS it — the
	// mechanism is keyed on `RegNum` and is class-agnostic — so an argument live into a later same-file
	// argument move can never be colored to an EARLIER argument register, and forward-order emission then
	// reads each argument's register before any later move overwrites it (the read-after-clobber class
	// `project_call_arg_parallel_copy_fix` names, now covering the XMM arg registers too). The `callDirect` /
	// `callReg` carries no explicit operands, so its caller-saved `implicitDefs` clobber is applied ONLY to
	// values live across the call (never to the just-established argument registers). A REGISTER argument
	// whose value is still live at a later STACK store is protected by that same rule, which is what makes
	// the two-phase order safe in the other direction too.
	function emitArgMovesByFloatMask(args ValueIdArray, argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let capacity = x64RegisterArgCapacity()
		for k in 0 upto args.count() 'eachStackArg'
			if not abiArgIsOnStack(argFloatMask, index: k, capacity: capacity) 'inRegister'
				continue
			end 'inRegister'
			let argId = try args.get(k) otherwise panic("emitArgMovesByFloatMask: args.get OOB at {k}")
			self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(abiStackSlotsBefore(argFloatMask, index: k, capacity: capacity)), src: TargetVReg.virtual(argId), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		end 'eachStackArg'

		for k in 0 upto args.count() 'eachRegisterArg'
			if abiArgIsOnStack(argFloatMask, index: k, capacity: capacity) 'onStack'
				continue
			end 'onStack'
			let argId = try args.get(k) otherwise panic("emitArgMovesByFloatMask: args.get OOB at {k}")
			let slot = abiFileSlotIndex(argFloatMask, index: k)
			if argMaskBit(argFloatMask, argIndex: k) 'floatArg'
				self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(argFpRegisterAt(slot)), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(argId)))
			end 'floatArg' else 'intArg'
				self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(argRegisterAt(slot)), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(argId)))
			end 'intArg'
		end 'eachRegisterArg'
	end 'emitArgMovesByFloatMask'

	// Lower a `tryCall` — a call to a callee that uses the SECONDARY RETURN REGISTER: a THROWING one, whose
	// second word is the error flag, or (since P1.7a slice 2) an INTERFACE-RETURNING one, whose second word is
	// the WITNESS half of the fat pointer it hands back. Which of the two it is changes nothing here and
	// nothing in the ABI — same register, same op, `functionUsesSecondaryReturnSlot` is the one predicate that
	// decides a callee writes it. The arguments and `callDirect` exactly as `lowerCall` (via the shared
	// `emitCallArgMoves`), then capture BOTH return registers — the primary value from
	// `callResultRegister(callee)` (**XMM0** for a float-returning callee, else **R8**) and the second word
	// from **R10**. The two capture moves are a PARALLEL COPY that SSA destruction resequences through
	// `sequenceParallelCopy` (see `colorAndDropSelfMoves`): a naive `mov;mov` collapses a value/flag swap
	// when the allocator colors them cyclically. R10 is caller-saved, not an argument register, and
	// allocatable — mirroring R8's own "no return/argument register conflict" — so it is unconditionally
	// free to carry the flag out. The capture moves are emitted CONSECUTIVELY and right after the call, so
	// the resequencer's "a call followed by two virtual-dest/physical-src moves" test identifies the pair.
	//
	// ⚠ A FLOAT primary and the flag are in DIFFERENT register files (XMM0 value, GPR R10 flag), so they can
	// never colour to one physical register and the pair has no cross-file cycle — the resequencer is a
	// correct no-op for it, exactly as it is for the all-GPR (int result) pair it already handled.
	function lowerTryCall(resultId ValueId, errorFlagId ValueId, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.emitCallArgMoves(callee, args: args, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.callDirect(callee))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(self.callResultRegister(callee))))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(errorFlagId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.r10)))
	end 'lowerTryCall'

	// Lower the dual-register `errorReturn`: write the primary value to its return register (**XMM0** for a
	// float return, else **R8** — through the SAME `emitReturnValueMove` the ordinary `ret` uses, so the
	// float branch is not written twice) and the SECOND WORD to **R10**, then the return terminator. This is
	// the ONE place the second register is written — with the error flag for a THROWING function and with the
	// WITNESS half for an interface-returning one (P1.7a slice 2), a difference this emitter cannot see and
	// does not need to: `LowerMaxonToStd.secondaryReturnValue` decides which word rides. Both moves are unconditional (even a void throwing
	// function's caller reads the flag register — for a void return type `emitReturnValueMove` targets R8,
	// where the family-default zero belongs); the frame epilogue is inserted separately by
	// `X64PrologueEpilogue`, which recognizes the `ret` terminator this emits exactly as it does an
	// ordinary return's.
	//
	// A THROWING FLOAT function's THROW path IS handled (F3b / OPEN #60): `lowerThrowError`
	// (LowerMaxonToStd) types the throw-edge primary default to the RETURN type via
	// `emitZeroConstOfReturnType` — a float zero (XMM) for a float return, an i64 zero (GPR) otherwise —
	// keyed off the SAME `stdReturnTypeIsFloat`/`returnType` this move reads, so the default's register
	// file and this move's target register cannot disagree. Both the OK path (the real float) and the
	// throw path (a dead XMM zero the caller ignores once the flag is set) reach `emitReturnValueMove`
	// with a correctly-classed primary value.
	function lowerErrorReturn(retValId ValueId, errorFlagId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock, returnType StdReturnType, asTerminator bool)
		self.emitReturnValueMove(retValId, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: returnType)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r10), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(errorFlagId)))
		self.emitRet(dstBlock, asTerminator: asTerminator)
	end 'lowerErrorReturn'

	// Lower `osExit(code)` to the platform's process-exit primitive — `ExitProcess(code)` through the IAT
	// on Windows (the primitive a `panic(…)` ends on), `exit_group(code)` via a raw `syscall` on Linux
	// (`x64OsCallKind` — see X64LinuxRuntime). `code` is dead after (the process is gone), so nothing is
	// live across the call. The two ABIs put the first argument in DIFFERENT registers — Win64 in ECX,
	// the Linux syscall in EDI — which is why the argument move, not just the terminal call, is inside the
	// OS branch (unlike arm64, where AAPCS64 and the syscall ABI share x0).
	function lowerOsExit(codeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsExitWin64(codeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then self.lowerOsExitLinux(codeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsExit'

	// Win64: the code in ECX (the first-argument register), then `ExitProcess` through the IAT.
	function lowerOsExitWin64(codeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movReg32Reg32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(codeId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.ExitProcess)))
	end 'lowerOsExitWin64'

	// Linux: the code in EDI (syscall arg 0), then `exit_group` via a raw syscall.
	function lowerOsExitLinux(codeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movReg32Reg32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdi), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(codeId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.x64Syscall(X64LinuxSyscallExitGroup))
	end 'lowerOsExitLinux'

	// Lower `osPanic <label>` (a P1.9 range-check violation) to `mrt_panic`, which reads the baked
	// `panic at …` line, prints it plus a symbolized backtrace, and exits — the message address in RCX,
	// then the call. A `callDirect` (not `iatCall`) because `mrt_panic` is one of the program's own
	// `.text` runtime chunks, resolved by the intra-`.text` call fixup. `functionMakesCall` sees the
	// `callDirect` and reserves the shadow space it needs. RCX is written and immediately consumed, so no
	// intervening op can clobber it.
	//
	// ⭐ NO OS SPLIT HERE, and that is the point (rung A1j). It had one — a Windows arm that called
	// `mrt_panic` and a Linux arm that materialized a bare `exit_group(1)`, because `mrt_panic` was
	// appended only to the Windows lane. Once it is appended to both (`appendX64LinuxRuntimeChunks`) the
	// two arms are byte-identical, because RCX is not a Win64-ABI fact here: it is THIS FILE'S private
	// convention for "the cstr argument", and `X64Runtime.emitPrintString` — which both lanes' stderr
	// writers are called through — already spells it that way. The one genuine OS difference is the
	// process exit, and it lives inside the chunk, reached by a route `appendX64RuntimeChunks` derives
	// from `x64OsCallKind(target.os)` — the same door `lowerOsExit` consults here. A third spelling of
	// "which OS am I" bought nothing but a lane that silently said less.
	function lowerPanic(label ByteArray, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegRdata(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), label: label))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.callDirect(PanicName))
	end 'lowerPanic'

	// The RENDERED twin (A3n). The message is a runtime POINTER — an interpolation assembled the whole
	// `panic at …\n` line, NUL terminator included — so it MOVES into RCX where the baked form
	// materializes a label address there. Everything after is identical, which is the point: one
	// `mrt_panic`, one cstr contract, two ways of producing the bytes. RCX is written and immediately
	// consumed, so no intervening op can clobber it.
	function lowerPanicValue(messagePtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(messagePtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.callDirect(PanicName))
	end 'lowerPanicValue'

	// Lower `osAllocPages` to the x64-windows page source: `VirtualAlloc(NULL, size,
	// MEM_COMMIT|MEM_RESERVE, PAGE_READWRITE)` through the IAT.
	//
	// ⭐ THIS IS THE ONLY PLACE THE TWO ABIs MEET, and that is why `osAllocPages` is its own op
	// rather than a `call` with a funny callee. `lowerCall` moves arguments through Maxon's CUSTOM
	// ABI (`argRegisters` = rcx, rdx, **rax**, r9, …) and reads the result out of **R8**. Win64 is
	// neither: its integer arguments are rcx, rdx, **r8**, r9 and its result comes back in **RAX**.
	// The two disagree in exactly the places that matter here — argument 3 and the return register —
	// so routing this through `lowerCall` would put the allocation flags in the wrong register and
	// then read the size back as the base address. Both ABIs are spelled out where they are used.
	//
	// The Std tier never learns any of this: it asked for PAGES. `VirtualAlloc` is named only as
	// `IatSlot.VirtualAlloc`, whose spelling and import live in `Targets/Windows/PeWriter.maxon`.
	//
	// ⭐ ON LINUX THE PAGE SOURCE IS `mmap(NULL, size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_PRIVATE,
	// -1, 0)` via a raw syscall (`x64OsCallKind` — see X64LinuxRuntime), and the argument PLACEMENT
	// differs from Win64's, not just the terminal call: the Linux syscall ABI reads its six arguments
	// from rdi/rsi/rdx/**r10**/r8/r9 (the 4th in r10, NOT rcx — the `syscall` instruction destroys rcx),
	// where Win64 reads rcx/rdx/r8/r9. Both return the base in RAX. `MAP_ANONYMOUS` returns ZEROED pages,
	// satisfying the slab's zeroing contract exactly as Windows's `MEM_COMMIT` does. The immediate loads
	// go through `constMaterializationOp` (not a bare `movRegImm32`) so the -1 `fd` — outside the u32 the
	// 32-bit form zero-extends — takes the 64-bit `mov`. The single live operand `size` moves forward
	// soundly for the same reason the arm64 twin's does: each physical arg-register def liveness forbids
	// to a value live across it, so `size` can never be coloured to an argument register clobbered before
	// its own move.
	function lowerOsAllocPages(resultId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsAllocPagesWin64(sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then self.lowerOsAllocPagesLinux(sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		end 'route'
		// RAX, not R8 — Win64's VirtualAlloc and the Linux mmap syscall both return in RAX, where Maxon's
		// own ABI returns in R8. This capture is the whole reason `osAllocPages` is its own op.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax)))
	end 'lowerOsAllocPages'

	// Win64: `VirtualAlloc(NULL, size, MEM_COMMIT|MEM_RESERVE, PAGE_READWRITE)`, args rcx/rdx/r8/r9.
	function lowerOsAllocPagesWin64(sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		// lpAddress = NULL: the OS picks the base. A zeroed rcx is the request, not a missing argument.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: VirtualAllocLetOsChooseBase))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: VirtualAllocCommitAndReserve))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: VirtualAllocPageReadWrite))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.VirtualAlloc)))
	end 'lowerOsAllocPagesWin64'

	// Linux: `mmap(NULL, size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0)`, args in the
	// syscall ABI registers rdi/rsi/rdx/r10/r8/r9 (the 4th in r10, NOT rcx — the `syscall` instruction
	// destroys rcx). The immediates go through `constMaterializationOp` so the -1 `fd`, outside the u32
	// the 32-bit form zero-extends, takes the 64-bit `mov`.
	function lowerOsAllocPagesLinux(sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdi), value: MmapKernelChoosesBase))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsi), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: MmapProtReadWrite))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r10), value: MmapAnonPrivateLinux))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: MmapAnonFd))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: MmapNoOffset))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.x64Syscall(X64LinuxSyscallMmap))
	end 'lowerOsAllocPagesLinux'

	// Lower `osWriteStdout`/`osWriteStderr(result, buf, len)` to `GetStdHandle(<selector>)` +
	// `WriteFile(handle, buf, len, &written, NULL)`, both through the IAT (P1.2 wave A — `print`;
	// W35 — `printError`).
	//
	// ⭐ ONE GRAPH FOR BOTH STREAMS. They differ by the `GetStdHandle` selector on Windows and by the
	// descriptor on Linux, and by nothing else — so the stream is a parameter and the marshalling is
	// written once. A second copy would be free to drift on the out-param slot, which is exactly the
	// duplication `osReadFile`'s header refuses for `ReadFile`.
	//
	// ⭐ EVERYTHING GOES THROUGH THE CLOBBER-CORRECT `iatCall`. `iatCall` declares `implicitDefs:
	// 0xFFFF0FC7` (the caller-saved set), so `buf`/`len` — the two virtual registers live ACROSS the
	// GetStdHandle call — are spilled or forced into callee-saved registers by the allocator with no
	// hand-holding. This is exactly the path `lowerOsAllocPages` takes; hand-assembling the call (as the
	// PANIC runtime does, where there are no live virtuals) would bypass the clobber and miscompile.
	//
	// Win64 ABI (NOT Maxon's): args rcx/rdx/r8/r9 then stack, result in RAX. The 5th arg (`lpOverlapped
	// = NULL`) is a stack argument at `[rsp + win64OutgoingArgDisp(WriteFileOverlappedSlot)]`; the 4th
	// (`lpNumberOfBytesWritten`) must be a valid pointer (WriteFile writes the count there), so r9 points
	// at a scratch slot at `[rsp + win64OutgoingArgDisp(WriteFileBytesWrittenSlot)]`. Both live in the
	// writer's `outgoingArgSize` region.
	// ⭐ ON LINUX THE PRIMITIVE IS A SINGLE `write(fd, buf, len)` syscall — no handle call, no bytes-written
	// out-param, no stack argument, so none of the WriteFile apparatus above (`win64OutgoingArgDisp`, the
	// outgoing-arg slots, the RAX NULL scratch). The three arguments move into rdi/rsi/rdx (the Linux
	// syscall's first three), where Win64 uses rcx/rdx/r8; `buf`/`len` move forward soundly because each
	// physical arg-register def liveness forbids to a value live across it.
	function lowerOsWriteStream(resultId ValueId, bufId ValueId, lenId ValueId, stream OsOutputStream, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsWriteStreamWin64(resultId, bufId: bufId, lenId: lenId, stream: stream, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then self.lowerOsWriteStreamLinux(resultId, bufId: bufId, lenId: lenId, stream: stream, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsWriteStream'

	// Win64: `GetStdHandle(<selector>)` then `WriteFile(handle, buf, len, &written, NULL)`, both
	// through the IAT, with the 4th/5th arguments on the reserved outgoing-arg stack slots.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BYTES-WRITTEN SLOT IS PRE-ZEROED AND READ BACK AS A FULL WORD, AND BOTH HALVES ARE
	// LOAD-BEARING.** `lpNumberOfBytesWritten` is a `DWORD*` — WriteFile writes FOUR bytes — while the slot
	// is one of `win64OutgoingArgDisp`'s eight-byte words and `X64AccessWidth` carries no 32-bit form. So
	// the whole word is zeroed BEFORE the call (from the same rax NULL the `lpOverlapped` store uses, which
	// is why the materialization moved above both stores) and read back as `word64` after: the high half is
	// provably zero, so the count arrives zero-extended and exact. Reading a 4-byte answer out of an
	// uninitialized 8-byte slot would carry whatever the previous frame left in the high half.
	function lowerOsWriteStreamWin64(resultId ValueId, bufId ValueId, lenId ValueId, stream OsOutputStream, dstBlock IrBlock)
		// GetStdHandle(<selector>) -> RAX (the handle).
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: win64StdHandleSelector(stream)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetStdHandle)))

		// WriteFile(handle, buffer, length, &written, NULL). The handle move (rcx <- rax) FIRST, before
		// rax is reused as the NULL scratch below.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(lenId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), base: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(WriteFileBytesWrittenSlot)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(WriteFileOverlappedSlot), src: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(WriteFileBytesWrittenSlot), src: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.WriteFile)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), base: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(WriteFileBytesWrittenSlot), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
	end 'lowerOsWriteStreamWin64'

	// Linux: a single `write(fd, buf, len)` syscall, args rdi/rsi/rdx — no handle, no out-param, no stack.
	// The syscall answers the byte count (or a negative errno) in RAX, exactly as `lowerOsAllocPages`'s
	// `mmap` answers its base there.
	function lowerOsWriteStreamLinux(resultId ValueId, bufId ValueId, lenId ValueId, stream OsOutputStream, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdi), value: posixStreamFd(stream)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsi), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(lenId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.x64Syscall(X64LinuxSyscallWrite))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax)))
	end 'lowerOsWriteStreamLinux'

	// Lower `osReadClock(ticksPtr, freqPtr)` to the x64-windows MONOTONIC clock reads (P1.5 mid-body-yield):
	// `QueryPerformanceFrequency(&freq)` then `QueryPerformanceCounter(&ticks)`, both through the IAT. Each
	// takes a single LARGE_INTEGER out-param pointer in rcx (Win64's first argument register) and writes 8
	// bytes there; the builder (`__gt_now_ns`) hands in the two `.data` scratch addresses as value operands
	// and reads the results back with ordinary loads. Everything routes through the clobber-correct `iatCall`
	// exactly as `lowerOsAllocPages`/`lowerOsWriteStdout` do — so the address operand live across the first
	// call is spilled or forced callee-saved by the allocator with no hand-holding.
	//
	// ⭐ x64-WINDOWS ONLY. The QPC/QPF counter is a Win32 concept; a linux/arm64/wasm sleep is a later slice,
	// so the syscall route REJECTS rather than inventing a fake clock. (Sleep is x64-windows-gated by spec.)
	function lowerOsReadClock(ticksPtrId ValueId, freqPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsReadClockWin64(ticksPtrId, freqPtrId: freqPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsReadClock: the QueryPerformanceCounter/Frequency clock is x64-windows only — the green-thread sleep substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsReadClock'

	// Win64: `QueryPerformanceFrequency(&freq)` then `QueryPerformanceCounter(&ticks)`, each with its out-param
	// pointer in rcx. Frequency FIRST so the ticks read is the most recent instant returned to the caller.
	function lowerOsReadClockWin64(ticksPtrId ValueId, freqPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(freqPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.QueryPerformanceFrequency)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(ticksPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.QueryPerformanceCounter)))
	end 'lowerOsReadClockWin64'

	// Lower `osReadWallClock(ftPtr)` to `GetSystemTimeAsFileTime(ftPtr)` through the IAT — the out-param
	// pointer in rcx, no result (the 64-bit FILETIME lands in the caller's word). The `__Builtins` WALL clock.
	//
	// ⭐ x64-WINDOWS ONLY, for the same reason `osReadClock` is: the calendar's POSIX spelling is
	// `clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME)`, whose `timespec` needs a different out-param shape and a different
	// scale, so inventing one here would be untested code no test could reach. A program that reaches the
	// wall clock on another target is refused in SEMANTIC CHECK, at its own source span (E3104) — this
	// panic is the backstop for a compiler bug that got one past that gate, not the user-facing rejection.
	function lowerOsReadWallClock(ftPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsWin64Call1(IatSlot.GetSystemTimeAsFileTime, arg0: ftPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsReadWallClock: GetSystemTimeAsFileTime is x64-windows only — the wall clock is x64-windows-gated at this rung, and SemanticCheck refuses it on every other target")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsReadWallClock'

	// Lower `osSleepMs(ms)` to Windows `Sleep(ms)` through the IAT — the netpoller's real wait on the earliest
	// timer deadline (never a busy-spin). `ms` moves into rcx (Win64's first argument register); Sleep is void.
	function lowerOsSleepMs(msId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsSleepMsWin64(msId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsSleepMs: Windows `Sleep` is x64-windows only — the green-thread sleep substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsSleepMs'

	function lowerOsSleepMsWin64(msId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.lowerOsWin64Call1(IatSlot.Sleep, arg0: msId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerOsSleepMsWin64'

	// Lower `osProcessSpawn(cmdlinePtr, startupPtr, procInfoPtr)` to `CreateProcessA(NULL, cmdlinePtr, NULL, NULL,
	// FALSE, 0, NULL, NULL, startupPtr, procInfoPtr)` through the IAT (P1.5 subprocess seed). Ten Win64 arguments:
	// the first four in rcx/rdx/r8/r9, arguments 5-10 on the stack past the shadow space (the WriteFile stack-arg
	// template). Everything goes through the clobber-correct `iatCall`, so `procInfoPtr` — the ONE operand live
	// ACROSS the call (the runtime reads `hProcess`/`hThread` out of it afterwards) — is forced callee-saved by the
	// allocator with no hand-holding, exactly as WriteFile's `buf`/`len` are.
	//
	// ⭐ x64-WINDOWS ONLY, like the whole green-thread substrate; the syscall route rejects.
	function lowerOsProcessSpawn(cmdlinePtrId ValueId, cwdPtrId ValueId, creationFlagsId ValueId, startupPtrId ValueId, procInfoPtrId ValueId, inheritHandles bool, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsProcessSpawnWin64(cmdlinePtrId, cwdPtrId: cwdPtrId, creationFlagsId: creationFlagsId, startupPtrId: startupPtrId, procInfoPtrId: procInfoPtrId, inheritHandles: inheritHandles, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsProcessSpawn: CreateProcessA is x64-windows only — the green-thread subprocess substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsProcessSpawn'

	function lowerOsProcessSpawnWin64(cmdlinePtrId ValueId, cwdPtrId ValueId, creationFlagsId ValueId, startupPtrId ValueId, procInfoPtrId ValueId, inheritHandles bool, dstBlock IrBlock)
		// The two OUT-struct pointers (args 9/10) onto their stack slots FIRST, while every register is still
		// free — each reads its virtual and writes to the frame, clobbering no other operand's register.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(CreateProcessStartupInfoSlot), src: TargetVReg.virtual(startupPtrId), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(CreateProcessProcInfoSlot), src: TargetVReg.virtual(procInfoPtrId), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		// dwCreationFlags (arg 6) and lpCurrentDirectory (arg 8) are VALUE operands, so they go to their slots
		// with every register still free, exactly as the two out-struct pointers above did. ⚠ Both used to be
		// part of the zeroed run below; a NULL `lpCurrentDirectory` still means "inherit the parent's cwd" and a
		// 0 `dwCreationFlags` still means "no special creation", so a builder with nothing to say passes 0 and
		// gets exactly the bytes this lowering emitted before it was widened.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(CreateProcessCreationFlagsSlot), src: TargetVReg.virtual(creationFlagsId), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(CreateProcessCurrentDirSlot), src: TargetVReg.virtual(cwdPtrId), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		// lpCommandLine (arg 2) -> rdx.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(cmdlinePtrId)))
		// The two remaining FALSE/NULL middle stack args (5, 7): one zeroed scratch register (rax, clobbered by
		// the call anyway) stored into each slot. An 8-byte zero store into a slot the callee reads as a 4-byte
		// BOOL/DWORD is correct — CreateProcessA reads only the low bytes and the high bytes are its own slot's
		// padding. ⚠ `lpEnvironment` stays a hard NULL by the op's own contract — see `osProcessSpawnInherit`.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(CreateProcessInheritHandlesSlot), src: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(CreateProcessEnvironmentSlot), src: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		// The three NULL register args: lpApplicationName=rcx (1), lpProcessAttributes=r8 (3), lpThreadAttributes=r9 (4).
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: NullPointer))
		// osProcessSpawnInherit OVERWRITES the bInheritHandles stack slot (arg 5, zeroed above) with TRUE so the child
		// inherits the pipe write end its STARTUPINFO names as hStdOutput; osProcessSpawn leaves the 0. rax is free here.
		if inheritHandles 'inherit'
			self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), value: InheritHandlesTrue))
			self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(CreateProcessInheritHandlesSlot), src: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		end 'inherit'
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.CreateProcessA)))
	end 'lowerOsProcessSpawnWin64'

	// ⭐ **THE FOUR ATTACHED-SUBPROCESS HOST PRIMITIVES.** Each routes on `x64OsCallKind` through the shared
	// `requireWin64Substrate` rejection, exactly as every other member of the band does — the whole
	// subprocess substrate is x64-windows-gated, and `SemanticCheck` refuses such a program at its own source
	// span (E3104) long before it reaches here.

	// `SetHandleInformation(handle, HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT, HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT)` — three register arguments,
	// two of them the same immediate (the MASK and the VALUE, which is what "turn this flag on" spells).
	function lowerOsHandleSetInherit(handleId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osHandleSetInherit")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(handleId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: HandleFlagInherit))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: HandleFlagInherit))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.SetHandleInformation)))
	end 'lowerOsHandleSetInherit'

	// `PeekNamedPipe(handle, NULL, 0, NULL, availPtr, NULL)` — SIX arguments: four in rcx/rdx/r8/r9 and two
	// on the stack past the shadow space. Only the handle and the `lpTotalBytesAvail` out-pointer carry a
	// value; the read buffer, its size, the bytes-read out-word and the message-remaining out-word are all
	// NULL/0 because this call asks HOW MUCH is there and never consumes it.
	function lowerOsPipePeek(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, availPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osPipePeek")
		// The two stack args (5, 6) FIRST, while every register is still free — `lowerOsProcessSpawnWin64`'s
		// discipline, and for its reason.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(PeekPipeAvailSlot), src: TargetVReg.virtual(availPtrId), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: PeekPipeLeftSlot, value: NullPointer)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(handleId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.PeekNamedPipe)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsPipePeek'

	// `GetEnvironmentVariableA(name, buf, len)` — three register arguments, the character count in RAX.
	function lowerOsEnvRead(resultId ValueId, namePtrId ValueId, bufPtrId ValueId, bufLenId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osEnvRead")
		self.lowerOsWin64Call3(IatSlot.GetEnvironmentVariableA, arg0: namePtrId, arg1: bufPtrId, arg2: bufLenId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsEnvRead'

	// `TerminateProcess(handle, exitCode)` — two register arguments; the BOOL is discarded (a child that has
	// already exited answers FALSE, which is the same outcome as success for the deadline this serves).
	function lowerOsProcessTerminate(handleId ValueId, exitCodeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osProcessTerminate")
		self.lowerOsWin64Call2(IatSlot.TerminateProcess, arg0: handleId, arg1: exitCodeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerOsProcessTerminate'

	// Lower `osProcessExitCode(handle, codePtr)` to `GetExitCodeProcess(handle, codePtr)` — args rcx/rdx; the
	// child's DWORD exit code is written to `[codePtr]` (the runtime reads it back, like `osReadClock`'s
	// out-params). No result captured.
	function lowerOsProcessExitCode(handleId ValueId, codePtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsWin64Call2(IatSlot.GetExitCodeProcess, arg0: handleId, arg1: codePtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsProcessExitCode: GetExitCodeProcess is x64-windows only — the green-thread subprocess substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsProcessExitCode'

	// Lower `osWaitHandles(handleArrayPtr, count, timeoutMs)` to `WaitForMultipleObjects(count, handleArrayPtr,
	// FALSE, timeoutMs)` through the IAT — the netpoll's single-M block on the parked children. Win64 args:
	// count=rcx, handleArrayPtr=rdx, bWaitAll=FALSE=r8, timeoutMs=r9. No result captured (the netpoll re-scans).
	function lowerOsWaitHandles(handleArrayPtrId ValueId, countId ValueId, timeoutMsId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsWaitHandlesWin64(handleArrayPtrId, countId: countId, timeoutMsId: timeoutMsId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsWaitHandles: WaitForMultipleObjects is x64-windows only — the green-thread subprocess substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsWaitHandles'

	function lowerOsWaitHandlesWin64(handleArrayPtrId ValueId, countId ValueId, timeoutMsId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(countId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(handleArrayPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: WaitAnyHandle))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(timeoutMsId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.WaitForMultipleObjects)))
	end 'lowerOsWaitHandlesWin64'

	// Lower `osCloseHandle(handle)` to `CloseHandle(handle)` through the IAT — arg rcx, result ignored.
	function lowerOsCloseHandle(handleId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsWin64Call1(IatSlot.CloseHandle, arg0: handleId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsCloseHandle: CloseHandle is x64-windows only — the green-thread subprocess substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsCloseHandle'

	// Lower `osFreePages(addr, size)` — release a whole page region an earlier `osAllocPages` committed. Two
	// routes, and the two OSes want DIFFERENT THINGS from the same op: Windows wants the base and forbids a size,
	// POSIX requires both. Both discard the answer — a failed release leaves a caller nothing to do, exactly as
	// `lowerOsCloseHandle` discards `CloseHandle`'s BOOL.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SYSCALL ROUTE USED TO PANIC, AND THE VOCABULARY WAS THE REASON.** `osFreePages` carried the base
	// ALONE (`MEM_RELEASE` needs nothing else), and there is no `munmap` spelling that takes one — so the refusal
	// stated a fact about the OP rather than about Linux. F1a gave it a length; this is the lowering that was
	// available all along.
	function lowerOsFreePages(addrId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsFreePagesWin64(addrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then self.lowerOsFreePagesLinux(addrId, sizeId: sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsFreePages'

	// Win64: `VirtualFree(lpAddress = addr, dwSize = 0, dwFreeType = MEM_RELEASE)`, args rcx/rdx/r8. `addr` is
	// not live across the call (the completion path is done with the freed stack), so it moves forward soundly
	// under the allocator's per-arg-register def liveness, exactly as `lowerOsCloseHandle`'s does.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE `size` OPERAND IS READ BY NOTHING ON THIS LANE, AND THAT IS THE API'S RULE, NOT AN OVERSIGHT** —
	// which is why this function does not take one. `MEM_RELEASE` releases the WHOLE reservation from its base
	// and REQUIRES `dwSize == 0`; passing the real length makes `VirtualFree` return FALSE and leak the region.
	// The op still carries the size because the four POSIX lanes cannot be lowered without it (see
	// `StdDialect.osFreePages`), so a producer always states it and this one target throws it away.
	function lowerOsFreePagesWin64(addrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: VirtualFreeSizeMustBeZero))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: VirtualFreeMemRelease))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.VirtualFree)))
	end 'lowerOsFreePagesWin64'

	// Linux: `munmap(addr, len)`, args in the syscall ABI registers rdi/rsi. The kernel's `int` answer lands in
	// RAX and is discarded, exactly as the Win64 route discards `VirtualFree`'s BOOL. The two operands move
	// forward in order for `lowerOsAllocPagesLinux`'s reason: each physical arg-register def is forbidden to a
	// value live across it, so neither can be coloured to a register the other's move has already clobbered.
	function lowerOsFreePagesLinux(addrId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdi), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsi), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.x64Syscall(X64LinuxSyscallMunmap))
	end 'lowerOsFreePagesLinux'

	// Lower `osDecommitPages(addr, size)` — drop the range's physical backing and KEEP its reservation, so the
	// base stays valid and a later `osCommitPages` can re-back it in place. Unlike the release above, BOTH
	// routes read the size: a decommit is always about a sub-range.
	//
	// ⚠ The two routes do not promise the same CONTENTS on the next touch (Windows re-commits zeros; Linux
	// refaults a zero page; the Darwin lane this file does not serve may hand back a dirty one).
	// `StdDialect.osDecommitPages` carries that obligation for all four lanes at once.
	function lowerOsDecommitPages(addrId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsDecommitPagesWin64(addrId, sizeId: sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then self.lowerOsDecommitPagesLinux(addrId, sizeId: sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsDecommitPages'

	// Win64: `VirtualFree(lpAddress = addr, dwSize = size, dwFreeType = MEM_DECOMMIT)`, args rcx/rdx/r8 — the
	// SAME entry point as the release, with the two arguments that decide which of the two operations it is.
	function lowerOsDecommitPagesWin64(addrId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: VirtualFreeMemDecommit))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.VirtualFree)))
	end 'lowerOsDecommitPagesWin64'

	// Linux: `madvise(addr, len, MADV_DONTNEED)`, args rdi/rsi/rdx. On a MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_PRIVATE mapping —
	// which is the only kind `osAllocPages` makes — this drops the pages and the next touch refaults a ZERO one,
	// so the mapping survives and its contents do not. The `int` answer in RAX is discarded, like `munmap`'s.
	function lowerOsDecommitPagesLinux(addrId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdi), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsi), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: MadviseDontNeedLinux))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.x64Syscall(X64LinuxSyscallMadvise))
	end 'lowerOsDecommitPagesLinux'

	// Lower `osReservePages(size)` — take `size` bytes of address space with NO access and NO backing (F1c).
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS `osAllocPages` WITH TWO ARGUMENTS CHANGED, AND THOSE TWO ARGUMENTS ARE THE WHOLE FEATURE.** On
	// Win64 the allocation type drops `MEM_COMMIT` and the protection becomes `PAGE_NOACCESS`; on Linux the
	// protection becomes `PROT_NONE`. Everything else — the NULL base, the register placement, the RAX capture —
	// is the page source's, unchanged, which is why this needs no new import and no new syscall number.
	//
	// The single live operand (`size`) moves forward soundly for `lowerOsAllocPages`'s reason: each physical
	// arg-register def is forbidden by liveness to a value live across it.
	function lowerOsReservePages(resultId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsReservePagesWin64(sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then self.lowerOsReservePagesLinux(sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		end 'route'
		// RAX, for `lowerOsAllocPages`'s reason: both `VirtualAlloc` and the `mmap` syscall answer there, where
		// Maxon's own ABI would answer in R8.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax)))
	end 'lowerOsReservePages'

	// Win64: `VirtualAlloc(NULL, size, MEM_RESERVE, PAGE_NOACCESS)`, args rcx/rdx/r8/r9 — the allocation call
	// `lowerOsAllocPagesWin64` makes, with the two flag words that make it a reservation.
	function lowerOsReservePagesWin64(sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: VirtualAllocLetOsChooseBase))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: VirtualAllocReserveOnly))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: VirtualAllocPageNoAccess))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.VirtualAlloc)))
	end 'lowerOsReservePagesWin64'

	// Linux: `mmap(NULL, size, PROT_NONE, MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0)` — Go's `sysReserveOS`
	// (`vendor/go/src/runtime/mem_linux.go:163`) verbatim, and `lowerOsAllocPagesLinux`'s six arguments with one
	// protection word changed.
	function lowerOsReservePagesLinux(sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdi), value: MmapKernelChoosesBase))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsi), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: MmapProtNone))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r10), value: MmapAnonPrivateLinux))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: MmapAnonFd))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: MmapNoOffset))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.x64Syscall(X64LinuxSyscallMmap))
	end 'lowerOsReservePagesLinux'

	// Lower `osCommitPages(addr, size)` — make `[addr, addr + size)` readable, writable and backed (F1c).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO OSes DO GENUINELY DIFFERENT THINGS HERE, AND THAT IS THE OP'S REASON FOR EXISTING.** Windows
	// has a commit state, so this is `VirtualAlloc` over an address it already reserved. Linux has none — a
	// mapping IS the commitment — so a commit is a `MAP_FIXED` re-map of the sub-range, which is Go's `sysMapOS`
	// (`mem_linux.go:172`) verbatim. Both answer the base in RAX, or a failure the caller's guard reads:
	// `VirtualAlloc` answers NULL, and the raw `mmap` syscall answers a small negative errno.
	//
	// ⛔ The `MAP_FIXED` route DESTROYS whatever was mapped in the range. `StdDialect.osCommitPages` carries that
	// obligation for all four lanes at once; nothing here may soften it.
	function lowerOsCommitPages(resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsCommitPagesWin64(addrId, sizeId: sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then self.lowerOsCommitPagesLinux(addrId, sizeId: sizeId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		end 'route'
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax)))
	end 'lowerOsCommitPages'

	// Win64: `VirtualAlloc(lpAddress = addr, dwSize = size, flAllocationType = MEM_COMMIT,
	// flProtect = PAGE_READWRITE)`, args rcx/rdx/r8/r9. The base is NAMED here where `lowerOsAllocPagesWin64`
	// passes NULL — that is the whole difference between "give me pages anywhere" and "back these pages".
	function lowerOsCommitPagesWin64(addrId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: VirtualAllocCommitOnly))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: VirtualAllocPageReadWrite))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.VirtualAlloc)))
	end 'lowerOsCommitPagesWin64'

	// Linux: `mmap(addr, size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_FIXED, -1, 0)`. `MAP_FIXED` is
	// what makes the answer the address that was asked for rather than one the kernel picked, and it is ORed into
	// the anon-private word HERE — the one site on this backend that materializes that immediate — so the Linux
	// flag word stays stated once (`PosixMmap.MmapAnonPrivateLinux`).
	function lowerOsCommitPagesLinux(addrId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdi), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsi), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: MmapProtReadWrite))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r10), value: MmapAnonPrivateLinux or MmapFixed))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: MmapAnonFd))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: MmapNoOffset))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.x64Syscall(X64LinuxSyscallMmap))
	end 'lowerOsCommitPagesLinux'

	// Lower `memFill(dest, byteCount, value)` — the destination and the length into the two registers the
	// size ladder reads, then the macro-op that IS the ladder. NOT OS-routed and not target-gated: a byte
	// fill is arithmetic and stores, so this is the same sequence on every x64 OS.
	//
	// The pre-move shape every OS-call lowering in this file uses, and it is sound for their reason: each
	// physical arg-register def is forbidden to a value live across it, so the count cannot be coloured into
	// the cursor register that the first move has already clobbered, and the ladder's own `implicitDefs`
	// closes the window at exactly the op that consumes them (`sweepEstablishedRegisters`).
	//
	// ⭐ The fill BYTE is moved nowhere. It is a compile-time constant, broadcast across the word HERE and
	// carried on the op as an immediate the encoder materialises — which is the entire reason `StdOp.memFill`
	// takes a `ParsedInt` rather than a third `ValueId` (see that op, and `memFillPatternWord`).
	function lowerMemFill(destId ValueId, byteCountId ValueId, value ParsedInt, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(MemFillCursorReg), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(destId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(MemFillCountReg), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(byteCountId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.x64MemFill(memFillByte(value)))
	end 'lowerMemFill'

	// Lower `atomicCas(result, addr, expected, replacement)` (F2) — the three-instruction x86 sequence:
	//   mov rax, expected           ; the comparand into the accumulator cmpxchg reads implicitly
	//   lock cmpxchg [addr], repl   ; ZF=1 + store on a match, else ZF=0 and rax = the observed word
	//   setz result                 ; result = 1 on success / 0 on failure — the op's stated contract
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SHAPE IS `lowerDivMod`'s, AND FOR THE IDENTICAL REASON.** cmpxchg pins its comparand to RAX
	// exactly as `idiv` pins its dividend, so the value is moved into the physical register first and the
	// remaining operands stay VIRTUAL — the allocator keeps them out of RAX because
	// `x64LockCmpxchg`'s `implicitUses`/`implicitDefs` name it (`forbidOperandsFromImplicit` covers even an
	// operand that DIES here, which `applyForbidden` alone would miss).
	//
	// ⚠ **shv2's THIRD INTEGER ARGUMENT REGISTER IS RAX**, so a CAS whose `expected` arrived as argument 3
	// is already in the accumulator and this `mov` self-elides through the ordinary coalescer. There is
	// deliberately no hand-detection of that case: the implicit pair is what makes it safe, and a special
	// case would be a second, weaker statement of the same fact.
	//
	// ⚠ **NOTHING MAY BE SPLICED BETWEEN THE PRE-MOVE AND THE CAS, AND THAT IS THE ESTABLISHED-REGISTER
	// WINDOW rather than adjacency.** The `mov rax, …` writes a PHYSICAL register the next op consumes, and
	// `sweepEstablishedRegisters` closes that window at the op whose `implicitDefs` name it — which is why
	// `x64LockCmpxchg` must declare RAX as a DEF and not only as a use. It is the identical arrangement
	// `lowerDivMod` and every OS-call argument pre-move in this file already rest on.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND NOTHING MAY BE SPLICED BETWEEN THE CAS AND THE `setcc`**, which reads ZF. The only ops a
	// post-ISEL pass splices are `mov`-shaped — a spill store, a reload, a rematerialized constant — and
	// none of them writes flags (see `TargetOpMeta`'s header on where the flags fact lives). The
	// `cmp`+`setccReg` pair `lowerCmp` emits rests on exactly this, one op family over.
	//
	// `setccReg` zero-extends its byte result to the full register (see the op), so the boolean is a clean
	// 0/1 with no separate `movzx`.
	function lowerAtomicCas(resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, expectedId ValueId, replacementId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(expectedId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.x64LockCmpxchg(TargetVReg.virtual(addrId), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(replacementId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.setccReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), cond: X64CondCode.equal))
	end 'lowerAtomicCas'

	// Lower `atomicRmw(result, addr, operand, opcode)` (F2). Two shapes, chosen by whether anything READS
	// the prior value:
	//   result read     → `lock xadd [addr], result`, `result` a reuse-def over `operand` so the allocator
	//                     supplies the copy into the destination xadd's two-address form requires.
	//   result unread   → `lock add [addr], operand` — the same atomic update with no destination at all.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE UNREAD SHAPE IS A REGISTER SAVING, NOT A CODE-SIZE ONE, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS WORTH A
	// SECOND PATH.** `atomicInc`/`atomicDec` — the reason `StdAtomicRmwOpcode` exists rather than three
	// variants — are `atomicRmw(_, addr, ±1, add)`: a result nothing reads. Through `lock xadd` that
	// result is still a DEF, so it takes a real register (`TargetLiveness.addOpTransientPressure`'s
	// DEAD-DEF transient: *"a def that is live at NO program point still gets a real register"*) plus the
	// allocator's copy into it, on the path a refcount update runs everywhere. `lock add` reads two
	// registers and writes none.
	//
	// The deadness question is `self.valuesReadFromRegisters`, the per-function census this backend already
	// computes for `materializeCompareIfRead` — the same shape, the same set, no new analysis.
	//
	// ⚠ **`lock inc`/`lock dec` ARE NOT EMITTED, AND THE MISSING PIECE IS NAMED.** They would additionally
	// fold a ±1 operand into the opcode, dropping the `mov reg, 1` that materializes it. That needs a
	// Std-tier CONSTANT query — "is the value behind `operandId` the literal 1?" — and this isel has none:
	// constants reach it only pre-folded into `binOpImm`/`cmpImm` operand forms, and `atomicRmw` has no
	// immediate form to fold into. Adding one is a Std-tier `atomicRmwImm` variant plus a `FoldConstOperands`
	// arm, which is dialect vocabulary this rung deliberately does not add ahead of a measured consumer.
	function lowerAtomicRmw(resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, operandId ValueId, opcode StdAtomicRmwOpcode, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let priorValueIsRead = self.valuesReadFromRegisters.contains(resultId)
		let insn = match opcode 'op'
			add gives x64AtomicAdd(resultId, addrId: addrId, operandId: operandId, priorValueIsRead: priorValueIsRead)
		end 'op'
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: insn)
	end 'lowerAtomicRmw'

	// Lower `currentThreadToken(result)` (F2) — the one member of this family with a genuine OS split, so it
	// is routed through the same `x64OsCallKind` door every OS-dependent lowering in this file uses.
	//
	// Windows: `mov result, gs:[0x30]`, the TEB self-pointer. One instruction, no call, no setup.
	// Linux: the `gettid` syscall, whose answer lands in RAX like every other syscall's — see
	// `StdOp.currentThreadToken` for why a raw static Linux binary cannot read `fs:[0]` instead, and
	// `X64LinuxSyscallGettid` for the number.
	function lowerCurrentThreadToken(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.x64TebSelfPointer(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId)))
			syscall then self.lowerCurrentThreadTokenLinux(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerCurrentThreadToken'

	// `gettid()` — a ZERO-argument syscall, so there is nothing to marshal; only the answer is captured out
	// of RAX, exactly as `lowerOsAllocPages` captures `mmap`'s.
	function lowerCurrentThreadTokenLinux(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.x64Syscall(X64LinuxSyscallGettid))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax)))
	end 'lowerCurrentThreadTokenLinux'

	// Lower `osWaitHandle(handle, timeoutMs)` to `WaitForSingleObject(handle, timeoutMs)` — args rcx/rdx — and
	// CAPTURE the wait result from RAX (the one subprocess op whose result is read: `__gt_proc_check` polls with
	// `timeoutMs = 0` and tests `== WAIT_OBJECT_0`).
	function lowerOsWaitHandle(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, timeoutMsId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsWaitHandleWin64(resultId, handleId: handleId, timeoutMsId: timeoutMsId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsWaitHandle: WaitForSingleObject is x64-windows only — the green-thread subprocess substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsWaitHandle'

	function lowerOsWaitHandleWin64(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, timeoutMsId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.lowerOsWin64Call2(IatSlot.WaitForSingleObject, arg0: handleId, arg1: timeoutMsId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax)))
	end 'lowerOsWaitHandleWin64'

	// The shared Win64 ONE-argument import-call shape: the single value into rcx, then the `iatCall`. Sleep and
	// CloseHandle both take exactly it, factored to one place so their rcx spelling cannot drift.
	function lowerOsWin64Call1(slot IatSlot, arg0 ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(arg0)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(slot)))
	end 'lowerOsWin64Call1'

	// The shared Win64 THREE-argument import-call shape (rcx/rdx/r8, then the `iatCall`), which
	// `GetEnvironmentVariableA` takes. Its two siblings' argument for existing applies unchanged: the
	// allocator's per-arg-register def liveness keeps each still-needed source out of a register clobbered
	// before its own move, so the emission order is free.
	function lowerOsWin64Call3(slot IatSlot, arg0 ValueId, arg1 ValueId, arg2 ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(arg0)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(arg1)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(arg2)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(slot)))
	end 'lowerOsWin64Call3'

	// The shared Win64 TWO-argument import-call shape (arg0 in rcx, arg1 in rdx, then the `iatCall`) that
	// GetExitCodeProcess and WaitForSingleObject both take. The register allocator's per-arg-register def
	// liveness keeps each still-needed source out of a register clobbered before its own move, so the emission
	// order is free — the same guarantee `lowerOsWriteStdout`/`lowerOsAllocPages` rely on.
	function lowerOsWin64Call2(slot IatSlot, arg0 ValueId, arg1 ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(arg0)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(arg1)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(slot)))
	end 'lowerOsWin64Call2'

	// --- P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the IOCP overlapped-read substrate lowerings ---
	// Every one routes on `x64OsCallKind` and REJECTS the syscall path — the whole substrate is x64-windows-gated.

	// A Win64 outgoing stack argument set to an IMMEDIATE: `mov rax, imm ; mov [rsp + shadow + slot*8], rax`. rax is
	// caller-saved (clobbered by the coming call anyway) so it is a free scratch, exactly as `lowerOsProcessSpawnWin64`
	// uses it. Shared by every >4-argument IOCP call so the `[rsp+disp]` spelling lives in one place.
	function emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock IrBlock, slot MachineWord, value MachineWord)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), value: value))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(slot), src: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
	end 'emitWin64StackArgImm'

	// Capture a Win64 import call's primary result (RAX) into `resultId` — the one place the five value-defining IOCP
	// ops read their handle/BOOL/error back, mirroring `lowerOsWaitHandleWin64`'s RAX capture.
	function emitCaptureRax(dstBlock IrBlock, resultId ValueId)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax)))
	end 'emitCaptureRax'

	// Lower `osPipeCreate` to the five-call overlapped-read-pipe sequence (ported from v1's
	// `__subp_create_overlapped_pipe`, read path). The two handles land in `[readPtr]`/`[writePtr]`; each later call
	// RELOADS the read end from `[readPtr]` rather than pinning a register across an intervening `iatCall`.
	function lowerOsPipeCreate(namePtrId ValueId, saPtrId ValueId, iocpId ValueId, readPtrId ValueId, writePtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsPipeCreateWin64(namePtrId, saPtrId: saPtrId, iocpId: iocpId, readPtrId: readPtrId, writePtrId: writePtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsPipeCreate: the overlapped named pipe is x64-windows only — the IOCP substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsPipeCreate'

	function lowerOsPipeCreateWin64(namePtrId ValueId, saPtrId ValueId, iocpId ValueId, readPtrId ValueId, writePtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		// 1) CreateNamedPipeW(name, PipeReadOpenMode, PipeMessageMode, PipeMaxInstances, nOutBuf, nInBuf, timeout, NULL).
		//    Stack args 5-8 first (rax scratch, while every arg register is free), then the register args.
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 0, value: PipeBufferSize)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 1, value: PipeBufferSize)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 2, value: PipeDefaultTimeout)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 3, value: NullPointer)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(namePtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: PipeReadOpenMode))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: PipeMessageMode))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: PipeMaxInstances))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.CreateNamedPipeW)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(TargetVReg.virtual(readPtrId), disp: 0, src: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		// 2) CreateFileW(name, GenericWrite, FileShareNone, saPtr, OpenExisting, FileAttributeNormal, NULL) → write end.
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 0, value: OpenExisting)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 1, value: FileAttributeNormal)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 2, value: NullPointer)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(namePtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: GenericWrite))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: FileShareNone))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(saPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.CreateFileW)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(TargetVReg.virtual(writePtrId), disp: 0, src: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		// 3) SetHandleInformation(readEnd, HandleFlagInherit, HandleFlagClear) — strip inheritance from the parent read end.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), base: TargetVReg.virtual(readPtrId), disp: 0, width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: HandleFlagInherit))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: HandleFlagClear))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.SetHandleInformation)))
		// 4) CreateIoCompletionPort(readEnd, iocp, key=0, threads=0) — register the read end with the process IOCP.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), base: TargetVReg.virtual(readPtrId), disp: 0, width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(iocpId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: IocpNoCompletionKey))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: IocpNoConcurrentThreads))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.CreateIoCompletionPort)))
		// 5) SetFileCompletionNotificationModes(readEnd, FileSkipCompletionPortOnSuccess) — a SYNC read posts no packet.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), base: TargetVReg.virtual(readPtrId), disp: 0, width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: FileSkipCompletionPortOnSuccess))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.SetFileCompletionNotificationModes)))
	end 'lowerOsPipeCreateWin64'

	// Lower `osIocpCreate` to `CreateIoCompletionPort(INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE, NULL, 0, 1)`. INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE is a
	// FULL 64-bit -1, so it uses `movRegImm` (64-bit) — `movRegImm32` would zero-extend to 0x00000000FFFFFFFF, a
	// different (invalid) handle.
	// ⭐ **R4.1: `CreateFileA(path, GENERIC_READ, FILE_SHARE_READ, NULL, OPEN_EXISTING, FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL,
	// NULL)`** — the file-IO runtime's OPEN primitive, and the first site in this file that opens an actual
	// FILE rather than the far end of a named pipe.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE `A` ENTRY POINT, NOT THE `W` ONE ALREADY IMPORTED FOR THE PIPES — see `IatSlot.CreateFileA`.**
	// A Maxon path is a UTF-8 BYTE buffer, and `CreateFileW` would read those bytes as UTF-16 code units,
	// which is wrong even for pure ASCII.
	//
	// The failure classification is NOT here: this yields the raw handle (or `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE`) and
	// `ManagedFileRuntime`'s builder-built Std reads `osLastError` to choose the error variant. See the
	// `osFileOpenRead` op's own comment in `StdDialect` for why that split is deliberate.
	function lowerOsFileOpenRead(resultId ValueId, pathPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osFileOpenRead")
		self.emitWin64CreateFileA(resultId, pathPtrId: pathPtrId, access: GenericRead, shareMode: FileShareRead, disposition: OpenExisting, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerOsFileOpenRead'

	// ⭐ **R4.2: `CreateFileA(path, GENERIC_WRITE, FILE_SHARE_READ | FILE_SHARE_WRITE, NULL, CREATE_ALWAYS,
	// FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL, NULL)`.** The read open's call with three constants changed — see
	// `FileShareReadWrite` and `CreateAlways` for why each is what it is.
	//
	// ⭐ **`openWriteExecutable` REACHES THIS SAME LOWERING**, because its only difference in either
	// reference is a Unix 0755 mode bit and shv2's file IO is host-only Windows by the R4.1 ruling. See
	// `StdDialect.osFileOpenWrite`.
	function lowerOsFileOpenWrite(resultId ValueId, pathPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osFileOpenWrite")
		self.emitWin64CreateFileA(resultId, pathPtrId: pathPtrId, access: GenericWrite, shareMode: FileShareReadWrite, disposition: CreateAlways, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerOsFileOpenWrite'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE `CreateFileA` EMISSION, PARAMETERIZED BY THE THREE CONSTANTS THE TWO OPENS DIFFER IN.**
	// The read and write opens differ ONLY in `dwDesiredAccess`, `dwShareMode` and `dwCreationDisposition`;
	// the argument REGISTERS, the three stack slots, the entry point and the RAX capture are one shape, so
	// they are written once. Two copies is how one of them would quietly acquire a different `hTemplateFile`.
	//
	// Stack args 5-7 first (rax scratch, while every argument register is still free), then the register
	// args — the ordering `lowerOsPipeCreateWin64` establishes and for its reason. The access mask uses the
	// 64-bit `movRegImm` because `GENERIC_READ` is `0x80000000`, which reads as a sign bit in an imm32
	// spelling; one form for both keeps the two opens byte-comparable.
	function emitWin64CreateFileA(resultId ValueId, pathPtrId ValueId, access MachineWord, shareMode MachineWord, disposition MachineWord, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 0, value: disposition)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 1, value: FileAttributeNormal)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 2, value: NullPointer)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(pathPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: access))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: shareMode))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.CreateFileA)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'emitWin64CreateFileA'

	// --- The socket slice: eight ws2_32 lowerings ---
	//
	// ⭐ Each spells the Win32 constants its Std op deliberately does not carry, exactly as
	// `emitWin64CreateFileA` spells `GENERIC_READ`. Every one guards with `requireWin64Import` first: these
	// are Win32 imports, so a non-Windows target reaching them is a compiler bug that got past E3104 rather
	// than a user-facing rejection.

	function lowerOsSocketStartup(resultId ValueId, wsaDataPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osSocketStartup")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: WinsockVersion22))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(wsaDataPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.WSAStartup)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsSocketStartup'

	// `getaddrinfo(host, NULL, hints, out)` - the service argument is always NULL; see the op's own header
	// for why no port is ever formatted as a string.
	function lowerOsSocketResolve(resultId ValueId, hostPtrId ValueId, hintsPtrId ValueId, outPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osSocketResolve")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(hostPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(hintsPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(outPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.getaddrinfo)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsSocketResolve'

	function lowerOsSocketFreeAddrInfo(addrInfoPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osSocketFreeAddrInfo")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrInfoPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.freeaddrinfo)))
	end 'lowerOsSocketFreeAddrInfo'

	function lowerOsSocketCreate(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osSocketCreate")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: AfInet))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: SockStream))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: IpprotoTcp))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.socket)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsSocketCreate'

	function lowerOsSocketConnect(resultId ValueId, sockId ValueId, addrPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osSocketConnect")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sockId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: SockaddrInBytes))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.connect)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsSocketConnect'

	// `send`/`recv` differ ONLY in their import slot, so the register setup is written once. Two near-identical
	// emitters is the duplication this file's own conventions refuse.
	function emitWin64SocketTransfer(slot IatSlot, resultId ValueId, sockId ValueId, bufPtrId ValueId, lenId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sockId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(lenId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: SendRecvNoFlags))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(slot)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'emitWin64SocketTransfer'

	function lowerOsSocketSend(resultId ValueId, sockId ValueId, bufPtrId ValueId, lenId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osSocketSend")
		self.emitWin64SocketTransfer(IatSlot.send, resultId: resultId, sockId: sockId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, lenId: lenId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerOsSocketSend'

	function lowerOsSocketRecv(resultId ValueId, sockId ValueId, bufPtrId ValueId, lenId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osSocketRecv")
		self.emitWin64SocketTransfer(IatSlot.recv, resultId: resultId, sockId: sockId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, lenId: lenId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerOsSocketRecv'

	function lowerOsSocketClose(sockId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osSocketClose")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sockId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.closesocket)))
	end 'lowerOsSocketClose'

	// ⭐⭐ **`osCpuCount(result)` ⇒ `GetActiveProcessorCount(ALL_PROCESSOR_GROUPS)`** — one immediate into
	// rcx, one import call, and the count captured out of RAX. `emitCaptureRax` is the same capture
	// `osGetPid` takes for `GetCurrentProcessId`'s DWORD, and the answer is left EXACTLY as the OS gave it:
	// a 0 (the API's failure answer) reaches the caller, because the `< 1 ⇒ 1` clamp is builder-built Std's
	// (`CpuParallelRuntime.buildCpuCount`) and stubbing it here as well would be one contract in two places.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE REFERENCE CALLS `GetSystemInfo` AND THIS DOES NOT, WHICH IS A DECISION AND NOT AN OVERSIGHT.**
	// The bootstrap's WINDOWS emitter carves a 48-byte `SYSTEM_INFO` out of its own hand-built frame and
	// reads `dwNumberOfProcessors` at offset 0x20 (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:8076`); its arm64 emitter asks
	// `sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN)` and needs no buffer at all (`ARM64CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:115`) — which
	// is itself the evidence that the struct is a Windows shape rather than the question's.
	// shv2's lowerings do not build frames — a scratch buffer would have to arrive from Std, i.e. a Windows
	// struct in the target-neutral tier or a heap allocation for a 4-byte read (`StdOp.osCpuCount`'s comment
	// carries that argument in full). This entry point needs neither, and its answer is the STRICTLY BETTER
	// of the two on the one host class where they differ: `dwNumberOfProcessors` reports the CALLING
	// THREAD's processor group, so a 128-processor machine answers 64, while `ALL_PROCESSOR_GROUPS` answers
	// 128. On every host with at most 64 logical processors — one group, hence every machine either compiler
	// is tested on — the two are identical (MEASURED: both answer 12 on the 12-CPU development host).
	function lowerOsCpuCount(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osCpuCount")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: AllProcessorGroups))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetActiveProcessorCount)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsCpuCount'

	// ⭐⭐ **`osThreadCpuTicks(outPtr)` ⇒ `QueryThreadCycleTime((HANDLE)-2, outPtr)`** — one immediate into
	// rcx, the caller's `.data` scratch address into rdx, one `iatCall`. Nothing is captured from RAX: the
	// API answers through the out-param, and the `BOOL` it returns is DISCARDED for `osFileClose`'s reason
	// one shape over — its only documented failure is an invalid handle, and this handle is a compile-time
	// constant naming the running thread, so there is no runtime condition under which it can fail and
	// nothing a caller measuring its own cost could do if it somehow did.
	function lowerOsThreadCpuTicks(outPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osThreadCpuTicks")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: CurrentThreadPseudoHandle))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(outPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.QueryThreadCycleTime)))
	end 'lowerOsThreadCpuTicks'

	// ⭐ **THE x64-WINDOWS GATE FOR EVERY OP THAT LOWERS TO A WIN32 IMPORT, ASKED ONCE.** All of them are
	// x64-windows only, and `SemanticCheck.requireTargetSupportsCallee` refuses such a program at its own
	// source span (E3104) long before lowering — so this is the backstop for a compiler bug that got one
	// past that gate, never the user-facing rejection. A copy of the same `match x64OsCallKind` wrapper per
	// op is the duplication this replaces; the op's name is the only thing that differed between them, so
	// it is the only thing passed in.
	// ⚠ **THE SENTENCE NAMES THE OP AND NOT A RUNG, BECAUSE THE CALLERS ARE NO LONGER ONE FAMILY.** It read
	// "file IO is x64-windows-gated at R4.1/R4.2" while every caller was a file or directory primitive;
	// `osCommandLine` joined and is neither, so that clause became a diagnostic naming a rung the reader's
	// op has nothing to do with — D12's wrong-noun defect, at the helper that outlived its first family. The
	// gate itself is one fact for all of them (`SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate`), so that is what
	// the message points at.
	// ⛔ **AND THE NAME OUTLIVED IT TOO, WHICH THAT FIX MISSED.** It was `requireWin64FileIo` until the
	// machine query joined, by which point MOST of its callers were not file IO at all: the command line,
	// the standard handles, the executable path, the DebugStream segment, the attached-subprocess
	// primitives and the whole `ws2_32` socket band all ask it too. (Count them off the call sites, never
	// off this sentence — a roster in prose has nothing between it and drift.) A helper named for one
	// caller family is the same wrong-noun defect one level up, and a reader checking "is this op file IO?"
	// against the name would have been told yes by every one of them.
	function requireWin64Import(opName String)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then return
			syscall then panic("lowerStdToX64: `{opName}` lowers to a Win32 import and is therefore x64-windows only. `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` refuses such a program at its own source span (E3104), so reaching here is a compiler bug that got one past that gate rather than the user-facing rejection")
		end 'route'
	end 'requireWin64Import'

	// ⭐ **R4.1: `CloseHandle(handle)`** — register-only (one argument in rcx), and the BOOL it returns is
	// DISCARDED, exactly as `osWriteStdout` discards `WriteFile`'s. A close that fails leaves a caller
	// nothing to do, and `close()` is documented idempotent, so there is no verdict worth capturing.
	function lowerOsFileClose(handleId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osFileClose")
		self.lowerOsWin64Call1(IatSlot.CloseHandle, arg0: handleId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerOsFileClose'

	// ⭐ **R4.2: `ReadFile(handle, buf, len, bytesOutPtr, NULL)`** — SYNCHRONOUS, and the exact mirror of
	// `osWriteFile`'s emission (which is why both go through `emitWin64FileIoCall`). The handle R4.1 opens
	// carries `FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL` with no `FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED` and is associated with no completion
	// port, so this is the read that handle supports; the bootstrap's overlapped `__io_submit_read` is a
	// different call for a different handle.
	function lowerOsReadFile(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, bufId ValueId, lenId ValueId, bytesOutPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osReadFile")
		self.emitWin64FileIoCall(IatSlot.ReadFile, resultId: resultId, handleId: handleId, bufId: bufId, lenId: lenId, bytesOutPtrId: bytesOutPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerOsReadFile'

	// ⭐ **R4.2: `GetFileSizeEx(handle, sizeOutPtr)`** — the 64-bit size lands in the caller's out-param
	// word and the BOOL verdict in `resultId`. Register-only (two arguments).
	function lowerOsFileSize(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, sizeOutPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osFileSize")
		self.lowerOsWin64Call2(IatSlot.GetFileSizeEx, arg0: handleId, arg1: sizeOutPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsFileSize'

	// ⭐ **R4.2: `DeleteFileA(path)`** — register-only, the BOOL captured. The `A` entry point for the
	// reason every path-taking import here uses it: a Maxon path is a UTF-8 byte buffer.
	function lowerOsFileDelete(resultId ValueId, pathPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osFileDelete")
		self.lowerOsWin64Call1(IatSlot.DeleteFileA, arg0: pathPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsFileDelete'

	// ⭐ **R4.2: `MoveFileExA(old, new, MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING | MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH)`** — three
	// register arguments, the BOOL captured. See `MoveFileReplaceWriteThrough` for the flags.
	function lowerOsFileRename(resultId ValueId, oldPathPtrId ValueId, newPathPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osFileRename")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(oldPathPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(newPathPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: MoveFileReplaceWriteThrough))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.MoveFileExA)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsFileRename'

	// ⭐ **R4.2: `FindFirstFileA(path, findDataPtr)`** — register-only; the search HANDLE (or
	// `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE`) is captured and the 320-byte `WIN32_FIND_DATAA` record is written through the
	// caller's buffer pointer.
	function lowerOsFileFindFirst(resultId ValueId, pathPtrId ValueId, findDataPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osFileFindFirst")
		self.lowerOsWin64Call2(IatSlot.FindFirstFileA, arg0: pathPtrId, arg1: findDataPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsFileFindFirst'

	// ⭐ **R4.2: `FindClose(handle)`** — register-only, the BOOL discarded for the reason `osFileClose`
	// discards `CloseHandle`'s.
	function lowerOsFileFindClose(handleId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osFileFindClose")
		self.lowerOsWin64Call1(IatSlot.FindClose, arg0: handleId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerOsFileFindClose'

	// ⭐ **R4.3: `FindNextFileA(handle, findDataPtr)`** — register-only; the BOOL is captured because a FALSE
	// is the only thing that distinguishes end-of-iteration from an OS error, and `ManagedDirectoryRuntime`
	// makes that distinction from `GetLastError`.
	function lowerOsFileFindNext(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, findDataPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osFileFindNext")
		self.lowerOsWin64Call2(IatSlot.FindNextFileA, arg0: handleId, arg1: findDataPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsFileFindNext'

	// ⭐ **R4.3: `CreateDirectoryA(path, NULL)`** — the BOOL captured. The `lpSecurityAttributes` NULL is an
	// IMMEDIATE, which is why this does not go through `lowerOsWin64Call2` (that one moves two virtual
	// registers): a second argument that is a constant has no ValueId to move from.
	function lowerOsDirCreate(resultId ValueId, pathPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osDirCreate")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(pathPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.CreateDirectoryA)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsDirCreate'

	// ⭐ **R4.3: `GetCurrentDirectoryA(bufLen, bufPtr)`** — register-only, and note that the LENGTH is the
	// first argument and the buffer the second, which is the reverse of every other path-taking import in
	// this file. The three-way answer (0 / fits / required size) is classified in builder-built Std.
	function lowerOsDirCurrentPath(resultId ValueId, bufLenId ValueId, bufPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osDirCurrentPath")
		self.lowerOsWin64Call2(IatSlot.GetCurrentDirectoryA, arg0: bufLenId, arg1: bufPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsDirCurrentPath'

	// ⭐ **R4.3: `GetFileAttributesA(path)`** — register-only, the raw attribute mask captured. See the op's
	// own comment in `StdDialect` for why this import exists beside `FindFirstFileA` rather than being
	// folded into it.
	function lowerOsFileAttributes(resultId ValueId, pathPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osFileAttributes")
		self.lowerOsWin64Call1(IatSlot.GetFileAttributesA, arg0: pathPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsFileAttributes'

	// ⭐ **`GetCommandLineA()`** — the ONLY import in this file with no arguments to marshal, so there is no
	// `lowerOsWin64Call0` to reach for and none is invented: the `iatCall` IS the whole call, and a helper
	// whose body is one `appendOp` would be the thin wrapper the house rules forbid. `GetLastError` (the
	// other zero-argument import) is emitted the same way one function up.
	//
	// The returned pointer is into the PEB and is owned by the OS — never freed, and the same address on
	// every call. `CommandLineRuntime` copies out of it and frees only its own scratch.
	function lowerOsCommandLine(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osCommandLine")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetCommandLineA)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsCommandLine'

	// ⭐ **`GetStdHandle(which)`** — ONE marshalled argument and a captured RAX. It is close to what
	// `lowerOsWriteStdoutWin64` opens with, and deliberately not shared with it: that one goes on to consume
	// the handle in RAX within the same block, while this one CAPTURES it as a Std value the allocator then
	// keeps live across whatever the builder does next.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SELECTOR ARRIVES AS A VALUE, NOT AS AN IMMEDIATE, SINCE THE SUBPROCESS RUNG** — and this
	// paragraph opened by saying the opposite, two lines above a body that marshals it. It was
	// `movRegImm32 rcx, StdInputHandle` while standard INPUT was the only stream the language could ask for;
	// `OutputDestination.inherit` asks for the other two, and one op reading a selector beats three ops
	// differing in an immediate. The DWORD-ness is unchanged and now lives at the producer
	// (`ConsoleRuntime.StdInputHandleSelector` and its two siblings are the zero-extended 32-bit spellings),
	// so the 64-bit move here delivers exactly the same ECX the immediate did.
	//
	// The handle is the OS's, not a duplicate: `__con_read_stdin` reads through it and must never close it,
	// and neither must a spawn that hands it to a child's STARTUPINFO.
	function lowerOsStdHandle(resultId ValueId, whichId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osStdHandle")
		self.lowerOsWin64Call1(IatSlot.GetStdHandle, arg0: whichId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsStdHandle'

	// ⭐ **`GetEnvironmentVariableA(name, buf, len)` then `OpenFileMappingA(FILE_MAP_ALL_ACCESS, FALSE, buf)`
	// — the DebugStream producer's attach, in one op** (Workstream O3). The env value is pure scratch that
	// no Std value ever reads, which is the whole reason the two calls are one op rather than two: splitting
	// them would put a buffer pointer into the IR for no consumer.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS BRANCH-FREE, AND THE UNSET CASE FALLS OUT.** `__slab_alloc` hands back ZEROED memory, so a
	// variable that is not set leaves `buf` naming the EMPTY string — and `OpenFileMappingA("")` fails and
	// returns NULL, which is the same answer "no monitor is listening" gets by every other route. One value
	// out, one test at the caller. (`GetEnvironmentVariableA`'s own return, the character count, is
	// therefore discarded: it can say nothing the empty buffer does not.)
	//
	// ⚠ `bufPtr` is live ACROSS the first `iatCall` and needs no hand-holding: it is a VIRTUAL operand of
	// this op, so the allocator forces it callee-saved exactly as `CreateProcessA`'s `procInfoPtr` is.
	function lowerOsOpenNamedSegment(resultId ValueId, namePtrId ValueId, bufPtrId ValueId, bufLenId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osOpenNamedSegment")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(namePtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufLenId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetEnvironmentVariableA)))

		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: FileMapAllAccess))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.OpenFileMappingA)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsOpenNamedSegment'

	// ⭐ **`MapViewOfFile(handle, FILE_MAP_ALL_ACCESS, 0, 0, size)`** — five arguments, four in registers
	// and the byte count on the stack past the shadow space (the `CreateFileA` stack-arg template).
	//
	// A NULL handle maps nothing and returns NULL, so `osOpenNamedSegment`'s failure flows through here
	// untested for the reason it flowed into it — see that lowering. `size` is the WHOLE segment length,
	// and it is the CONSUMER's number: a view longer than the section returns NULL, which is why
	// `DebugStreamRuntime` derives it from the reference's own three parts rather than choosing one.
	function lowerOsMapNamedSegment(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osMapNamedSegment")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(0), src: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(handleId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: FileMapAllAccess))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.MapViewOfFile)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsMapNamedSegment'

	// ⭐ **`GetTickCount64()`** — no arguments to marshal, so the `iatCall` IS the whole call and there is
	// no helper to reach for, exactly as `GetCommandLineA` above states.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THIS COUNTER AND NOT `osReadClock`'s QPC, AND THE REASON IS THE OTHER PROCESS.** `maxon
	// monitor` seeds the segment header's `start_timestamp` with `Environment.TickCount64` — GetTickCount64
	// — and every ring entry carries `now - start` as a millisecond delta. Two different clocks either end
	// would make that subtraction meaningless, and nothing would report it.
	function lowerOsTickCountMs(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osTickCountMs")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetTickCount64)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsTickCountMs'

	// ⭐ **W5: `GetModuleFileNameA(NULL, bufPtr, bufLen)`** — the character count captured, which is what
	// tells the builder's retry loop "fits" from "truncated".
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS IS WHERE THE OP'S FIELD ORDER MEETS kernel32's, AND THEY DISAGREE.** The op reads
	// `(result, bufLen, bufPtr)` because that is `osDirCurrentPath`'s shape and one shape is better than
	// two at the target-neutral tier; `GetModuleFileNameA` takes the BUFFER second and the SIZE third,
	// where `GetCurrentDirectoryA` takes them the other way round. The transposition is one line each and
	// it belongs here, in the only function that knows the ABI.
	//
	// ⚠ It cannot use `lowerOsWin64Call2`: that helper moves TWO virtual registers into rcx/rdx, and the
	// first argument here is an IMMEDIATE with no ValueId to move from — exactly the case `lowerOsDirCreate`
	// documents, whose hand-written form this follows.
	function lowerOsExecutablePath(resultId ValueId, bufLenId ValueId, bufPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.requireWin64Import("osExecutablePath")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: CurrentModuleHandle))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufLenId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetModuleFileNameA)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsExecutablePath'

	function lowerOsIocpCreate(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsIocpCreateWin64(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsIocpCreate: CreateIoCompletionPort is x64-windows only — the IOCP substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsIocpCreate'

	function lowerOsIocpCreateWin64(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: IocpInvalidHandleValue))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: IocpNoCompletionKey))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: IocpSingleConcurrentThread))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.CreateIoCompletionPort)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsIocpCreateWin64'

	// Lower `osEventCreate` to `CreateEventA(NULL, FALSE, FALSE, NULL)` — an AUTO-reset, initially-unsignaled event.
	function lowerOsEventCreate(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsEventCreateWin64(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsEventCreate: CreateEventA is x64-windows only — the IOCP substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsEventCreate'

	function lowerOsEventCreateWin64(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: CreateEventManualReset))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: CreateEventInitialUnsignaled))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.CreateEventA)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsEventCreateWin64'

	// Lower `osThreadCreate(entryPtr)` to `CreateThread(NULL, 0, entryPtr, NULL, 0, NULL)` — start the IOCP completion
	// thread on its own OS stack. `entryPtr` (arg 3, a `.text` address from a `funcAddr`) → r8; args 5-6 on the stack.
	function lowerOsThreadCreate(resultId ValueId, entryPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsThreadCreateWin64(resultId, entryPtrId: entryPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsThreadCreate: CreateThread is x64-windows only — the IOCP substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsThreadCreate'

	function lowerOsThreadCreateWin64(resultId ValueId, entryPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 0, value: ThreadRunImmediately)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 1, value: NullPointer)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: ThreadDefaultStackSize))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(entryPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.CreateThread)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsThreadCreateWin64'

	// Lower `osReadOverlapped(handle, buf, cap, bytesOutPtr, ovl)` to `ReadFile(handle, buf, cap, bytesOutPtr, ovl)`.
	// `bytesOutPtr` (arg 4) → r9; `ovl` (arg 5) is the ONE stack argument. The BOOL result is captured into `resultId`.
	function lowerOsReadOverlapped(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, bufId ValueId, capId ValueId, bytesOutPtrId ValueId, ovlId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsReadOverlappedWin64(resultId, handleId: handleId, bufId: bufId, capId: capId, bytesOutPtrId: bytesOutPtrId, ovlId: ovlId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsReadOverlapped: ReadFile (overlapped) is x64-windows only — the IOCP substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsReadOverlapped'

	function lowerOsReadOverlappedWin64(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, bufId ValueId, capId ValueId, bytesOutPtrId ValueId, ovlId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		// The one stack arg (`ovl`, slot 0) FIRST via rax scratch, then the four register args.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(0), src: TargetVReg.virtual(ovlId), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(handleId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(capId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bytesOutPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.ReadFile)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsReadOverlappedWin64'

	// Lower `osLastError` to `GetLastError()` — no arguments, the DWORD result captured into `resultId`. Called
	// IMMEDIATELY after a failing `osReadOverlapped` (no intervening call clobbers the thread's last-error).
	function lowerOsLastError(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsLastErrorWin64(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsLastError: GetLastError is x64-windows only — the IOCP substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsLastError'

	function lowerOsLastErrorWin64(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetLastError)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsLastErrorWin64'

	// Lower `osCancelIo(handle, ovl)` to `CancelIoEx(handle, ovl)` — args rcx/rdx, result ignored. The
	// in-flight-reader-promise DROP path cancels the specific pending overlapped read so the completion thread
	// drains it (an ERROR_OPERATION_ABORTED packet) and is DONE with the GT before the drop arm frees it.
	function lowerOsCancelIo(handleId ValueId, ovlId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsWin64Call2(IatSlot.CancelIoEx, arg0: handleId, arg1: ovlId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsCancelIo: CancelIoEx is x64-windows only — the IOCP substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsCancelIo'

	// Lower `osGetPid` to `GetCurrentProcessId()` — no arguments, the DWORD process id captured into `resultId`.
	// Makes the overlapped pipe name cross-process unique (its high 32 bits).
	function lowerOsGetPid(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsGetPidWin64(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsGetPid: GetCurrentProcessId is x64-windows only — the IOCP substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsGetPid'

	function lowerOsGetPidWin64(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetCurrentProcessId)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'lowerOsGetPidWin64'

	// Lower `osPipeCreateOutbound(namePtr, saPtr, childReadPtr, parentWritePtr)` (streaming slice 1b) — the
	// direction MIRROR of `osPipeCreate`. The parent WRITE end is a synchronous OUTBOUND server pipe (no
	// FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED, no IOCP); the child READ end is a GENERIC_READ inheritable client end.
	function lowerOsPipeCreateOutbound(namePtrId ValueId, saPtrId ValueId, childReadPtrId ValueId, parentWritePtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsPipeCreateOutboundWin64(namePtrId, saPtrId: saPtrId, childReadPtrId: childReadPtrId, parentWritePtrId: parentWritePtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsPipeCreateOutbound: the streaming stdin pipe is x64-windows only — the subprocess substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsPipeCreateOutbound'

	function lowerOsPipeCreateOutboundWin64(namePtrId ValueId, saPtrId ValueId, childReadPtrId ValueId, parentWritePtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		// 1) CreateNamedPipeW(name, PipeWriteOpenMode, PipeMessageMode, PipeMaxInstances, nOutBuf, nInBuf, timeout, NULL) → parent WRITE end.
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 0, value: PipeBufferSize)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 1, value: PipeBufferSize)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 2, value: PipeDefaultTimeout)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 3, value: NullPointer)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(namePtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: PipeWriteOpenMode))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: PipeMessageMode))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), value: PipeMaxInstances))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.CreateNamedPipeW)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(TargetVReg.virtual(parentWritePtrId), disp: 0, src: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		// 2) CreateFileW(name, GenericRead, FileShareNone, saPtr, OpenExisting, FileAttributeNormal, NULL) → inheritable child READ end.
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 0, value: OpenExisting)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 1, value: FileAttributeNormal)
		self.emitWin64StackArgImm(dstBlock, slot: 2, value: NullPointer)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(namePtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: GenericRead))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: FileShareNone))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(saPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.CreateFileW)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(TargetVReg.virtual(childReadPtrId), disp: 0, src: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		// 3) SetHandleInformation(parentWriteEnd, HandleFlagInherit, HandleFlagClear) — strip inheritance from the parent write end.
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), base: TargetVReg.virtual(parentWritePtrId), disp: 0, width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), value: HandleFlagInherit))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), value: HandleFlagClear))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.SetHandleInformation)))
	end 'lowerOsPipeCreateOutboundWin64'

	// Lower `osWriteFile(handle, buf, len, bytesOutPtr)` (streaming slice 1b) to `WriteFile(handle, buf, len,
	// bytesOutPtr, NULL)` — a synchronous write to the child's stdin pipe. `bytesOutPtr` (the caller's out-param
	// slot) goes in r9; the 5th arg (lpOverlapped = NULL) is the one stack argument (slot 0). The BOOL is captured.
	function lowerOsWriteFile(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, bufId ValueId, lenId ValueId, bytesOutPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match x64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.emitWin64FileIoCall(IatSlot.WriteFile, resultId: resultId, handleId: handleId, bufId: bufId, lenId: lenId, bytesOutPtrId: bytesOutPtrId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			syscall then panic("lowerOsWriteFile: the streaming stdin write is x64-windows only — the subprocess substrate is x64-windows-gated at this rung")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsWriteFile'

	// ⭐ **THE SHARED `ReadFile`/`WriteFile` EMISSION.** The two imports take the IDENTICAL Win64 shape —
	// `(handle, buf, len, bytesOutPtr)` in rcx/rdx/r8/r9 plus a NULL `lpOverlapped` in stack slot 0, BOOL
	// in RAX — so the entry point is the only thing that differs and is the only thing passed in (R4.2).
	// The one stack arg goes FIRST via the rax scratch, then the four register args; the register
	// allocator's per-arg-register def liveness keeps a still-needed source out of a register clobbered
	// before its own move, so the emission order is free (the same guarantee `lowerOsWriteStdout` relies on).
	function emitWin64FileIoCall(slot IatSlot, resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, bufId ValueId, lenId ValueId, bytesOutPtrId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegImm32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), value: NullPointer))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp), disp: win64OutgoingArgDisp(0), src: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rax), width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(handleId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdx), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(lenId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.r9), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bytesOutPtrId)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(slot)))
		self.emitCaptureRax(dstBlock, resultId: resultId)
	end 'emitWin64FileIoCall'

	// Lower a Std `cmpImm` (constant right operand) to `cmp reg, imm32` — the same two-part
	// protocol `lowerCmp` uses, so the fold that moved the right operand into an immediate
	// changes only the compare INSTRUCTION, never what the compare MEANS. The literal
	// occupies no register.
	function lowerCmpImm(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, imm Imm32, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.cmpRegImm32(TargetVReg.virtual(lhs), imm: imm))
		// ⚠ THE OP'S OWN TYPE, NOT A RESTATED `StdType.i64` (X5 review). This read `operandType:
		// StdType.i64` under a comment beginning "`cmpImm` carries no `operandType`" — true when it was
		// written and made FALSE by X5, which gave the op the field and updated the SIBLING reader
		// (`recordFusableCompare`) without updating this one. Both ask `materializeCompareIfRead` the same
		// question and it reads the type for exactly one thing, `stdTypeIsFloat`; two spellings of one
		// fact, and the stale one was the more inviting, because a later editor could read "i64" as a
		// WIDTH. `foldConstOperands` refuses a float rhs (no SSE form takes an immediate), so the derived
		// answer is `false` today for the same reason the constant was `i64` — derived rather than
		// asserted is the only difference, and it is the difference that cannot go stale.
		self.materializeCompareIfRead(resultId, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerCmpImm'

	// Lower a Std `cmp` to an x64 `cmp reg, reg`. A comparison's answer lives in the EFLAGS
	// the instruction sets, and where it goes from there depends on who reads it — the two
	// consumers are INDEPENDENT and either, both, or neither may apply:
	//
	//   • the `condBranch` terminating this block, if `computeFusedCmps` proved this compare
	//     is the last flag-clobbering op before it ⇒ `lowerCondBranch` emits a `jcc` and no
	//     boolean is ever written to a register (the common `if a > b` case);
	//   • anything that reads the result as a VALUE — a `let`, a call argument, a phi operand
	//     on a short-circuit edge, an `if` in a LATER block ⇒ `setcc` writes the boolean into
	//     its register here.
	//
	// Both at once is legal and correct: `setcc`/`movzx` READ the flags, they do not write
	// them, so the `jcc` at the terminator still sees the flags this `cmp` set.
	function lowerCmp(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
			self.lowerFloatCmp(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			return
		end 'float'
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.cmpRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(lhs), rhsReg: TargetVReg.virtual(rhs)))
		self.materializeCompareIfRead(resultId, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerCmp'

	// Lower a FLOAT `cmp` to `ucomisd`, with the same two independent consumers `lowerCmp`
	// describes — the fused branch reads its flags, anything reading the result as a VALUE gets a
	// `setcc` sequence.
	//
	// ⭐ THE OPERANDS MAY BE SWAPPED HERE, and that is the whole NaN story for four of the six
	// predicates. `a < b` emits `ucomisd b, a`, and `floatCondCodeForPred` answers `above` to
	// match — one decision, spelled in two places that MUST be read together (see
	// `floatCmpSwapsOperands` for why `jb` would be wrong and why the swap is free).
	function lowerFloatCmp(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		requireScalarDouble(operandType, what: "lowerFloatCmp")
		let swapped = floatCmpSwapsOperands(pred)
		let cmpLhs = rhs if swapped else lhs
		let cmpRhs = lhs if swapped else rhs
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.ucomisdRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(cmpLhs), rhs: TargetVReg.virtual(cmpRhs)))
		self.materializeCompareIfRead(resultId, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerFloatCmp'

	// Write a compare's boolean into its register — but only when something actually reads it
	// as a value (`computeValuesReadFromRegisters`). A compare read ONLY by the branch it fuses with
	// needs no register at all, which is why `if a > b` still lowers to a bare `cmp` + `jcc`.
	function materializeCompareIfRead(resultId ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		if not self.valuesReadFromRegisters.contains(resultId) 'flagsOnly'
			return
		end 'flagsOnly'
		if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) and predicateIsEquality(pred) 'floatEquality'
			self.materializeFloatEquality(resultId, pred: pred, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			return
		end 'floatEquality'
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.setccReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), cond: condCodeForPred(pred, isFloat: stdTypeIsFloat(operandType))))
	end 'materializeCompareIfRead'

	// MATERIALIZE a float `==`/`!=` — the one compare whose boolean a single `setcc` cannot write.
	//
	// `ucomisd` aliases UNORDERED with EQUAL in every bit but PF, so the answer is TWO conditions,
	// and this is the SECOND lowering of the unordered edge rather than a free consequence of the
	// branch one: a compare whose result is a VALUE has no jump to hang a parity test on, so the
	// bit must fold into the byte instead. The ORDERED predicates need none of it — they
	// materialize with a single `seta`/`setae` on swapped operands, exactly like an integer's.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ BOTH FORMS ARE BUILT FROM `!=`, AND `!=` IS AN `lea`, BECAUSE ITS TWO `setcc`s ARE
	// MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE. `!=` is `setne OR setp`, and unordered SETS ZF — so `setne` is 0 in
	// exactly the case `setp` is 1, and the two can never both be 1:
	//
	//     ordered a==b   ZF=1 PF=0   ->  0 + 0 = 0   != is FALSE
	//     ordered a<b    ZF=0 PF=0   ->  1 + 0 = 1   != is TRUE
	//     ordered a>b    ZF=0 PF=0   ->  1 + 0 = 1   != is TRUE
	//     unordered      ZF=1 PF=1   ->  0 + 1 = 1   != is TRUE   (IEEE: nan != nan)
	//
	// Their OR is therefore their SUM, and a sum of two registers is `lea dest, [ne + p]` — a
	// THREE-OPERAND op whose destination is INDEPENDENT of its inputs, which writes no flags and
	// needs no copy. `==` is its complement, spelled as one: truth-test the `!=` value against
	// zero and `sete`.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ THE `lea` IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE — IT IS THE ONLY LEGAL SHAPE, and the two constraints
	// that force it are worth naming because each rejects an obvious alternative:
	//
	//   1. `setcc dest` + `and dest, scratch` DEFINES `dest` TWICE. The two-address `and` is a
	//      REUSE-of-`lhs` def and `lhs` would BE `dest`; Std ValueIds are SSA and the colorer
	//      relies on single-def (`xchgRegReg` is exempt only because it is spliced in AFTER
	//      coloring). No other lowering in this file writes a vreg twice.
	//   2. Moving the fold into FIXED registers to dodge (1) trades it for the opposite bug:
	//      `operandReuseInputValueId` PANICS on a reuse op with a physical input — *"no reuse op
	//      carries a physical input at coloring time"* (TargetOperands.maxon). A two-address op is
	//      simply not available here in either spelling.
	//
	// And the lowering cannot mint a fresh virtual to escape between them: the parser owns the
	// value space (`IrFunction.valueTags` is dense by ValueId) and `growValueSpace` belongs to the
	// splitter, which runs after liveness. `lea`'s independent destination is what threads the
	// needle — no reuse, no second def, no new id.
	//
	// The two `setcc`s READ the flags and write none, so the `ucomisd`'s answer survives to the
	// second. `lea` writes none either. The `==` form's `cmp` DOES write flags — which is why a
	// float equality that is also branched on is un-fused first (`settleFloatEqualityCondition`).
	function materializeFloatEquality(resultId ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match pred 'p'
			// `!=` IS the inequality value — it lands straight in its own virtual, one def, done.
			ne then self.appendFloatInequality(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), dstBlock: dstBlock)
			eq then self.appendFloatEqualityAsNotInequality(resultId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			lt to ge then panic("materializeFloatEquality: {pred.name} is an ORDERED float predicate — it materializes with a single `seta`/`setae` on swapped operands and has no parity bit to fold")
		end 'p'
	end 'materializeFloatEquality'

	// IEEE `a != b` into `dest`: `setne` + `setp` summed with an `lea`. See the header for why the
	// sum IS the OR, and why `lea` is the only shape the operand model permits here.
	//
	// The two `setcc`s write FIXED registers, which is safe by the mechanism `lowerShiftCl`
	// documents: a physical def has liveness forbid that register to every value live ACROSS it, so
	// the fold cannot destroy anything a later op still needs. `dest` itself is defined AT the
	// `lea`, so it is not live across either — it may legally be coloured into one of them, and
	// `lea dest, [rcx + rdx]` reads both inputs before writing its destination.
	function appendFloatInequality(dest TargetVReg, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let unequal = x64PhysReg(FloatInequalityUnequalReg)
		let unordered = x64PhysReg(FloatInequalityUnorderedReg)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.setccReg(unequal, cond: X64CondCode.notEqual))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.setccReg(unordered, cond: X64CondCode.parity))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegRegReg(dest, base: unequal, index: unordered))
	end 'appendFloatInequality'

	// IEEE `a == b` = NOT `a != b`. The inequality is built in a fixed register and then
	// truth-tested against zero — the same `cmp reg, 0` idiom every non-fused boolean condition
	// already uses (`BoolFalseImm`), and the reason the complement needs no `xor`: `xorRegImm32` is
	// two-address, so it would want a reuse input, which is the shape the header rules out.
	function appendFloatEqualityAsNotInequality(resultId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let inequality = x64PhysReg(FloatInequalityUnequalReg)
		self.appendFloatInequality(inequality, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.cmpRegImm32(inequality, imm: BoolFalseImm))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.setccReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), cond: X64CondCode.equal))
	end 'appendFloatEqualityAsNotInequality'

	// Lower a Std `condBranch` to `jcc <then>` + `jmp <else>`. The `jcc` is a body op and the
	// `jmp` is the block's terminator (a two-instruction two-way branch cannot fit one
	// terminator slot). An unconditional `jmp` to the else target is emitted for EVERY layout
	// so correctness never depends on block ordering (a later pass may elide a fall-through).
	//
	// THREE shapes, and which one applies is settled BEFORE the walk — by `computeFusedCmps` and
	// `settleFloatEqualityBranch` — never guessed here:
	//
	//   • A SPLIT FLOAT EQUALITY — a fused float `==`/`!=`, which reserved an `ordered` block
	//     because the parity bit is a second condition and a block may hold one. It takes
	//     precedence over both below; see the float-equality split section.
	//   • FUSED — the compare defining `condId` is the last flag-clobbering op of this block,
	//     so the flags it set are exactly the ones the `jcc` reads. The `jcc` tests that
	//     compare's predicate directly and nothing was materialized.
	//   • NOT FUSED — the condition is an ordinary boolean VALUE in a register: a `bool`
	//     parameter, a call's `bool` return, a short-circuit merge phi, a `let`-bound compare
	//     read a few statements later. It is TRUTH-TESTED against zero (`cmp reg, 0` + `jne`),
	//     which is the same instruction pair the old parser-synthesized `value != false`
	//     compare used to fold to — so `if flag` emits byte-identical code, with two fewer IR
	//     ops to get there.
	//
	// `appendCondBranch`, NOT `appendOp`: this is the ONE site in the compiler that makes a
	// block two-way, so it is the one site that records where the `jcc` landed. Without that
	// index a block's then-edge is only findable by scanning every op of the block, which is
	// what made building the CFG skeleton O(total ops) (see `CondBranch`). It also seals the
	// block: `appendOp` refuses to append past a conditional branch.
	function lowerCondBranch(condId ValueId, thenBlockId BlockId, elseBlockId BlockId, dstBlock IrBlock, blockRef BlockRef)
		let orderedId = try self.orderedBlockIds.get(blockRef) otherwise 'notFloatEquality'
			let cond = self.branchConditionCode(condId, blockRef: blockRef, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			self.targetModule.appendCondBranch(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.jcc(cond, targetBlockId: thenBlockId))
			self.targetModule.setTerminator(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.jmp(elseBlockId))
			return
		end 'notFloatEquality'

		self.lowerFloatEqualityBranch(blockRef, thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId, orderedId: orderedId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerCondBranch'

	// The FIRST block of a split float equality: it asks "unordered?" and nothing else. The
	// `ucomisd` is already in this block — `lowerCmp` emitted it as a body op — so all that is
	// left is the parity jump and the fall-through into `ordered` (which
	// `reserveFloatEqualityBlocks` has already emitted, complete).
	function lowerFloatEqualityBranch(blockRef BlockRef, thenBlockId BlockId, elseBlockId BlockId, orderedId BlockId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let pred = try self.cmpPreds.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("lowerFloatEqualityBranch: block ref {blockRef} fuses no compare, but an `ordered` block was reserved for its branch")
		let parityTarget = parityTargetOf(pred, thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId)
		self.targetModule.appendCondBranch(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.jcc(X64CondCode.parity, targetBlockId: parityTarget))
		self.targetModule.setTerminator(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.jmp(orderedId))

		// ⚠ THE OTHER PHI EDGE MUST GO, and this is the half that makes the split honest. This
		// block now branches to exactly two places: `parityTarget` and `ordered`. Its edge to the
		// OTHER original successor is stale — that successor is reached only THROUGH `ordered`
		// now, which was handed its own copy of the edge. Leaving it would have SSA destruction
		// place that phi's moves at the end of this block, on an edge it no longer takes, writing
		// a phi's register on the unordered path with the wrong incoming value. `ordered` itself
		// carries no phis (it is not a join), so the edge to it needs no `BranchEdge` at all.
		self.keepOnlyBranchEdgeIn(dstBlock, keepTarget: parityTarget)
	end 'lowerFloatEqualityBranch'

	// Drop every branch edge of `block` except the one to `keepTarget` — see the caller.
	function keepOnlyBranchEdgeIn(block IrBlock, keepTarget BlockId)
		var kept = BranchEdgeArray.create()
		for edge in block.branchEdges 'each'
			if edge.targetBlockId == keepTarget 'keep'
				kept.push(edge)
			end 'keep'
		end 'each'
		block.branchEdges = kept
	end 'keepOnlyBranchEdgeIn'

	// The condition code the branch's `jcc` tests, emitting the truth-test `cmp reg, 0` first
	// when the condition is a value rather than a fused compare's flags.
	function branchConditionCode(condId ValueId, blockRef BlockRef, dstBlock IrBlock) returns X64CondCode
		let pred = try self.cmpPreds.get(blockRef) otherwise 'notFused'
			self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.cmpRegImm32(TargetVReg.virtual(condId), imm: BoolFalseImm))
			return X64CondCode.notEqual
		end 'notFused'
		return condCodeForPred(pred, isFloat: self.fusedFloatCmps.contains(condId))
	end 'branchConditionCode'

	// ==========================================================================
	// The float-equality split
	// ==========================================================================
	//
	// ⭐⭐ AN f64 `==` NEEDS TWO CONDITIONAL JUMPS, AND A BLOCK MAY HAVE ONE.
	//
	// `ucomisd` reports UNORDERED (either operand NaN) as ZF=PF=CF=1 — the flags of "equal" but
	// for PF. IEEE-754 says `nan == nan` is FALSE and `nan != nan` is TRUE, so PF is the ONLY bit
	// separating those answers from equality, and a bare `je` would take the then-branch on a
	// NaN. The parity bit is a second condition, and a second condition is a second jump.
	//
	// v1 SHIPPED EXACTLY THIS AS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER, and how is worth knowing. It emitted ONE
	// block ending in TWO conditional jumps:
	//
	//     ucomisd a, b ; jp else ; jne else ; jmp then
	//
	// The IR said that block had one conditional branch and therefore ONE else edge. The machine
	// code had two. SSA destruction places a phi's copies on an edge by rewriting the jump that
	// takes it — so it found and rewired one of those jumps, and the other kept its original
	// target and bypassed the copies entirely. A phi whose value arrived on the `jp` path was
	// never written. (`project_x64_f64_compare_phi_copy_fix` — a positive double formatted as
	// negative, plus a runaway digit loop that spewed megabytes.)
	//
	// ⇒ shv2 SPLITS THE COMPARE INTO TWO BLOCKS, each ending in exactly one conditional branch:
	//
	//     cmp:     ucomisd a, b ; jp -> <the answer unordered decides> ; jmp -> ordered
	//     ordered:               je/jne -> then                        ; jmp -> else
	//
	// The machine code is the same three jumps. The difference is that the IR now SAYS what the
	// machine code does: two edges reach the far block, from two different blocks, and they ARE
	// two edges in the CFG. SSA destruction needs no float-specific case — it places copies on
	// both, because both are edges — and `IrBlock.CondBranch`'s one-conditional-branch-per-block
	// invariant is never bent. THE BUG IS NOT FIXED HERE. IT IS MADE UNREPRESENTABLE.
	//
	// specs-shv2/float-compare-branch.md puts a phi on the edge each of those jumps takes:
	// `eq-nan-else-edge-phi` rides the first block's `jp`, `ne-nan-then-edge-phi` rides it to the
	// other successor, and `eq-ordered-*-edge-phi` ride the second block's two.

	// Mint the `ordered` block of every float-equality branch in the function, emitted COMPLETE:
	// its `je`/`jne`, its `jmp`, and a copy of both of the original block's phi edges. The walk
	// is left only the original block's half, and needs nothing from here but a block id.
	//
	// ⚠ IT IS A SEPARATE PASS FOR A BORROW REASON, not for taste. Minting PUSHES to
	// `targetModule.blocks`, and the walk holds a live borrow out of that same array for the
	// whole of every block (its `dstBlock`) — the rule `moveBranchEdgeToSplit` scopes its helpers
	// apart to obey. It also pushes to `srcFunc.blockRefs`, which the walk reads by index.
	// Running it before either borrow exists is what makes both safe.
	function reserveFloatEqualityBlocks(funcIdx FuncIndex) returns OrderedBlockIdMap
		// COLLECT FIRST, THEN MINT — `splitCriticalEdgesInFunc`'s exact discipline, and for its
		// exact reason: the mint below fetches and MUTATES the function, so no borrow of it may
		// still be live, and it also appends to the very `blockRefs` this would otherwise iterate.
		// The classification is unaffected by the splits (each only adds a block), so the snapshot
		// stays valid across all of them.
		let toSplit = self.blocksNeedingFloatEqualitySplit(funcIdx)

		var reserved = OrderedBlockIdMap.create()
		for blockRef in toSplit 'eachSplit'
			reserved.upsert(blockRef, value: self.buildOrderedBlock(funcIdx, blockRef: blockRef))
		end 'eachSplit'
		return reserved
	end 'reserveFloatEqualityBlocks'

	// SETTLE EVERY FLOAT-EQUALITY BRANCH'S SHAPE, in one walk of the function's blocks: un-fuse the
	// ones whose boolean is also materialized, then collect the ones that still need a split.
	//
	// The two steps share this walk because they are one concern asked of one thing — "what shape
	// does this branch take?" — and because their ORDER is a requirement rather than a
	// coincidence: un-fusing must happen BEFORE the split test, or a branch that is about to lose
	// its fusion would still be handed an `ordered` block. Doing them per block rather than in two
	// passes makes that ordering local and structural instead of a comment about pass order, and
	// it is sound because each branch's decision reads only its OWN condition.
	//
	// It is also a scoped READ of the function (the un-fuse touches only `cmpPreds`), complete
	// before `reserveFloatEqualityBlocks` mints anything.
	function blocksNeedingFloatEqualitySplit(funcIdx FuncIndex) returns BlockRefArray
		let srcFunc = self.stdModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		var toSplit = BlockRefArray.create()
		for blockRef in srcFunc.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			if self.settleFloatEqualityBranch(blockRef) 'needsSplit'
				toSplit.push(blockRef)
			end 'needsSplit'
		end 'eachBlock'
		return toSplit
	end 'blocksNeedingFloatEqualitySplit'

	// Settle ONE block's float-equality branch and answer whether it still needs a split. Both the
	// un-fuse and the decision live here, in one terminator read, because they are one question
	// asked of one branch — and because their order is a REQUIREMENT (un-fuse first, or a branch
	// about to lose its fusion is still handed an `ordered` block), which is structural here
	// instead of a note about pass ordering.
	function settleFloatEqualityBranch(blockRef BlockRef) returns bool
		let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("settleFloatEqualityBranch: blockRef {blockRef} not in stdModule.blocks")
		if not block.hasTerminatorOp() 'noTerm'
			return false
		end 'noTerm'
		let term = try self.stdModule.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("settleFloatEqualityBranch: terminatorIndex not in stdModule.ops")
		return match term 'term'
			condBranch(condId, _, _) gives self.settleFloatEqualityCondition(blockRef, condId: condId)
			const to mod gives false
			branch to rdataAddr gives false
			// A `funcAddr`/`callIndirect` is never a block terminator — only a `condBranch` gets a
			// non-false answer here (P1.5-A1).
			tryCall to osFreePages gives false
			// The jump table is not a float-equality condBranch.
			switch gives false
			// A witness call is a body op, never a float-equality condBranch — nor is its throwing form.
			witnessCall gives false
			witnessTryCall gives false
			// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: an IOCP op is never a terminator, let alone a float-equality condBranch.
			osPipeCreate to osWriteFile gives false
			// The wall-clock read is never a terminator, let alone a float-equality condBranch.
			osReadWallClock gives false
			// R4.1 + R4.2: no file op is ever a terminator, let alone a float-equality condBranch.
			osFileOpenRead to osFileAttributes gives false
			// A panic is never a terminator, so it is never the float-equality condBranch this asks about.
			osPanicValue gives false
			// A command-line read is never a terminator either.
			osCommandLine gives false
			// W5: nor is a standard-handle read or an executable-path fill.
			osStdHandle gives false
			osExecutablePath gives false
			// W35: nor is a stream write.
			osWriteStderr gives false
			// Nor is a frame address.
			stackRecordAddr gives false
			osOpenNamedSegment to osTickCountMs gives false
			osHandleSetInherit to osThreadCpuTicks gives false
			// F1a: a decommit is never a terminator, let alone a float-equality condBranch.
			osDecommitPages gives false
			// F1b: nor is a byte fill.
			memFill gives false
			// F1c: nor is a page reserve or commit.
			osCommitPages to osReservePages gives false
			// F2: nor is an atomic or a thread-token read.
			atomicCas to currentThreadToken gives false
		end 'term'
	end 'settleFloatEqualityBranch'

	// The decision itself, for one branch condition. Three answers, and only the first needs a
	// second block:
	//
	//   • a FUSED FLOAT `==`/`!=` read ONLY by this branch ⇒ SPLIT. Its flags carry the answer and
	//     the parity bit is a second condition, so it needs a second block to test it in.
	//   • a fused float equality whose boolean is ALSO read as a value ⇒ UN-FUSE, no split. Its
	//     materialization folds the parity bit with an `and`/`or` (`materializeFloatEquality`), and
	//     that WRITES EFLAGS — destroying the very PF the split's `jp` would read. The Std-tier
	//     fusion scan cannot see this coming: it proves the `cmp` is the last flag-clobbering STD
	//     op before the branch, and the `and` does not exist at that tier. It is the hazard
	//     `computeFusedCmps`' header raises for the ALLOCATOR's inserted ops, arriving from the
	//     lowering instead. Dropping the fusion is the whole fix and costs nothing that matters:
	//     the branch then truth-tests the materialized bool (`cmp reg, 0` + `jne`), exactly as
	//     every other non-fused boolean condition already does. It is SELF-CONSISTENT — the value
	//     is in `valuesReadFromRegisters` precisely because something reads it, so the `setcc` the
	//     branch now depends on is emitted regardless of this.
	//   • anything else ⇒ no split. An UNFUSED condition is an ordinary bool in a register; an
	//     INTEGER compare has no unordered answer; an ORDERED float predicate is answered by a
	//     single `ja`/`jae` on swapped operands and needs no second jump at all.
	function settleFloatEqualityCondition(blockRef BlockRef, condId ValueId) returns bool
		if not self.isFusedFloatEquality(blockRef, condId: condId) 'notFloatEquality'
			return false
		end 'notFloatEquality'
		if not self.valuesReadFromRegisters.contains(condId) 'flagsOnly'
			return true
		end 'flagsOnly'

		// The entry is provably present — `isFusedFloatEquality` just read it — so the "was it
		// there?" answer carries no information and is discarded. Only `cmpPreds` is unwound, and
		// that is correct rather than a half-erasure: dropping the predicate makes this value's
		// `fusedFloatCmps` bit UNREACHABLE, because every reader of that set gates on a `cmpPreds`
		// hit first (see the field). A dense bool column has no `remove` to call anyway.
		_ = self.cmpPreds.remove(blockRef)
		return false
	end 'settleFloatEqualityCondition'

	// True iff `condId` is defined by a FUSED compare that is a FLOAT `==` or `!=` — the one
	// compare shape whose branch cannot be a single jump.
	function isFusedFloatEquality(blockRef BlockRef, condId ValueId) returns bool
		let pred = try self.cmpPreds.get(blockRef) otherwise 'notFused'
			return false
		end 'notFused'
		return self.fusedFloatCmps.contains(condId) and predicateIsEquality(pred)
	end 'isFusedFloatEquality'

	// Create the `ordered` block for `blockRef`'s float-equality branch, emit it complete, and
	// return its id.
	//
	// It is reached ONLY when `ucomisd` said ORDERED — the original block's `jp` has already
	// taken the unordered answer away — so its `je`/`jne` is the plain equality test with no NaN
	// case left in it. That is the whole trick: each block asks exactly ONE question.
	//
	// Every step is its own scoped helper, so no two live borrows of `targetModule.blocks` ever
	// coexist. Reads come first, then the mint, then the writes.
	function buildOrderedBlock(funcIdx FuncIndex, blockRef BlockRef) returns BlockId
		// The condition value is not needed here any more: the predicate is looked up by BLOCK
		// (see `CmpPredMap`), which is the same key this split was selected under.
		let (_, thenBlockId, elseBlockId) = self.condBranchFieldsOf(blockRef)
		let pred = try self.cmpPreds.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("buildOrderedBlock: block ref {blockRef} fuses no compare, but blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit said it needs a split")

		// The original's phi edges, cloned BEFORE the new block exists — the walk prunes the
		// original down to its one surviving edge later, and `ordered` needs both.
		let inheritedEdges = self.cloneBranchEdgesOf(blockRef)

		let newRef = self.addOrderedBlock(funcIdx)
		let newId = self.blockIdOf(newRef)
		self.emitOrderedBlockBody(newRef, cond: floatCondCodeForPred(pred), thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId, edges: inheritedEdges)
		return newId
	end 'buildOrderedBlock'

	// Mint an empty `ordered` block: a fresh function-local id, an entry in `targetModule.blocks`,
	// and its ref registered on the function. Mirrors `addSplitBlock` (SsaDestruction), which is
	// the established way a Target-tier pass grows the CFG — including its deliberate omission of
	// `blockSourceInfo`, which the Target module does not carry (`prepareSkeleton` never fills it).
	// The function is fetched HERE, by index, and mutated directly — its borrow never escapes to a
	// caller that could still be holding one. Same shape as `addSplitBlock`.
	function addOrderedBlock(funcIdx FuncIndex) returns BlockRef
		var srcFunc = self.stdModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		let newId = srcFunc.nextBlockId
		srcFunc.nextBlockId = srcFunc.nextBlockId + 1
		self.targetModule.blocks.push(IrBlock.create(newId, label: FloatOrderedLabel))
		let newRef = self.targetModule.blocks.count() - 1
		srcFunc.blockRefs.push(newRef)
		return newRef
	end 'addOrderedBlock'

	// Emit the `ordered` block's whole body: the ordered equality test to `then`, the `jmp` to
	// `else`, and the phi edges it inherited. Uniform for both `==` and `!=` — only `cond`
	// differs (`je` vs `jne`), because the unordered answer was already decided by the `jp`.
	function emitOrderedBlockBody(blockRef BlockRef, cond X64CondCode, thenBlockId BlockId, elseBlockId BlockId, edges BranchEdgeArray)
		var block = try self.targetModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("emitOrderedBlockBody: blockRef {blockRef} not in targetModule.blocks")
		block.branchEdges = edges
		self.targetModule.appendCondBranch(block, op: TargetOp.jcc(cond, targetBlockId: thenBlockId))
		self.targetModule.setTerminator(block, op: TargetOp.jmp(elseBlockId))
	end 'emitOrderedBlockBody'

	// A copy of every branch edge of `blockRef`'s block.
	//
	// FRESH `BranchEdge` objects, not the same ones: the Target tier OWNS its BranchEdges and
	// REBUILDS them (`extractPredEdge` rebuilds the array to move an edge onto a split block, and
	// `rewriteEdgeArgsIn` swaps an edge's `argIds` to repoint it at a reload), so two blocks
	// sharing one edge object would let a later rewrite of one silently move the other. `argIds`
	// IS shared, and that is correct — nothing mutates it in place (see `prepareSkeleton`).
	function cloneBranchEdgesOf(blockRef BlockRef) returns BranchEdgeArray
		let block = try self.targetModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("cloneBranchEdgesOf: blockRef {blockRef} not in targetModule.blocks")
		var copied = BranchEdgeArray.create()
		for edge in block.branchEdges 'each'
			copied.push(BranchEdge.create(edge.targetBlockId, argIds: edge.argIds))
		end 'each'
		return copied
	end 'cloneBranchEdgesOf'

	// The id of `blockRef`'s block — a scoped read, so the caller never holds this borrow.
	function blockIdOf(blockRef BlockRef) returns BlockId
		let block = try self.targetModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("blockIdOf: blockRef {blockRef} not in targetModule.blocks")
		return block.id
	end 'blockIdOf'

	// The three fields of the `condBranch` terminating `blockRef`'s Std block, in one read and one
	// match — a TUPLE, because three one-field readers would be the same lookup and the same two
	// panic arms written three times, and a fourth field would have to be added to all of them.
	// (A tuple, not a payload-carrying union: `fuseIfDefines`'s heap-box warning is about unions,
	// and this runs once per SPLIT rather than once per op regardless.)
	function condBranchFieldsOf(blockRef BlockRef) returns (ValueId, BlockId, BlockId)
		let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("condBranchFieldsOf: blockRef {blockRef} not in stdModule.blocks")
		let term = try self.stdModule.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("condBranchFieldsOf: terminatorIndex of block ref {blockRef} not in stdModule.ops")
		return match term 'term'
			condBranch(condId, thenBlockId, elseBlockId) gives (condId, thenBlockId, elseBlockId)
			const to mod panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			branch to rdataAddr panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			// A `funcAddr`/`callIndirect` is never a block terminator; like every non-`condBranch`
			// arm here it cannot occur and panics (P1.5-A1).
			tryCall to osFreePages panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			switch panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is a jump table, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			witnessCall panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is a witness call, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			witnessTryCall panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is a throwing witness call, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osPipeCreate to osWriteFile panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is a P1.5 IOCP op, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osReadWallClock panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is an `osReadWallClock`, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osFileOpenRead to osFileAttributes panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is a file-IO op, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osPanicValue panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is an `osPanicValue`, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osCommandLine panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is an `osCommandLine`, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osStdHandle panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is an `osStdHandle`, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osExecutablePath panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is an `osExecutablePath`, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osWriteStderr panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is an `osWriteStderr`, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			stackRecordAddr panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is a `stackRecordAddr`, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osOpenNamedSegment to osTickCountMs panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is a DebugStream host primitive, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osHandleSetInherit to osProcessTerminate panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is an attached-subprocess host primitive, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osSocketStartup to osSocketClose panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is a socket host primitive, not a condBranch")
			osCpuCount panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is an `osCpuCount`, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osThreadCpuTicks panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is an `osThreadCpuTicks`, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osDecommitPages panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is an `osDecommitPages`, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			osCommitPages to osReservePages panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is a page reserve or commit, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			memFill panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is a `memFill`, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			atomicCas to currentThreadToken panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is an atomic or a thread-token read, not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
		end 'term'
	end 'condBranchFieldsOf'

	// Lower a Std unconditional `branch` to a single `jmp` terminator.
	function lowerBranch(targetBlockId BlockId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.setTerminator(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.jmp(targetBlockId))
	end 'lowerBranch'

	// Lower the dense-match jump table to the x64 `x64JumpTable` terminator. Reserves the `.rdata` blob of
	// N i32 slots HERE (the lowering owns `globalData`, exactly as `lowerFloatConst` registers its float
	// island), and the op carries the unique blob label the encoder emits a `lea` to and the writer patches.
	// The biased index rides as a virtual register; `caseTargets`/`defaultBlockId` are the CFG the successor
	// walker and the encoder read. RE-MATCHES `op` in a SMALL match — the `lowerIndirectCall` discipline.
	function lowerSwitch(op StdOp, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match op 'sw'
			switch(subjectId, denseTargetBlockIds, defaultBlockId) then self.emitJumpTable(subjectId, caseTargets: denseTargetBlockIds, defaultTarget: defaultBlockId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			default panic("lowerSwitch: expected a `switch` op, got a different StdOp variant — `lowerTerminatorOp` routes only `switch` here")
		end 'sw'
	end 'lowerSwitch'

	function emitJumpTable(subjectId ValueId, caseTargets BlockIdArray, defaultTarget BlockId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let tableLabel = self.globalData.registerJumpTable(caseTargets.count())
		self.targetModule.setTerminator(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.x64JumpTable(TargetVReg.virtual(subjectId), defaultTarget: defaultTarget, caseTargets: caseTargets, tableLabel: tableLabel.toByteArray()))
	end 'emitJumpTable'

	// Value return: move the value into the primary-return register (R8), then emit the
	// `ret`. A VOID return (`StdOp.retVoid`) carries no value at all and goes straight to
	// `emitRet` — it does not come through here.
	function lowerReturn(retValId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock, returnType StdReturnType, asTerminator bool)
		self.emitPrimaryReturnMove(retValId, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: returnType)
		self.emitRet(dstBlock, asTerminator: asTerminator)
	end 'lowerReturn'

	// Void return: nothing moves to the return register, because the caller reads
	// nothing from it.
	//
	// The DECLARED return type must agree, and this is the guard that says so. A
	// `retVoid` in a VALUE-returning function would emit the `ret` with R8 never
	// written, handing the caller whatever the last op happened to leave there — the
	// silent miscompile a bare `return` in a value-returning function used to produce,
	// back when the parser chose the return op from the token after `return` instead of
	// from the declared type. The parser now rejects that source (E3013), so a
	// disagreement reaching here is a LOWERING bug, and it fails loudly. This is the
	// exact mirror of `emitPrimaryReturnMove`'s guard: between them, neither direction
	// of the value/void contract can be broken quietly.
	function lowerVoidReturn(dstBlock IrBlock, returnType StdReturnType, asTerminator bool)
		if not stdReturnTypeIsVoid(returnType) 'valueExpected'
			panic("lowerStdToX64: a `retVoid` in a value-returning function — the return register would never be written, and the caller would read garbage")
		end 'valueExpected'

		self.emitRet(dstBlock, asTerminator: asTerminator)
	end 'lowerVoidReturn'

	// The bare `ret`, shared by the value and void returns — the ONE thing they have in
	// common. Frame teardown (`epilogue`) is inserted separately by the prologue/epilogue
	// pass. `asTerminator` toggles setTerminator (block-final) vs appendOp (the rare
	// mid-block ret the Maxon tier can emit for a partially-terminated block).
	function emitRet(dstBlock IrBlock, asTerminator bool)
		if asTerminator 'asTerm'
			self.targetModule.setTerminator(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.ret)
		end 'asTerm' else 'asBody'
			self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.ret)
		end 'asBody'
	end 'emitRet'

	// Move a return VALUE into its primary-return register — **XMM0** for a float return, **R8** otherwise
	// (Maxon's internal calling convention returns in R8, NOT RAX). A float value is XMM-classed by the
	// value-class column (from its f64 Std type), so `movRegReg(xmm0, retVal)` sees two XMM ends and emits
	// `movsd`; an integer value and R8 are both GPR. The register is chosen from the RETURN TYPE so the
	// ordinary `ret` path is ABI-correct, and `emitRegRegMove` would panic on any cross-file pair — the
	// backstop against colouring a return value into the wrong file.
	//
	// Shared by the ordinary `ret` (via `emitPrimaryReturnMove`, which rejects a void return type first)
	// and the throwing `errorReturn` (whose void form legitimately carries a family-default zero-int value:
	// `stdReturnTypeIsFloat(void)` is `false`, so it lands in R8, where a GPR zero belongs and the caller
	// ignores it once the flag is set).
	function emitReturnValueMove(retValId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock, returnType StdReturnType)
		let retReg = X64Register.xmm0 if stdReturnTypeIsFloat(returnType) else X64Register.r8
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(retReg), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(retValId)))
	end 'emitReturnValueMove'

	// The ordinary `ret`'s primary move. A `void` StdReturnType reaching here means a VALUE return
	// (`StdOp.ret`) inside a function declared void. What ESTABLISHES that it cannot is the parser: a return
	// statement's op is chosen from the function's DECLARED return type, and a `return <expr>` in a void
	// function is rejected there as E3005 (Parser.parseReturnStatement) — it is not merely assumed to be
	// absent. Before that check existed this panic was reachable from ordinary source (`function f(x int)
	// return x end`), which is how a compiler crash gets shipped behind a comment asserting it cannot happen.
	//
	// So a `void` here is now a LOWERING bug, not a user error, and it panics rather than silently dropping
	// the value. `lowerVoidReturn` guards the opposite direction. (The throwing `errorReturn` path does NOT
	// come through here — its void form has a real dummy value to place, so it calls `emitReturnValueMove`
	// directly.)
	function emitPrimaryReturnMove(retValId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock, returnType StdReturnType)
		if stdReturnTypeIsVoid(returnType) 'void'
			panic("lowerStdToX64: a value `ret` in a void-returning function — a void return lowers to `StdOp.retVoid`, which never reaches this path")
		end 'void'
		self.emitReturnValueMove(retValId, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: returnType)
	end 'emitPrimaryReturnMove'
end 'StdToX64Lowering'

// The memory-access WIDTH door (`accessWidthFor`) and the byte-offset → displacement door
// (`memoryDisplacement`) are TARGET-INDEPENDENT and live in `Targets/Shared/StdLoweringShared.maxon`,
// shared with the arm64 lowering — the width says HOW MANY BITS, which is not ISA-specific; only the
// encoder that turns `byte`/`word64` into `movzx`/`mov` (vs `ldrb`/`ldr`) is. `lowerLoadIndirect` /
// `lowerStoreIndirect` call them.
